/	LAST EDIT:	05-MAR-2016	12:00:00	CJL

/	MUST BE ASSEMBLED WITH '/J' [OS/8 PAL8 '/F'] OPTION SWITCH SET.

/	P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER FOR PRIMARY TD8E [DEVICE CODE 77] WITH TU56H [ONE
/	DRIVE ONLY] OR TU56 [TWO DRIVES].

/	AUTOMATICALLY REMEMBERS PROPER INITIAL SEARCH DIRECTION WHEN THE LOGICAL
/	CONSOLE OVERLAY IS IN EFFECT; OTHERWISE ALWAYS STARTS SEARCHES IN REVERSE
/	SIMILAR TO TC12.  [NOTE: THIS APPLIES TO CALLS FOR EITHER LOGICAL DRIVE UNIT.]

/	IGNORES THE TWO HIGH-ORDER BITS OF THE LOGICAL UNIT FOR ALL CALLS.  THUS, THE
/	CALL CANNOT DISTINGUISH BETWEEN DRIVES 0, 2, 4, 6 OR 1, 3, 5, 7; FUTURE
/	RELEASES WILL CAUSE A SELECT ERROR ON LOGICAL UNITS 2 THROUGH 7 WHEN THE
/	LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY IS IN EFFECT.  [NOTE: THE MR8E-C ROM ONLY SUPPORTS THE
/	DEVICE 77 TD8E CONTROLLER; NON-SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS CAPABLE OF SUPPORTING 4
/	TD8E CONTROLLERS AND UP TO EIGHT DRIVES WILL EVENTUALLY BE IMPLEMENTED.]

/	CAN ONLY BE BOOTSTRAPPED TO LOGICAL DRIVE UNIT 0.  THIS IS A RESTRICTION OF
/	THE MR8E-C ROM.  ALTERNATE BOOTSTRAPS MAY BE DEVELOPED IN FUTURE RELEASES TO
/	OVERCOME THIS [WITH DIFFICULTY].

/	THE BASIC DEVICE HANDLER HALTS/RETRIES ON ALL ERRORS DETECTED BY THE ROM.  DUE
/	TO LACK OF SPACE IN FIELD 0, THE ROM-BASED RETRY COUNT LOGIC IS NOT SUPPORTED.
/	HOWEVER, IN KEEPING WITH THE P?S/8 PHILOSOPHY OF NOT OBFUSCATING HARDWARE
/	PROBLEMS, THIS DESIGN DECISION WOULD APPLY EVEN IF THE CODE SPACE WERE
/	AVAILABLE.  IT IS NECESSARY TO MAKE THE USER AWARE OF DEGRADING HARDWARE
/	SITUATIONS; THIS CAN ONLY BE REALIZED BY ALERTING THE USER WITH APPROPRIATE
/	ERROR HANDLING.  WHEN THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY IS IN EFFECT, THE NATURE OF
/	THE ERROR WILL BE EVEN MORE OBVIOUS.  HOWEVER, PROPERLY FUNCTIONING EQUIPMENT
/	AND DECTAPE MEDIA DOES NOT ROUTINELY CAUSE ERRORS; THEY SHOULD BE TREATED AS A
/	SIGN OF IMPENDING FAILURE OF THE DRIVE GUIDES AND/OR THE DECTAPE MEDIA.  IF
/	TAKEN CARE OF EARLY, ALL DATA ON THE DECTAPE SHOULE BE RECOVERABLE BEFORE THE
/	PROBLEM WORSENS.  [NOTE: THE RETRY COUNT IS USED THROUGHOUT OS/8 DECTAPE
/	HANDLING AND IS THE ROOT CAUSE OF THIS PROBLEM; BY PROPER DESIGN, THIS
/	"FEATURE" WITHIN THE MR8E-C IS AVOIDED.]

/	INTERRUPT STATUS IS RESTORED AFTER A CALL TO THE SYSTEM HANDLER; INTERNALLY,
/	THE HANDLER OPERATES WITH INTERRUPTS OFF TO PREVENT TIMING PROBLEMS.  WHILE
/	THERE IS NO RULES REGARDING THIS THROUGHOUT P?S/8, GIVEN THAT THE TD8E IS TO
/	BE USED ONLY ON OMNIBUS MACHINES ALLOWS THIS FEATURE TO BE ADDED.  [NOTE: DUE
/	TO SPEED CONSIDERATIONS, IT IS RECOMMENDED THE TD8E ONLY BE USED WHERE THE
/	KK8E CPU IS AVAILABLE [PDP-8/E, PDP-8/F, PDP-8/M AND PDP-8/A-600 SERIES].
/	THIS IS ESPECIALLY PROBLEMATIC WHEN THE MR8E-C IS IN USE DUE TO THE OVERHEAD
/	CAUSED BY INTER-FIELD SUBROUTINE CALLS.

/	ADDITIONAL NOTEWORTHY FEATURES WILL BE DESCRIBED HERE AS THE BINARY IMAGE CODE
/	IS DISSASSEMBLED.
/	NOTE: CERTAIN FEATURES OF THE HANDLER ARE BASED ON THE RECOLLECTION OF
/	CHARLES LASNER, P?S NY, WHO HASTILY WROTE THE ORIGINAL VERSION 40 YEARS AGO,
/	WHEN BRIEFLY AN APPROPRIATE PDP-8/E WITH TD8E AND MR8E-C CONFIGURATION WAS
/	AVAILABLE DURING A TRANSIENT PROGRAMMING ASSIGNMENT.  DUE TO CIRCUMSTANCES,
/	THIS SYSTEM CONFIGURATION WAS NEVER UPDATED; THE ORIGINAL SOURCE CODE IS NOT
/	AVAILABLE AND PRESUMED PERMANENTLY LOST.  IT IS KNOWN THAT THE SOURCE CODE OF
/	THE MR8E-C ROM WAS INCLUDED WITHIN THE SOURCE FILE OF THE HANDLER TO ALLOW
/	OCCASIONAL DEVELOPMENT CONSISTENT WITH THE OVERALL EVOLVEMENT OF P?S/8.
/	HOWEVER, NO SIGNIFICANT CHANGES TO P?S/8 OF ANY RELEVANCE TO THE TD8E WERE
/	MADE AFTER THE LAST KNOWN CONTACT WITH AN ALTERNATE PDP-8/E SYSTEM IN 1976
/	[AND PERHAPS THE EARLY PART OF 1977.  THE LAST KNOWN SYSTEM CONSISTED OF A
/	TU56H, TD8E AND 32K OF CORE MEMORY [AND UNRELATED PERIPHERALS].  THE MR8E-C
/	ROM IMAGE WAS LOADED BEFORE BOOTING THE SYSTEM TO ALLOW CREATING AN IMAGE OF A
/	FUNCTIONAL SYSTEM FOR TESTING.

/	DUE TO THE CIRCUMSTANCES OF P?S/8 DEVELOPMENT, ALL TD8E-SPECIFIC CODE WAS
/	STRIPPED OUT OF THE TYPICAL SET OF DEVELOPMENTAL SOURCE FILES DUE TO FILE SIZE
/	LIMITATIONS.  DUE TO THE APPLICABLE LOGISTICS, ALL DEVELOPMENT FILES WERE
/	SPLIT INTO TWO SECTIONS EACH CAPABLE OF BEING STORED ON HALF OF AN RK8E/RK05
/	PACK.  IT WAS IMPRACTICAL TO PHYSICALLY TRANSPORT THE MEDIA; DECISIONS WERE
/	MADE TO SPLIT OFF THE TD8E-RELATED FILES [AS WELL AS CERTAIN OTHER FILES
/	RELEVANT TO OTHER CONFIGURATIONS].  IT IS HOPED THE SOURCE FILES FOR THESE
/	OTHER CONFIGURATIONS CAN BE RECONSTRUCTED FROM SURVIVING BINARY MEDIA; IF THIS
/	CANNOT BE ACCOMPLISHED, THE CONFIGURATION WILL REQUIRE COMPLETE SOURCE
/	RECREATION AND ACCESS TO THE APPLICABLE HARDWARE AT THE MINIMUM.  NOTE:
/	CERTAIN ENHANCEMENTS TO THE SOURCE CODE SPECIFIC TO THESE SYSTEMS WILL ALSO
/	REQUIRE ADDITIONAL CONTACT WITH THE RELEVANT HARDWARE; P?S/8 DEVELOPMENT
/	IS NOW ACTIVE AGAIN AFTER A SHORT RECESS OF ABOUT 22 YEARS.  SOME OF THESE
/	ISSUES WILL BE RESOLVED AS TIME PERMITS AS WELL AS ACCESS TO THE RELEVANT
/	HARDWARE.  THE TD8E ISSUES WILL BE RESOLVED USING THE SIMH PDP-8 SIMULATOR
/	FOLLOWED BY PERIODIC TESTING ON TD8E-BASED PDP-8/E HARDWARE.

/	UNTIL ALL TD8E-SPECIFIC CODE CAN BE DISASSEMBLED FROM THE RECENTLY RECOVERED
/	VERSION 8T DECTAPE, IT IS NOT CLEAR IF THIS TAPE REPRESENTS THE FINAL POINT IN
/	THE DEVELOPMENT OF THIS SYSTEM.  CERTAIN COMPONENTS NOT YET CONFIRMED SHOULD
/	PROVIDE PLAUSIBLE ANSWERS.  [IT IS KNOWN THERE WAS A SPECIAL INTERACTION
/	BETWEEN THIS HARDWARE CONFIGURATION AND THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY WHEN
/	CONFIGURED EXPRESSLY FOR THE VT8E.]
/	PROGRAM EDIT HISTORY.

/	01-APR-1975	-	ORIGINAL RELEASE OF THE BINARY CODE [ESTIMATED]; THIS
/				IS LIKELY THE ONLY SURVIVING COPY OF THE P?S/8
/				OPERATING SYSTEM FOR THE TD8E/MR8E-C.  MOST OF THE
/				SOURCE CODE WILL BE OBTAINED BY DISASSEMBLY OF THIS
/				RELEASE RECOVERED ON A SURVIVING DECTAPE.

/				NOTE: IT IS SUSPECTED THIS IS THE ONLY KNOWN SURVIVING
/				COPY OF THE CONFIGURATION FOR THE TD8E WITH MR8E-C
/				ROM.  IT IS LIKELY IT WAS NOT PROPERLY TESTED.  AS OF
/				THIS WRITING, IT IS NOW KNOWN THE CONFIGURATION IS
/				FATALLY FLAWED, BUT TENTATIVELY FUNCTIONAL BY A
/				CONTRIVED KLUDGE THAT WAS APPARENTLY APPLIED AFTER
/				USING ANY P?S/8 OPERATION THAT MODIFIED BLOCK 0000.
/				LATER RELEASES WILL OVERCOME THIS BY MAKING FURTHER
/				CHANGES IN THE DESIGN WHICH WILL CORRECT THE PROBLEM.
/				THE ONLY KNOWN PROBLEM THAT CANNOT BE OVERCOME IS THAT
/				THE DESIGN CHANGE WILL ELIMINATE THE FATAL PROBLEM,
/				BUT THE SYSTEM BOOTUP PROCEDURE WILL REQUIRE AN
/				ADDITIONAL TAPE TURN-AROUND.  [USERS OF THE TC01/TC08
/				RELEASE OF P?S/8 MAY NOTICE THIS TINY LOSS OF
/				PERFORMANCE; BY CONTRAST, OS/8 BOOTUP TAKES MUCH MORE
/				TIME THAN EITHER RELEASE.]

/	18-DEC-2015	-	IMAGE OF THE 1975 RELEASE AVAILABLE FOR USE WITH THE
/				SIMH PDP-8 SIMULATOR.  EXAMINATION OF THE TAPE
/				CONFIRMS IT IS A SYSTEM CREATED SOMETIME IN 1974/1975.
/				HOWEVER, THE TAPE CANNOT BE BOOTED BECAUSE THE IMAGE
/				OF THE MR8E-C ROM IS NOT AVAILABLE TO THE SIMH PDP-8
/				SIMULATOR.

/	26-JAN-2016	-	OPERATIONAL PRE-FINAL RELEASE OF THE SOURCE CODE OF
/				THE MR8E-C ROM IS COMPLETED.  USING THE MR8E-C BINARY
/				IMAGE, THE ORIGINAL RELEASE IS BOOTED ON THE SIMH
/				PDP-8 SIMULATOR; ALTHOUGH THIS IS A VERY OLD RELEASE
/				OF P?S/8 [APPARENTLY VERSION 8T BASED ON ASSOCIATED
/				DECTAPE PACKAGING], MOST OF THE BASIC ELEMENTS OF
/				DEVICE-SPECIFIC FUNCTIONALITY ARE CONFIRMED WORKING ON
/				THE SIMH PDP-8 SIMULATOR [OTHER THAN OPERATIONS THAT
/				MODIFY THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000 SUCH AS THE "DATE"
/				COMMAND, WHICH WRITES THE CURRENT DATE VALUE ON THE
/				DECTAPE].  DISASSEMBLY WILL PROCEED FOR IDENTIFIABLE
/				ELEMENTS; DIRECT BINARY COMPARISON WILL BE USED TO
/				CONFIRM SOURCE FILE AUTHENTICITY.  UNTIL THIS IS
/				ACCOMPLISHED, ANY IDEAS REGARDING CODE IMPROVEMENT
/				WILL BE DEFERRED; HOWEVER, CERTAIN TENTATIVE CHANGES
/				ARE BEING MADE TO THE SOURCE FILE AS COMMENTS TO BE
/				CONSIDERED AFTER ALL RECOVERABLE SOURCE CODE
/				OPERATIONS HAVE CONCLUDED.
/	31-JAN-2016	-	NEW P?S/8-SPECIFIC TD8E BOOTSTRAP ADDED; THE BINARY
/				RELEASE IS CONFIRMED COMPATIBLE WITH THIS NEWER CODE.
/				[NOTE: SEE SPECIFICS OF THIS CODE ELSEWHERE IN THIS
/				FILE FOR DESCRIPTION OF NEW FEATURES.]  [NOTE: IN
/				LIGHT OF THE FATAL FLAW NOW KNOWN TO EXIST IN THIS
/				SPECIFIC RELEASE, THIS BOOTSTRAP MAY EITHER BE
/				WITHDRAWN OR MODIFIED TO CREATE A CUSTOM BOOTSTRAP FOR
/				VARIOUS P?S/8-SPECIFIC PURPOSES SUCH AS SUPPORTING AN
/				ALTERNATE BOOTSTRAP TO DRIVE UNIT 1 IF AT ALL
/				FEASIBLE.  IT IS PRESENTLY IN THE SOURCE CODE BECAUSE
/				IT IS A FUNCTIONAL EXAMPLE OF HOW TO BOOT THIS
/				PARTICULAR SYSTEM DECTAPE; ALSO IT CAN BE ASSEMBLED
/				TO BOOT THIS SYSTEM IN ONE LESS WORD THAN THE
/				STANDARD DEC TD8E BOOTSTRAP.  [NOTE: ALL OF THESE
/				CONSIDERATIONS ARE MOOT SINCE THIS IS THE MR8E-C ROM
/				SYSTEM WHICH HAS THE EQUIVALENT FUNCTIONALITY BUILT
/				IN; HOWEVER, THIS MAY BE REVISITED WHEN THE P?S/8
/				TD8E NON-ROM SYSTEM IS ATTEMPTED SOME TIME IN THE
/				FUTURE; IT IS HOPED THAT AN EXAMPLE OF THAT SYSTEM IS
/				RECOVERED.  OTHERWISE, IT WILL HAVE TO BE REDESIGNED
/				AND RECODED FROM SCRATCH.]  FUTURE RELEASES OF THIS
/				FILE WILL REMOVE ALL NON-FUNCTIONAL CODE FRAGMENTS AT
/				THE APPROPRIATE TIME AS PART OF A CODE CLEANUP
/				OPERATION, ETC.


/	05-FEB-2016	-	IMAGE OF BLOCK 0 RECREATED IN SOURCE CODE [CONFIRMED
/				ACCURATE.  NOTE: THIS RELEASE IS ACTUALLY DEFECTIVE AS
/				A PRACTICAL MATTER; WORK STARTED ON CORRECTING DEFECTS
/				FOR A FUTURE RELEASE ONCE ENOUGH OF THE CODE IS
/				RECOVERED TO ALLOW APPLICATION TO P?S/8 VERSION 8Z.
/				CERTAIN LOCATIONS WITHIN THE HANDLER ARE INDICATED AS
/				CONTAINING SPECIFIC VALUES THAT ARE A PART OF THE
/				ATTEMPT TO BOOT THE SYSTEM IN ONE TAPE PASS.  WHEN
/				THE FATAL FLAW IS CORRECTED, THESE LOCATIONS WILL BE
/				MODIFIED TO REFLECT THEIR INTENDED VALUE.

/	18-FEB-2016	-	WORK COMMENCING ON THE SLURP LOADER WHICH WAS
/				DISCOVERED BY TRIAL AND ERROR.  P?S/8 CONVENTIONS HAVE
/				CHANGED OVER THE YEARS FOR PLACEMENT OF THIS PORTION
/				OF THE OPERATING SYSTEM DEVICE-DEPENDENT COMPONENTS.
/				WHEN VERSION 8T WAS RELEASED CIRCA 1974/1975, THE
/				"BIN" SYSTEM PROGRAM [THAT LAUNCHES THE SLURP LOADER
/				WHEN NEEDED], INCLUDED THIS BLOCK WITHIN ITS OWN
/				CORE IMAGE.
/				THIS IS POOR DESIGN SINCE THAT MEANS THAT SHOULD A
/				TD8E DECTAPE BE ACCESSED ON A TC01/TC08 [OR OTHER
/				POTENTIAL PROBLEMATIC INTERACTION] OCCUR, THE "BIN"
/				PROGRAM ON THAT SYSTEM WILL LOAD THE INCORRECT SLURP
/				LOADER.  WHILE THERE CANNOT BE PROTECTION FROM THIS IN
/				GENERAL [AS CERTAIN SYSTEM CONVENTIONS ONLY CHANGE
/				SLOWLY IF AT ALL], THIS SYSTEM IS SO FAR REMOVED FROM
/				NEWER RELEASES, THE ABILITY TO EVEN ACCESS "BIN" FROM
/				THESE EARLIER RELEASES IS ACTUALLY IMPOSSIBLE [AND WHY
/				THE DECTAPE HAD TO BE SEARCHED MANUALLY TO EVEN LOCATE
/				THE CODE!].  HOWEVER, THE NEWER DESIGN ELIMINATES
/				DEVICE-DEPENDENT CODE FROM THE "BIN" PROGRAM AND
/				PLACES THE COMPONENT ON AN IMPLIED SYSTEM BLOCK.
/				THUS, IF ALL SYSTEMS ARE WITHIN A CLOSE RANGE OF
/				RELEASE, THIS PROBLEM IS ELIMINATED AS THE SLURP
/				LOADER WILL ALWAYS BE READ FROM THE IMPLIED BLOCK ON
/				THE SYSTEM DEVICE LOGICAL UNIT FOR BOOTUP.

/				NOTE: THE '/I' DEVICE HANDLER RELOAD CODE HAS ALSO
/				BEEN LOCATED; THIS CAN BE WORKED ON ONCE THE SLURP
/				LOADER HAS BEEN COMPLETELY DOCUMENTED AT THE SOURCE
/				LEVEL, ETC.  [NOTE: FORTUNATELY, WHILE THESE BLOCK
/				ASSIGNMENTS HAVE CHANGED OVER THE YEARS, THE OVERALL
/				FUNCTIONALITY HAS NOT, ALTHOUGH A FEW MINOR
/				ADDITIONAL LIMITATIONS MAY APPLY THAT WILL REQUIRE
/				MODIFICATION FOR FUTURE RELEASES [AS WELL AS FOR USE
/				WITH THE PRESENT RELEASE VERSION 8Z].  ALSO, ANY
/				CHANGES MADE TO OVERCOME THE FATAL FLAW MAY INTERACT
/				WITH THIS ASPECT OF THE OPERATING SYSTEM AND THUS
/				AFFECT CERTAIN SPECIFICS OF THE RELOAD; THE RELOAD
/				CONCEPT INTERACTS THREE SPECIFIC PORTIONS OF THE
/				SYSTEM CONFIGURATION: 1) THE SPECIFICS OF THE SYSTEM
/				DEVICE HANDLER WHICH WILL BE UNDERGOING NECESSARY
/				CHANGES TO OVERCOME THE FATAL FLAW, 2) THE SLURP
/				LOADER SPECIFICS THAT WILL BE MODIED, AND 3) THE
/				RELOAD BLOCK ITSELF AS NECESSARY; ALL THREE MUST BE
/				UPDATED IN A COOPERATIVE EFFORT TO ACHIEVE A WORKABLE
/				RELEASE FOR ANY SYSTEM DEVICE THAT SUPPORTS A SLURP
/				LOADER.]  [NOTE: ALL TAPE-BASED SYSTEMS AND RX-BASED
/				SYSTEMS SUPPORT A SLURP LOADER AND FEW ELSE.  DEVICES
/				THAT RUN ON VERY FAST OR ONE-CYCLE DMA INTERFACES
/				MUST ALWAYS USE THE VIRTUAL LOADER WHICH IS ALWAYS AN
/				OPTION FOR ALL SYSTEMS [BUT MAY BE DEFAULT ON THESE
/				HIGHER-PERFORMANCE CONFIGURATIONS].]

/				THE REWIND AND UNLOAD ROUTINES ARE NOW ROUGHED OUT,
/				BUT NOT FLESHED OUT YET; THE DEVICE SIZE PATCH IS
/				NOW PRESENT.
/	25-FEB-2016	-	SLURP LOADER FOR THE TD8E WITH MR8EC ROM NOW RECREATED
/				IN SOURCE FORM [AND CONFIRMED CORRECT BY DIRECT BINARY
/				COMPARISON].  CERTAIN CONVENTIONS OF P?S/8 HAVE
/				CHANGED SLIGHTLY; MINOR EDITING WILL BE NECESSARY TO
/				UPDATE THE SOURCE CODE TO FULL COMPATIBILITY WITH
/				P?S/8 VERSION 8Z.  OTHER THAN THE CHANGE IN INTERNAL
/				SYSTEM CONVENTIONS, THE SOURCE CODE APPEARS TO BE
/				COMPLETELY FUNCTION AS INTENDED.  MOREOVER, MOST OF
/				THE CODE WILL BE EASILY ADAPTED TO THE P?S/8
/				CONFIGURATION FOR THE TD8E WHEN USED WITH EXTENDED
/				MEMORY [WITHOUT THE MD8E-C ROM].  CERTAIN P?S/8
/				GLOBAL DEFINITIONS ARE TEMPORARILY MODIFIED TO PREVENT
/				ASSEMBLY ERRORS.  [NOTE: ONCE THIS SOURCE FILE IS
/				COMPLETED WITH REGARD TO SOURCE CODE RECOVERY, IT WILL
/				BE ARCHIVED; ADDITIONAL CHANGES WILL BE MADE TO
/				ACHIEVE COMPATIBILITY WITH P?S/8 VERSION 8Z.]

/	29-FEB-2016	-	REWIND AND UNLOAD ROUTINE FULLY RECOVERED.  CERTAIN
/				DEFINITIONS ARE CONDITIONAL ON VERSION 8T.  ALTERNATE
/				DEFINITIONS ARE AVAILABLE TO UPGRADE THE SOURCE CODE
/				TO VERSION 8Z STANDARDS [ASSUMING OTHER CHANGES ARE
/				MADE TO CORRECT ALL RELATED ISSUES].

/				THE ALTERNATE STANDALONE BOOTSTRAP FOR THE TD8E IS NOW
/				SHORTER THAN THE DEC STANDARD TD8E BOOTSTRAP
/				REGARDLESS OF CONFIGURATION.  DUE TO THE DISCOVERY OF
/				THE FATAL FLAW IN THE BOOTSTRAP OPERATION OF THE
/				SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER [AND PROJECTED CHANGES TO BE
/				MADE TO REMEDY THE SITUATION], THIS CODE MAY BE
/				IRRELEVANT TO A CORRECTED RELEASE OF THIS SYSTEM.  IT
/				IS ESSENTIALLY EXTRANEOUS DUE TO THE BUILT-IN
/				BOOTSTRAP FUNCTION IN THE MR8E-C ROM; HOWEVER, THIS
/				BOOTSTRAP PROGRAM MAY BE SIGNIFICANT TO THE TD8E
/				SYSTEM REQUIRING EXTENDED MEMORY [WITHOUT THE MR8E-C
/				ROM].  AS SUCH, IT WAS TESTED ON THE PRESENT [FLAWED]
/				SYSTEM AND RETAINED FOR REFERENCE PURPOSES ONLY.
/				[NOTE: THE FLAWED SYSTEM WILL BOOT UP ONLY IF NO
/				CHANGES ARE MADE TO THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000 UNLESS
/				A KLUDGE IS APPLIED THAT MUST BE CALCULATED EACH AND
/				EVERY TIME A CHANGE IS MADE; THIS IS AN UNACCEPTABLE
/				SITUATION THAT WILL REQUIRE SIGNIFICANT REDESIGN.  BY
/				AVOIDING SUCH CHANGES FOR THE PRESENT, IT CAN BE
/				BOOTED BY ANY PROPERLY DESIGNED BOOTSTRAP ROUTINE.]

/	02-MAR-2016	-	SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER RELOADER CODE ROUGHED OUT.  SOME
/				COSMETIC CLEANUP, COMMENT CHANGES AND REPAGINATION.
/	05-MAR-2016	-	SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER RELOADER CODE FULLY RECOVERED.

/				SOME COMMENTS ADDED REGARDING FUTURE RELEASES; DESPITE
/				THE AMPLE CODE SPACE ALLOCATED FOR THE RELOADER, SOME
/				TECHNIQUES CAN BE IMPROVED TO BE MORE INLINE WITH THE
/				STANDARD OF PROGRAMMING USED IN OTHER ANALOGOUS CODE
/				FOR OTHER DEVICES.  THIS WILL BETTER SERVE AS AN
/				EXAMPLE OF HOW TO WRITE FUTURE RELOADER CODE FOR OTHER
/				DEVICES.

/				INTERACTION WITH THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY FOR THIS
/				SYSTEM WILL REQUIRE A LOT OF DISCOVERY AS THE OVERLAY
/				WAS IN A MORE PRIMITIVE FORM IN VERSION 8T; IT WAS
/				ONLY FOR THE VT8E AND NON-CONFIGURABLE.  MANY OF THE
/				SAME PRINCIPLES CARRIED OVER IN LATER DEVELOPMENT
/				YEARS, BUT IT IS NOT CLEAR IF ANY OF THIS WORK WAS
/				DONE ON THIS PARTICULAR SYSTEM [AS IT IS AN OPTION OF
/				P?S/8 AND IF NO PARTICULAR IMPROVEMENT IS SOUGHT, IT
/				COULD BE IGNORED.  HOWEVER, VIRTUALLY EVERY SUPPORTED
/				DEVICE HAS INCLUDED SOME FORM OF INTERACTION WITH THE
/				LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY; THE TD8E SYSTEM DEVICE
/				SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER CAN CLEARLY BENEFIT FROM THE
/				KIND OF ENHANCEMENT PROVIDED FOR OTHER DEVICES SUCH AS
/				THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE.

/				FROM THE RECOLLECTIONS OF CHARLES LASNER, IT WOULD
/				APPEAR SOME MEASURE OF OVERLAY SUPPORT WAS ADDED.
/				HOWEVER, IT IS POSSIBLE THIS IS A PRELIMINARY RELEASE
/				CREATED FOR EXPEDIENT PURPOSES;  THE CODE MAY SIMPLY
/				NOT BE PRESENT ON THIS PARTICULAR SYSTEM IMAGE FROM
/				THE RECOVERED DECTAPE.  MOREOVER, ESPECIALLY IN LIGHT
/				OF THE FATAL FLAW DISCOVERED IN THE LOGIC OF THE
/				SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER [PREVENTING MODIFICATION OF
/				BLOCK 0000 WITHOUT SOME EXTERNAL REMEDY APPLIED AFTER
/				THE FACT], IT IS LIKELY THAT AT LEAST SOME PORTION OF
/				THIS SUPPORT NEVER EXISTED BEFORE.  A COMPLETED FUTURE
/				RELEASE OF A SYSTEM FOR THE TD8E/MR8E-C ROM SHOULD
/				INCLUDE ALL SUCH IMPROVEMENTS [AS WELL AS RESOLVING
/				THE FATAL FLAW AND RECONCILING CERTAIN SYSTEM
/				DEFINITIONS THAT HAVE CHANGED OVER THE YEARS].
/				CERTAIN OF THESE CHANGES HAVE BEEN DOCUMENTED IN THE
/				SOURCE CODE IN THE FORM OF COMMENTS; HOWEVER,
/				ADDITIONAL WORK IS REQUIRED TO PRODUCE A COMPLETELY
/				FUNCTIONAL SYSTEM AS INTENDED [FOR VERSION 8Z AND
/				COMPATIBILITY WITH THE FUTURE REQUIREMENTS OF VERSION
/				9A WHICH IS CURRENTLY IN DESIGN DEVELOPMENT].

/				DEPENDING ON THE LEAD TIME FOR FURTHER WORK ON THIS
/				CONFIGURATION, A PROVISIONAL SYSTEM MAY BE PRODUCED TO
/				PROVIDE NOMINAL COMPATIBILITY WITH VERSION 8Z TO
/				ALLOW TESTING ON BOTH THE SIMH SIMULATOR AND ALSO ON
/				AVAILABLE HARDWARE.
/				IT IS BECOMING INCREASINGLY CLEAR THAT THIS SPECIFIC
/				CONFIGURATION CANNOT SUPPORT BOOTUP FROM DRIVE UNIT 1.
/				AS SUCH, REFERENCES TO THIS WILL EVENTUALLY BE REMOVED
/				FROM ALL SOURCE CODE.  [THE NON-ROM TD8E WITH EXTENDED
/				MEMORY SYSTEM VARIANT COULD STILL SUPPORT SUCH AN
/				EXTENDED BOOTSTRAP; WHERE CODE IS INCLUDED FOR FUTURE
/				USE IN DEVELOPING THAT ALTERNATIVE SYSTEM, DRIVE 1
/				REFERENCES WILL BE RETAINED BUT NOTED THAT THE CODE
/				IS PROVIDED [OR COMMENTED ON] PRESUMING THIS IS
/				POSSIBLE [IF ONLY IN THEORY].
/	P?S/8 SYSTEM EXTERNAL EQUATED SYMBOLS.

/	THE FOLLOWING SYMBOLS ARE SPECIFIC TO VERSION 8Z OF P?S/8 AND ARE SUBJECT TO
/	CHANGE IN ANY OTHER RELEASE.  [NOTE: MOST SYMBOLS ARE INHERENTLY STABLE AS
/	CORE DEFINITIONS OF THE SYSTEM; HOWEVER, OTHER SYMBOLS ARE DEPENDENT ON EITHER
/	SPECIFIC SYSTEM COMPONENTS OR SYSTEM GENERATION METHODS WHICH MAY CHANGE
/	CONSIDERABLY OR EVEN BECOME OBSOLETE.]

/	THE SYMBOLS ARE DEFINED IN A PRESCRIBED WAY: THE STARTING AND ENDING SYMBOLS
/	MUST BE WITHIN TWO COMMENTS WHICH START WITH '//'; NO OTHER FORM OF COMMENTARY
/	IS ALLOWED OTHER THAN A SINGLE COMMENT ON EACH DEFINITION LINE.

//	P?S/8 EQUATED SYMBOLS.

	ADRLST=	7603			/ZAP ADDRESS LIST.
	BBLEN=	0043			/SLURP LOADER BOOTSTRAP LENGTH.
	BCIF=	7734/ **** 8Z **** 7746	/SLURP LOADER STARTING FIELD LOCATION.
	BINADR=	0020			/"%" FILE STARTING BLOCK.
	BINIT=	0020			/SLURP INITIALIZE ROUTINE.
	BINITLN=0020			/LENGTH OF SLURP INITIALIZE CODE.
	BINIT2=	0031			/SECONDARY SLURP INITIALIZE ADDRESS.
	BISTRT=	7776			/DEFAULT STARTING ADDRESS IF '/I' OR '/V'.
	CALADR=	0020			/CALLING ADDRESS.
	CALFLD=	0021			/CALLING FIELD.
	CHLOOP=	1416			/CHARACTER INPUT ROUTINE.  **** 8U ONLY ****
	CNTLST=	7615			/CONTENTS LIST FOR ZAPPED ADDRESSES.
	DEVSIZE=6760			/DEVICE SIZE VALUE IN SYSGEN ROUTINE.
	DSPBLB=	0024			/BLURB DISPLAY ROUTINE.
	ERRTRP=	0046			/ERROR TRAP POINTER.
	ESTATUS=0077			/ERROR STATUS.
	FILBLB=	0023			/BLURB FILL ROUTINE.
	GENAD1=	7400			/SYSIO GENERATION ADDRESS.
	HIDWRD=	7600			/SYSTEM HANDLER ID WORD.
	IGEN=	4020			/I GENERATION FIELD AND ADDRESS.
	IOTRAP=	0045			/I/O TRAP POINTER.
	IOWAIT=	0047			/I/O WAIT POINTER.
	INSERT=	1305			/CHARACTER INSERT ROUTINE.
	L200=	0105			/CONSTANT 0200.
	L7=	0177			/CONSTANT 0007.
	L77=	0056/ **** 8Z **** 0103	/CONSTANT 0077.
	L7000=	0077			/CONSTANT 7000.  **** 8U ONLY ****
	L7756=	0064/ **** 8Z **** 0065	/CONSTANT 7756.
	L7770=	0040			/CONSTANT 7770.
	MONSTRT=0353			/KEYBOARD MONITOR STARTUP ADDRESS.
	MOVBLB=	0022			/ERROR BLURB MOVE ROUTINE.
	PCHLOOP=0162			/POINTER TO CHARACTER INPUT ROUTINE.
	REWIND=	1547/ **** 8Z **** 1335	/REWIND/UNLOAD ADDRESS.
	SBLOCK=	7635			/SYSTEM LOADER BLOCK ARGUMENT.
	SBOOT=	7600			/MONITOR RESTART ADDRESS.
	SBTFUN=	7602			/SYSTEM BOOTSTRAP FUNCTION.
	SCORE=	7633			/SYSTEM LOADER TRANSFER ADDRESS.
	SCRSIZE=7611			/SYSTEM MEMORY SIZE, ETC. WORD.
	SDATE=	7610			/SYSTEM LOW-ORDER DATE WORD.
	SFILES=	7757			/FILE LIST PASSED HERE.
	SFUN=	7634			/SYSTEM LOADER FUNCTION WORD.
	SLOAD=	7632			/SYSTEM LOADER ADDRESS.
	SLPGEN=	4420			/SLURP LOADER GENERATION FIELD AND ADDRESS.
	SLPSA=	7777			/SLURP LOADER STARTING ADDRESS WORD.
	SLUPAGE=2600			/SLURP LOADING PAGE.
	SOUTFLS=7607			/OUTPUT FILE COUNT PASSED HERE.
	SPARM=	7756			/EQUALS PARAMETER PASSED HERE.
	SWAL=	7604			/COMMAND-LINE OPTION SWITCHES '/A'-'/L'.
	SWMX=	7605			/COMMAND-LINE OPTION SWITCHES '/M'-'/X'.
	SWY9=	7606			/COMMAND-LINE OPTION SWITCHES '/Y'-'/9'.
	SYSIO=	7640			/SYSTEM I/O ENTRY POINT.
	SYSTART=7637			/SYSTEM LOADER STARTUP ADDRESS.
	TEMP1=	0100			/TEMPORARY.
	TRAPIO=	1041			/ENTRY POINT FOR I/O TRAPS.
	TRPERR=	1200			/ENTRY POINT FOR I/O ERROR TRAPS.
	TRPGEN=	1020			/TRAP ROUTINE GENERATION FIELD AND ADDRESS.
	UNIT=	0000			/KEYBOARD MONITOR UNIT.
	WAITIO=	1110			/ENTRY POINT FOR I/O WAIT TRAPS.
	WRITE=	4000			/SYSIO WRITE BIT.
	ZAPLST=	7627			/REPLACEMENT LIST FOR ZAPPED ADDRESSES.

//	END OF P?S/8 EQUATED SYMBOLS.

/	TD8E SIMPLE DECTAPE INPUT/OUTPUT DEFINITIONS.

	SDCODE=	77			/DEVICE CODE FOR TD8E SIMPLE DECTAPE [PRIMARY].

	SDSS=	SDCODE^10+6001		/SIMPLE DECTAPE SKIP ON SINGLE LINE FLAG.
	SDST=	SDCODE^10+6002		/SIMPLE DECTAPE SKIP IF TIME ERROR FLAG SET.
	SDSQ=	SDCODE^10+6003		/SIMPLE DECTAPE SKIP ON QUAD LINE FLAG.
	SDLC=	SDCODE^10+6004		/SIMPLE DECTAPE LOAD COMMAND REGISTER.
	SDLD=	SDCODE^10+6005		/SIMPLE DECTAPE LOAD DATA REGISTER.
	SDRC=	SDCODE^10+6006		/SIMPLE DECTAPE READ COMMAND REGISTER.
	SDRD=	SDCODE^10+6007		/SIMPLE DECTAPE READ DATA REGISTER.

/	NUMERIC LOAD DEFINITIONS.

	NL0001=	CLA IAC			/LOAD AC WITH 0001.
/	NL0002=	CLA CLL CML RTL		/LOAD AC WITH 0002.
	NL2000=	CLA CLL CML RTR		/LOAD AC WITH 2000.
	NL4000=	CLA CLL CML RAR		/LOAD AC WITH 4000.
/	NL7775=	CLA CLL CMA RTL		/LOAD AC WITH 7775.
	NL7776=	CLA CLL CMA RAL		/LOAD AC WITH 7776.
/	NL7777=	CLA CMA			/LOAD AC WITH 7777.

/	MISCELLANEOUS EQUATED SYMBOLS.

/	SBOFF=	SLUPAGE-SBOOT		/OFFSET VALUE FOR SLURP LOADER.
	SYSBLK=	0000			/SYSTEM BOOTSTRAP BLOCK.
	SYSNUM=	0014			/SYSTEM ID NUMBER; **** MUST BE UNIQUE. ****
	TDSIZE=	2702			/STANDARD DECTAPE BLOCK LENGTH.
/	SLURP FORMAT CONTROL DEFINITIONS.

	DA=	0			/DATA WORD.	
	EF=	1			/END-OF-FILE.
	FL=	3			/FIELD SETTING.
	OR=	2			/ORIGIN SETTING.

/	ROM ADDRESS DEFINITIONS.

	F2SET=	7552			/CONTINUATION OF FUNCTION CALL ADDRESS.
	ROMBOOT=7470			/ROM-BASED BOOTSTRAP ROUTINE ADDRESS.
	ROMFLD=	70			/ROM LOADS INTO FIELD 7.
	ROMGO=	7405			/RETRY ADDRESS AFTER ERRORS.
	READLP=	7677			/READ DATA WORD LOOP ADDRESS IN THE ROM.

/	ROM-DEFINED LOCATIONS WITHIN THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.

	BUFADR=	7751			/READ/WRITE BUFFER ADDRESS.
	BUFPTR=	7753			/BUFFER POINTER FOR READ AND STORE ROUTINES.
	CIFR=	7674			/INTERNAL RETURN ADDRESS FOR WRITING.
	DRVUNIT=7731			/DECTAPE DRIVE UNIT.
	EQUFUN=	7704			/EQUIVALENCE CHECKSUM ROUTINE.
	EQUTMP=	7750			/TEMPORARY FOR EQUIVALENCE CHECKSUM ROUTINE.
	GETWRD=	7647			/GET A WORD FROM THE CALLER'S BUFFER ROUTINE.
	PAGCNT=	7752			/TRANSFER PAGE COUNT.
	PUTWRD=	7654			/PUT A WORD INTO THE CALLER'S BUFFER ROUTINE.
	RDQUAD=	7676			/READ A 12-BIT WORD ROUTINE.
	SBLK=	7734			/LATEST DECTAPE BLOCK NUMBER.
	SCKSUM=	7733			/RUNNING CHECKSUM WORD.
	SERR=	7661			/ERROR HANDLER ADDRESS.
	SEXIT=	7722			/ROM EXIT WHEN FINISHED.
	SFUNCTI=7732			/FUNCTION WORD.
	SUNIT=	7735			/DRIVE UNIT FOR LATEST HANDLER CALL.
	TRYCNT=	7730			/RETRY COUNTER.
	WORDCT=	7754			/WORD COUNTER FOR LATEST DATA BLOCK.
	WRQ=	7724			/WRITE QUAD ROUTINE ROM CONTINUATION ADDRESS.
	WRQUAD=	7671			/WRITE A 12-BIT WORD ROUTINE.
/	TD8E BOOTSTRAP METHODS.

/	1.	MR8E-C ROM TD8E BOOTSTRAP.

/	ASSUMING THE MR8E-C ROM IS AVAILABLE [WHICH IS A REQUIRMEMENT OF THIS SYSTEM
/	CONFIGURATION], THE FOLLOWING IS THE SHORTEST WAY TO BOOTSTRAP ANY COMPATIBLE
/	TD8E OPERATING SYSTEM [ON LOGICAL UNIT 0 ONLY] BY THE USE OF AN EXECUTABLE
/	PROGRAM.  [USING THE FRONT PANEL SWITCH REGISTER TO BOOTSTRAP THE SYSTEM IS
/	ESSENTIALLY THE MANUAL EQUIVALENT OF THIS PROGRAM.]

	NOPUNCH				/PREVENT BINARY OUTPUT; THIS SECTION IS FOR
					/DOCUMENTATION PURPOSES ONLY.

	*200				/ANYWHERE ON ANY PAGE OF ANY FIELD.

/	CLA				/THE AC MUST BE CLEAR.
/	IOF				/INTERRUPTS MUST BE OFF.
/	CDF	XX			/THE DATA FIELD IS IGNORED.
	CIF	70			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	.+1;	ROMBOOT		/START THE BOOTSTRAP WITHIN THE ROM.

/	NOTE: THIS BOOTSTRAP CAN BE SHORTENED BY ONE INSTRUCTION BY LOADING ANYWHERE
/	ON PAGE 36 [7400-7577].  THE "JMP I" INSTRUCTION CAN THEN BE REPLACED BY
/	"JMP ROMBOOT"; THE ADDRESS POINTER TO "ROMBOOT" BECOMES SUPERFLUOUS.

/	2.	STANDARD DEC TD8E BOOTSTRAP.

	NOPUNCH				/PREVENT BINARY OUTPUT; THIS SECTION IS FOR
					/DOCUMENTATION PURPOSES ONLY.

	FIELD	0			/LOADS IN FIELD 0.

	*7300				/THIS IS WHERE THE STANDALONE BOOTSTRAP LOADS.

K1000,	TAD	GET/(2000)		/GET REVERSE DIRECTION BIT.  ALSO CONSTANT
					/1000 [ONLY THE HIGH-ORDER FOUR BITS ARE USED].
	JMS	GET			/LOCATE REVERSE END ZONE.
	JMS	GET			/LOOK FOR 31 CODE GOING FORWARD.
RD,	SDSQ				/QUAD FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, READ IT IN.
	DCA I	WCT			/STORE IT INTO MEMORY.
	ISZ	WCT			/BUMP TO NEXT; ALL DONE YET?
	JMP	RD			/NO, KEEP GOING.

/	AT THIS POINT BLOCK 0000 HAS BEEN READ INTO 07360-07557 AND BLOCK 0001 HAS
/	BEEN READ INTO 07600-07777.  [NOTE: ONLY OS/8 AND COS-300/310 REQUIRE THE
/	BLOCK OOO1 DATA LOAD; EDU-SYSTEM 15/30 AND P?S/8  ONLY REQUIRE BLOCK 0000
/	LOADING INTO MEMORY.]  FOR ALL SUPPORTED SYSTEMS, THE OFFSET LOADING INTO
/	FIELD 0 7360-7557 WILL BE EXPECTED AND DEALT WITH ACCORDINGLY.

	JMP I	STRT/(STARTIT)		/GO START IT UP.
/	GET TO DESIRED MARK WINDOW VALUE ROUTINE.  [NOTE: INITIALIZED TO SAVE A WORD.]

GET,	2000				/GET TO DESIRED MARK ROUTINE.
	TAD	K1000/(1000)		/ADD ON UNIT [0] AND GO BITS TO DIRECTION BIT
					/PASSED IN AC.  NOTE: LOW-ORDER BITS IGNORED.
	SDLC				/LOAD COMMAND REGISTER TO START THE TAPE MOVING.
MRKSRCH,SDSS				/NEW LINE IN?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRC				/YES, READ THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	AND	K77/(77)		/JUST MARK WINDOW BITS.

/	THE FIRST TIME THIS ROUTINE IS USED, THE REVERSE END ZONE MARK VALUE IS
/	SEARCHED FOR; THE SECOND TIME, THE 31 CODE IS SEARCHED FOR.

MARKSEL,TAD	MRKVALUE		/COMPARE TO DESIRED [END MARK OR 31 CODE].
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF IT MATCHES.
	JMP	MRKSRCH			/GO BACK IF NOT.
	ISZ	MARKSEL			/BUMP TO NEXT MARK CODE FOR NEXT TIME.
	JMP I	GET			/RETURN TO CALLER.

WCT,	BOOTBUFFER			/WORD COUNT AND STORE POINTER.

MRKVALU,-22;	-31			/REVERSE END AND 31 CODE MARK CONSTANTS.
K77,	77				/CONSTANT 0077.
STRT,	STARTIT				/WHERE TO START THE BOOT CODE READ IN.

	ZBLOCK	7354-.			/EMPTY SPACE.

	BOOTBUF=.			/DECTAPE BLOCK 0000 CONTENTS START LOADING HERE.

	ZBLOCK	4			/THESE LOCATIONS ARE LOADED WITH PRE-DATA WORDS.

	BLKDATA=.			/THE DATA WORDS FROM BLOCK 0000 LOAD HERE.

	ZBLOCK	7400-.			/THE FIRST 0020 WORDS OF BLOCK 0000 LOAD HERE.

	STARTIT=.			/THE REST OF THE WORDS IN BLOCK 0000 LOAD
/					HERE.  CONTROL IS TRANSFERRED HERE AFTERWARDS.
/					ALL /COMPATIBLE OPERATING SYSTEMS NEED TO BE
/					AWARE OF THIS OFFSET LOADING AND PROVIDE A
					/SYSTEM-SPECIFIC PROCESS TO CONTINUE THE OVERALL
					/OPERATING SYSTEM LOADING PROCESS.
/	NOTES:

/	1)  THE STANDARD DEC TD8E BOOTSTRAP ALSO LOADS THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0001 INTO
/	    07600-07777; THIS IS ONLY MEANINGFUL TO OS/8 AND COS-300/310.

/	2)  THE MR8E-C ROM BOOTSTRAP FUNCTIONS IDENTICALLY TO THE STANDARD DEC TD8E
/	    BOOTSTRAP; CLEARLY THE STANDARD DEC TD8E BOOTSTRAP IS THE TEMPLATE FOR THE
/	    DESIGN OF THE ROM-BASED ROUTINES.

/	3)  THE STANDARD DEC TD8E BOOTSTRAP IS NOT WELL DEVELOPED.  IT CAN BE MADE
/	    MORE ROBUST BY CHANGING THE "K1000" SYMBOLIC REFERENCE TO THE WORD AT
/	    "GET+1" [WHICH WOULD MAKE IT SELF-REFERENTIAL].  THE ONLY REQUIREMENT OF
/	    THE CONSTANT USED IS THE HIGH-ORDER FOUR BITS ARE 0, 0, 1, 0; AS WRITTEN,
/	    EITHER LOCATION ACCOMPLISHES THIS; ONCE THIS CHANGE IS MADE, ADDITIONAL
/	    CHANGES ARE POSSIBLE TO MAKE THIS PROGRAM MORE ROBUST [BY ITSELF, THIS
/	    CHANGE IS MERELY INNOCUOUS].

/	4)  ASSUMING 3) ABOVE HAS BEEN PERFORMED, THE INSTRUCTION AT 07300 CAN BE
/	    CHANGED TO "CLA CLL CML RTR" WHICH LOADS THE ACCUMULATOR WITH 2000 [AKA
/	    "NL2000"].  THIS CHANGE HAS THE ADDED BENEFIT OF ENSURING THE PROPER
/	    CONTENTS OF THE AC AS THE PROGRAM STARTS, WITHOUT RELIANCE ON POWER CLEAR
/	    CONSIDERATIONS [WHICH COULD BE IMPROPERLY CARRIED OUT BY USERS UNFAMILIAR
/	    WITH THE PROCESS].

/	5)  ASSUMING 3) AND 4) ABOVE HAVE BEEN PERFORMED, THE CONTENTS OF "GET" BECOME
/	    UNIMPORTANT.  THIS ALLOWS THE FRONT PANEL OPERATIONS NEEDED TO TOGGLE IN
/	    THIS CODE TO BE ONE OPERATION SHORTER [THE PREVIOUS VALUE CAN BE LEFT ON
/	    THE FRONT PANEL SWITCHES THEN PRESS DEPOSIT TWICE].

/	IT IS CLEAR THIS BOOTSTRAP WAS NOT OPTIMIZED AS IS USUALLY THE CASE WITH OTHER
/	DEC BOOTSTRAP CODING; HOWEVER, IT IS KNOWN THAT AT THE TIME THE TD8E WAS FIRST
/	PRODUCED, THERE WAS WIDESPREAD PROGRAMMER APATHY OR EVEN OPEN DISDAIN FOR THE
/	DEVICE.  THE NON-ROM VERSION OF A DEVICE HANDLER FOR OS/8 TO SUPPORT THE TD8E
/	[THAT REQUIRES AT LEAST 12K OF MEMORY] IS DOCUMENTED IN SURVIVING OS/8 SOURCE
/	FILES AS "THE TD8E PROBLEM".  THIS ALSO IMPACTED NEGATIVELY ON SUBSEQUENT
/	DEVICE HANDLERS ALSO REQUIRING AT LEAST 12K OF MEMORY; THE HANDLERS FOR THESE
/	NEWER DEVICES WERE PRAGMATICALLY WRITTEN TO SUPERFICIALLY MIMIC QUIRKS OF THE
/	TD8E HANDLER.  THIS BECAME NECESSARY TO ALLOW CERTAIN PROGRAMS TO HANDLE "THE
/	TD8E PROBLEM" GENERICALLY EVEN IF THIS REQUIREMENT WAS DETRIMENTAL TO THE
/	HANDLERS FOR THESE OTHER DEVICES.

/	IT WAS SUGGESTED TO THE PDP-8 PROGRAMMING STAFF THAT OS/8 BE REWRITTEN TO
/	CONFORM TO THE EMERGING P?S/8 MEMORY MANAGEMENT STANDARD USED IN ALL P?S/8
/	RELEASES FROM THAT ERA TO THE PRESENT; THEY CONSIDERED IT A GOOD IDEA BUT TOO
/	MUCH WORK TO BOTHER WITH.  AS SUCH, THE RELEVANT RELEASES OF OS/8 BASIC AND
/	FORTRAN IV HAVE WEAKNESSES WITHIN THEM DIRECTLY TRACEABLE TO THIS "PROBLEM".
/	3.	P?S/8 TD8E BOOTSTRAP.

/	THIS BOOTSTRAP IS ONLY FOR P?S/8 SYSTEMS BASED ON THE TD8E DECTAPE CONTROLLER.
/	THE STANDARD DEC TD8E BOOTSTRAP WILL ALSO WORK; HOWEVER, IT CANNOT ACCESS
/	DRIVE UNIT 1 AS AN ALTERNATE BOOTUP DEVICE CHOICE.  [NOTE: THE CODE PRESENTED
/	HERE DOES NOT ACTUALLY BOOTUP DRIVE UNIT 1; IT IS HOPED THAT FUTURE RELEASES
/	OF P?S/8 FOR TD8E WILL BE COMPATIBLE WITH THIS CODE WITH SUITABLE CHANGES MADE
/	THAT WILL SUPPORT DRIVE UNIT 1.]  [NOTE: SUPPORT FOR DRIVE UNIT 1 BOOTUP IN
/	THE P?S/8 TD8E/MR8E-C CONFIGURATION IS CURRENTLY UNDER DEVELOPMENT; IT MAY
/	PROVE IMPOSSIBLE GIVEN THE NEED TO SOLVE THE FATAL FLAW IN THE PRESENT CODE AS
/	WAS RECOVERED FROM THE SPECIFIC DECTAPE IN QUESTION.  THIS BOOTSTRAP MAY
/	BECOME WORTHLESS AS THE FATAL FLAW IS REMOVED, OR PERHAPS MIGHT BE USEFUL
/	WHERE IT IS POSSIBLY NEEDED, NAMELY ON THE TD8E CONFIGURATION WITH 8K OR MORE
/	MEMORY WITHOUT THE MR8E-C ROM; FOR THE PRESENT IT IS RETAINED FOR REFERENCE
/	PURPOSES ONLY.]

/	THE STANDARD DEC TD8E BOOTSTRAP IS SELFISHLY DESIGNED TO ALLOW OS/8 BOOTUP
/	EASIER AT THE EXPENSE OF OTHER SYSTEMS COMPATIBLE WITH THIS CONVENTION; AS
/	SUCH, IT IS NEEDLESSLY INFLEXIBLE.  [NUANCES OF OTHER SYSTEMS SUCH AS MULTIPLE
/	DRIVE UNIT SUPPORT CANNOT BE IMPLEMENTED.]

/	THE STANDARD DEC TD8E BOOTSTRAP LOADS THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0001 INTO
/	07600-07777 WHERE OS/8 [AND COS-300/310] REQUIRE THIS; THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK
/	0000 ARE LOADED INTO 07360-07557 WHICH IS OF NO PARTICULAR ADVANTAGE TO ANY
/	SUPPORTED SYSTEM [AND TENDS TO CAUSE NEEDLESS BOOTUP COMPLEXITY].  AS SUCH,
/	FOR ALL SUPPORTED SYSTEMS [INCLUDING OS/8 AND COS-300/310], WHATEVER ASPECT OF
/	BLOCK 0000 REQUIRING PROPER PAGE ALIGNMENT [GENERALLY TO 7600-7777, AND IN THE
/	CASES OF OS/8 AND COS-300/310 INTO FIELD 1] MIGHT BE NEEDED, IT IS NOT
/	PROVIDED.  IT IS NECESSARY TO COPY THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000 TO THE PROPER
/	MEMORY LOCATIONS VIA ADDITIONAL CODING WITHIN EACH SYSTEMS DEVICE HANDLER
/	STRUCTURE.

/	IN THE CASE OF THE P?S/8 TD8E/MR8E-C SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER, THIS REQUIRES
/	HANDLER CODING TO SUPPORT THE CIRCUMSTANCES OF THIS ODD RELOCATION; MEASURES
/	ARE THEN TAKEN TO UNDO SPECIFIC CORRUPTION CAUSED BY THE MISALIGNMENT.
/	AFTERWARDS, THE HANDLER CODE IS COPIED TO THE INTENDED MEMORY LOCATIONS.  DUE
/	TO THE LIMITATIONS OF THE DEC STANDARD TD8E BOOTSTRAP, ONLY DEVICE UNIT 0 CAN
/	BE ACCESSED AT BOOTUP TIME.

/	THE P?S/8-SPECIFIC BOOTSTRAP CORRECTS THESE PROBLEMS BY PROPERLY LOADING THE
/	ENTIRE BLOCK 0000 CONTENTS DIRECTLY INTO 07600-07777 AS INTENDED.  THIS WILL
/	ALLOW FUTURE RELEASES TO POTENTIALLY SUPPORT BOOTING TO DRIVE UNIT 1
/	[ASSUMING PROPER REVISIONS ARE MADE TO ALL AFFECTED COMPONENTS].  THE PRESENT
/	VERSION OF THIS BOOTSTRAP IS SEVERAL WORDS SHORTER THAN THE DEC BOOTSTRAP.

/	NOTE: THE BUILT-IN BOOTSTRAP WITHIN THE MR8E-C ROM IS CONSISTENT WITH THE
/	DEC STANDARD TD8E BOOTSTRAP IN ALL RELEVANT WAYS.  ONLY THE P?S/8 TD8E
/	BOOTSTRAP HAS THE POTENTIAL TO ACCESS DRIVE UNIT 1 AS DESCRIBED ABOVE.
	NOPUNCH				/PREVENT BINARY OUTPUT; THIS SECTION IS FOR
					/DOCUMENTATION PURPOSES ONLY.

	*7544				/STARTING ADDRESS OF BOOT CODE.

/	NOTE: THE FIRST WORD BELOW NEED NOT BE LOADED INTO MEMORY IF THE BOOTSTRAP
/	PROGRAM IS STARTED AT "BUTSTRT" [07562].  IT IS INCLUDED TO CAUSE THE LOWEST
/	LOADING ADDRESS TO BE AN ALTERNATE STARTING ADDRESS; THIS WILL MINIMIZE THE
/	INFORMATION NEEDED FOR AN AVERAGE USER TO SUCCESSFULLY START P?S/8 FOR THE
/	TD8E.  [NOTE: IT IS HOPED THIS BOOTSTRAP WILL BE COMPATIBLE WITH THE P?S/8
/	TD8E CONFIGURATION FOR USE WITH 8K OR LARGER SYSTEMS THAT DO NOT INCLUDE THE
/	MR8E-C ROM.]

GTMARK,	JMP	BUTSTRT			/JUMP TO REAL START.
GT1000,	TAD	GT1000/(1000)		/ADD ON UNIT 0, GO BIT TO PASSED DIRECTION.
	SDLC				/LOAD COMMAND REGISTER TO START THE TAPE MOVING.
GTSRCH,	SDSS				/NEW LINE IN?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRC				/YES, READ THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	AND	GT77/(77)		/JUST MARK TRACK BITS.
TADGTCM,TAD	GTCMPVALUE		/GET END MARK [OR 40 MARK] COMPARISON VALUE.
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF IT MATCHES.
	JMP	GTSRCH			/GO BACK IF NO MATCH.
	ISZ	TADGTCMP		/LOOK FOR 40 CODE SECOND TIME THROUGH.
	JMP I	GTMARK			/RETURN TO CALLER.

GT77,	77				/CONSTANT 0077.
GTCT,	SBOOT-1				/WORD COUNT AND STORE POINTER.

/	THE BOOTSTRAP STARTS HERE [OR JUMPED TO FROM THE LOWEST LOADING ADDRESS].

BUTSTRT,NL2000				/SET REVERSE DIRECTION BIT.
	JMS	GTMARK			/REWIND TAPE 0 INTO THE END ZONE.
	JMS	GTMARK			/READ FORWARD TO 40 MARK.
GTLOOP,	SDSQ				/QUAD LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, GET A WORD.
	DCA I	GTCT			/STORE INTO BOOT AREA [MINUS ONE].
	ISZ	GTCT			/DONE ENOUGH?
	JMP	GTLOOP			/NO, GO DO ANOTHER ONE.
	SDLC				/YES, STOP THE TAPE.
	SKP				/SKIP TO THE SMA SZA CLA CONSTANT.
GTCMPVA,-22				/REVERSE END ZONE MARK CONSTANT.
	SMA SZA CLA			/40 CODE MARK CONSTANT; THIS WILL SKIP THE NEXT.

/	THIS IS THE END OF THE BOOTSTRAP CODE TO BE LOADED. THE NEXT LOCATION WILL BE
/	SKIPPED BECAUSE THE CONTENTS ARE COPIED FROM THE BOOTUP DECTAPE MEDIA AND ARE
/	NOT TRUSTWORTHY.  [THE CONTENTS OF PART OF THE REVERSE PCC MARK DATA IS NOW
/	LOADED INTO 07577; THIS VALUE IS OFTEN 7777.]  THE NEXT LOCATION TO BE
/	EXECUTED IS 07600 WHICH NOW CONTAINS THE CONTENTS OF THE FIRST DATA WORD [AND
/	IS FOLLLOWED BY THE REST OF THE DATA WORDS READ IN FROM BLOCK 0000.]

AT7577,	.-.				/THIS GETS STORED INTO.

/	THE CODE FALLS THROUGH INTO THE BOOTUP CODE READ IN FROM THE DECTAPE.  AS
/	APPROPRIATE, THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER MAY BE MODIFIED TO BOOT TO DRIVE UNIT 1
/	[ASSUMING THIS IS SUPPORTED].

	SBOOT=	.			/SYSTEM BOOTS HERE.

	ENPUNCH				/RESTORE NORMAL BINARY OUTPUT.

/	NOTE: THIS BOOTSTRAP MAY NOT BE FEASIBLE DEPENDING ON THE INTERNALS OF THE
/	P?S/8 TD8E HANDLER WHICH IS UNDERGOING DESIGN CHANGES; IT IS CONCEIVABLE THAT
/	ELEMENTS OF THIS BOOTSTRAP MAY BECOME RELEVANT TO THE NON-ROM CONFIGURATION.
/	HOWEVER, IT HAS BEEN TESTED WITH THE RECOVERED [BUT FLAWED] TD8E/MR8E-C
/	DECTAPE WHICH USES A METHOD OF BOOTUP THAT IS COMPATIBLE WITH THIS BOOTSTRAP
/	[AS WELL AS THE STANDARD DEC TD8E BOOTSTRAP, WHICH IS LONGER].
/	TD8E/MR8E-C DECTAPE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.

	FIELD	0			/WHERE THIS LOADS WHEN GENERATED/EXECUTED.

	*GENAD1				/REQUIRED FOR SYSTEM GENERATION PURPOSES.

	RELOC	SBOOT			/WHERE THE CODE EXECUTES

SBOOT,	JMS	SYSIO			/CALL SYSTEM I/O ROUTINES TO BOOTUP.
	SBOOT				/READ INTO 07600 AND ONWARD.
SBTFUN,	20^100+.-.			/FUNCTION WORD; LOW-ORDER UNIT BIT MAY BE SET.
	SYSBLK				/BLOCK 0000 AND ONWARD.

/	ALTHOUGH SEEMINGLY A SUBROUTINE CALL, THERE IS NO ACTUAL RETURN TO THE CALLER.
/	ONCE INITIATED, DATA TRANSFERS OVERLAY MEMORY; KEY CONTROLLING ADDRESSES AND
/	OTHER PARAMETERS READ IN FROM BLOCK 0000 TAKE CONTROL OF THE OVERALL PROCESS
/	IN MEMORY.  THE ACTUAL TRANSFER IS 07600-03577 [USING ADDRESS WRAP-AROUND
/	KNOWN TO CONTINUE IN FIELD 0].  THE FUNCTION WORD IS SET TO READ 20 BLOCKS
/	INTO MEMORY [BLOCKS 0000-0017].  THIS INITIALIZES THE KEYBOARD MONITOR [AND
/	BUILT-IN LINE EDITOR], THE RESIDENT COPY OF THE TFS DIRECTORY AND THE KEYBOARD
/	MONITOR TRANSIENT COMMAND AREA.  [NOTE: FINALIZATION OF THESE SYSTEM
/	COMPONENTS IS PERFORMED WITHIN THE KEYBOARD MONITOR.]

/	ONCE COMPLETELY LOADED [AND MOVED BY THE KEYBOARD MONITOR FOR CERTAIN AREAS]
/	THE MEMORY LAYOUT IS AS FOLLOWS:

 /	BLOCK[S]	  MEMORY	FUNCTION

/	  0000		07600-07777	THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.

/	0001-0014	00000-02777	THE RESIDENT KEYBOARD MONITOR AND LINE EDITOR.

/	   N/A		03000-06777	INTERNAL FILE BUFFER FOR THE LINE EDITOR.

/	0015-0016	07000-07377	CACHED COPY OF THE CURRENT UNIT TFS DIRECTORY.

/	  0017		07400-07577	THE TRANSIENT AREA OF THE KEYBOARD MONITOR.

/	NOTE: THE TRANSIENT AREA SERVES SEVERAL PURPOSES.  THE CONTENTS AND USAGE
/	DEPENDS ON VARIOUS COMMANDS.

SWAL,	.-.				/COMMAND-LINE OPTION SWITCHES '/A'-'/L'.
SWMX,	.-.				/COMMAND-LINE OPTION SWITCHES '/M'-'/X'.
SWY9,	.-.				/COMMAND-LINE OPTION SWITCHES '/Y'-'/9'.
SOUTFLS,2316/.-.			/OUTPUT FILE COUNT ETC. WORD.
SDATE,	2316/.-.			/SYSTEM DATE WORD.
SCRSIZE,4000/.-.			/SYSTEM CORE SIZE.
/	NOTES ON OUTPUT FILE COUNT, SYSTEM DATE AND SYSTEM CORE SIZE WORDS:

/	THE OUTPUT FILE COUNT WORD WILL BE REDEFINED IN P?S/8 VERSION 9A.  AS OF
/	VERSION 8Z, BITS[7-11] CONTAIN THE COUNT OF OUTPUT FILES IN THE PASSED FILE
/	LIST; HOWEVER, BITS[0-6] MUST BE 0000000.  BITS[1-6] WILL BECOME THE
/	HIGH-ORDER SIX BITS OF A REDESIGNED DATE WORD THAT COVERS 512 YEARS DIRECTLY.
/	BIT[0] WILL DETERMINE THE STATUS OF THE FILE AND COMMUNICATIONS AREA OF THE
/	OPTIONAL P?S/8 SHELL [CURRENTLY UNDER DEVELOPMENT].  AS SUCH, ALL CONFORMING
/	PROGRAMS MUST MASK OFF BITS[0-6] TO OBTAIN THE OUTPUT FILE COUNT.

/	THE FORMAT OF THE SYSTEM DATE WORD WILL BE MODIFIED TO FORM AN 18-BIT INTEGER
/	WHEN COMBINED WITH BITS[1-6] OF THE OUTPUT FILE COUNT WORD.  THIS WILL REQUIRE
/	ALL PROGRAMS THAT ACCESS THE SYSTEM DATE TO BE REWRITTEN.  THIS IS ALSO
/	SCHEDULED FOR IMPLEMENTATION IN P?S/8 VERSION 9A.

/	THE CORE SIZE WORD CONTAINS SEVERAL SETS OF MEMORY AND OTHER STATUS BITS:

/	BITS[0-2] CONTAIN THE PHYSICAL SIZE OF MEMORY OF THE SYSTEM.

/	BITS[3-5] CONTAIN THE MAXIMUM SIZE THE LOGICAL CORE SIZE CAN BE SET TO.  THIS
/	COULD DIFFER FROM THE PHYSICAL SIZE FOR SEVERAL REASONS SUCH AS THE PRESENCE
/	OF THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY IN THE PHYSICAL MAXIMUM FIELD, OR THE SYSTEM
/	DEVICE HANDLER REQUIRES AN EXTENSION SUCH AS IN THE CASE OF THE TD8E SYSTEM
/	THAT REQUIRES 8K OF MEMORY OR MORE [BUT DOES NOT USE THE MR8E-C ROM].

/	BITS[6-8] CONTAIN THE CURRENT LOGICAL CORE SIZE.  THIS VALUE CAN BE SET TO
/	ANY VALUE FROM 0 UP THROUGH THAT FOUND IN THE MAXIMUM LOGICAL SIZE STORED IN
/	BITS[3-5].  THIS ALLOWS PROGRAMS TO BE TESTED IN RESTRICTED CONFIGURATIONS TO
/	MIMIC THE LIMITATIONS OF SMALLER MACHINES OR TO PROTECT THE CONTENTS OF MEMORY
/	FOR USER-DEFINED PURPOSES.

/	BIT[9] IS SET IF THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER REQUIRES A DEVICE EXTENSION.  THE
/	DEVICE EXTENSION OCCUPIES LOCATIONS 6000-7777 OF THE HIGHEST PHYSICAL FIELD OF
/	MEMORY.  [NOTE: CERTAIN SYSTEM PROGRAMS SUCH AS P?S/8 PAL CAN ALLOCATE
/	LOCATIONS 0000-5777 OF THE HIGHEST PHYSICAL FIELD IN CASE THE LOGICAL CONSOLE
/	IS DISABLED AND THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER REQUIRES THE EXTENSION.]

/	BIT[10] IS SET IF THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY IS ACTIVE.  ENTRY POINTS ARE
/	IMPLIED WITH RESPECT TO SPECIFIC 12-BIT ADDRESSES; THE FIELD IS DEFINED AS ONE
/	FIELD MORE THAN THE FIELD VALUE IN BITS[3-5].  WHILE THIS IS GENERALLY
/	IDENTICAL TO THE FIELD REPRESENTED BY THE PHYSICAL CORE SIZE, THIS METHOD
/	ALLOWS MULTIPLE MEMORY FIELDS TO BE RESERVED FOR UNUSUAL CONFIGURATIONS.

/	BIT[11] IS THE LOGICAL ABORT SWITCH THAT ANY PROGRAM CAN SET TO SIGNAL PROGRAM
/	EXIT BY ABORT [GENERALLY CONTROL-C DETECTED ON THE SYSTEM CONSOLE DEVICE].
/	WHILE COMPATIBLE PDP-8 CONSOLE HARDWARE CAN PROVIDE THIS INFORMATION VIA THE
/	KEYBOARD FLAG BEING RAISED [AND THE KEYBOARD BUFFER CONTAINING THE CONTROL-C
/	CHARACTER], DECMATES ARE NOT CAPABLE OF THIS DUE TO THE INCOMPATIBLE KEYBOARD
/	HARDWARE ON ALL MODELS.  BY SETTING BIT[11] AS NECESSARY, FULLY CONFORMING
/	PROGRAMS CAN BE WRITTEN TO COMPLETELY FUNCTION ON ALL PDP-8 AND DECMATE MODELS
/	SUPPORTED.  [NOTE: P?S/8 ONLY SUPPORTS "FAMILY OF 8" MODELS WHICH EXPLICITLY
/	EXCLUDE THE PDP-5 AND PDP-8/S; THESE MACHINES ARE NOT COMPATIBLE WITH THE
/	ORIGINAL PDP-8 MODEL RELEASED IN 1965 WHICH IS CONSIDERED THE MINIMUM SYSTEM
/	THAT CAN BE SUPPORTED IN P?S/8 AND ALSO OS/8 WITH SUFFICIENT MEMORY.]
/	COMES HERE WITH INTERRUPTS OFF TO CONTINUE PROCESSING THE LATEST SYSTEM DEVICE
/	HANDLER CALL.

SMORE,	RDF				/GET THE CALLER'S FIELD.
	TAD	SCIDF/(CIF CDF)		/TURN INTO CIF CDF RETURN FIELD INSTRUCTION.
	DCA	SFLDXIT			/STORE INLINE FOR EXIT LATER.
	TAD I	SYSIO			/GET THE CALLER'S BUFFER ADDRESS.
	DCA	BUFADRESS		/STASH IT FOR THE ROM ROUTINES.
	ISZ	SYSIO			/BUMP TO FUNCTION WORD.

/	NOTE: THE FOLLOWING INSTRUCTIONS ARE EXECUTED IN LOCATIONS 07400 ONWARD DURING
/	THE STANDARD DEC TD8E [OR ROM-BASED] BOOTSTRAP; THE EFFECTS OF THIS WILL BE
/	INDICATED IN THE COMMENTS ON THE MISALIGNED CODE AFTER THE NORMAL USAGE BELOW.

	SDLC				/STOP THE TAPE NOW.  [SEEMINGLY EXTRANEOUS.]
	NL7776				/MASK THE UNIT BIT OFF.
	AND I	SYSIO			/GET ALL OTHER FUNCTION WORD BITS.
	SDLD				/LOAD DATA REGISTER TO CONFORM WITH THE ROM.
	AND	S70/(70)		/GET BACK FIELD BITS FOR CALLER'S BUFFER.
	TAD	SCIDF/(CIF CDF)		/FORM "CIF CDF" INSTRUCTION TO CALLER'S BUFFER
					/FIELD.  NOTE: THE "CIF" IS EXTRANEOUS BECAUSE
					/THE INSTRUCTION FIELD WILL BE CHANGED TO 7.
	DCA	GETFLD			/STORE INTO "GETWRD" ROUTINE.
	TAD	GETFLD			/GET IT BACK.
	DCA	PUTFLD			/ALSO STORE INTO "PUTWRD" ROUTINE.
/	TAD I	SYSTEM			/GET THE FUNCTION WORD AGAIN.
	JMP	SMORE2			/CONTINUE THERE.

/	THE FOLLOWING IS WHAT OCCURS WHEN THE ABOVE WORDS ARE LOCATED AT 07400 ONWARD
/	BECAUSE OF THE STANDARD DEC TD8E [OR ROM-BASED] BOOTSTRAP.

/	*7400				/WHERE THESE WORDS EXECUTE.

/	DECTAPE DRIVE UNIT 0 IS STILL MOVING FROM THE LAST BOOTUP DATA TRANSFER.

/	SDLC				/THE AC IS CLEAR; STOP THE DRIVE.
/	NL7776				/AC SET TO 7776.
/	AND I	SYSIO&177+7420/(5654)	/MASK AGAINST THE RANDOM CONTENTS OF 05654.
/	SDLD				/PUT JUNK VALUE INTO THE DATA REGISTER.
/	AND	S70&177+7420/(5625)	/MASK OFF SOME BITS.
/	TAD	S6203&177+7420/(2353)	/ADD WORTHLESS CONSTANT.
/	DCA	GETFLD&177+7420		/DESTROY "SROMGO" POINTER.
/	TAD	GETFLD&177+7420		/GET JUNK VALUE BACK.
/	DCA	PUTFLD&177+7420		/DESTROY "WRQUAD" ROUTINE RETURN INSTRUCTION.

/	THE DECTAPE DRIVE HAS CORRECTLY BEEN STOPPED; HOWEVER, TWO LOCATIONS OF THE
/	HANDLER HAVE BEEN DESTROYED.  THIS DAMAGE MUST BE REPAIRED BEFORE THE HANDLER
/	IS SAFE TO MOVE TO THE PROPER LOCATIONS.

/	JMP	SMORE2&177+7420		/CONTINUE IN DAMAGE RECOVERY ROUTINE.

	ZBLOCK	7632-.			/EMPTY SPACE [IF ANY].
/	THE SYSTEM LOADER STARTS HERE. VARIOUS ARGUMENTS ARE STORED AS NECESSARY TO
/	IMPLEMENT A VARIETY OF SPECIFIC TASKS [SUCH AS STARTING A SYSTEM PROGRAM OR
/	IMPLEMENTING SYSTEM PROGRAM OVERLAYS].

/	THE DEFAULT CONTENTS WILL SAVE ALL OF FIELD 0 IN BLOCKS 0020-0057 [WHICH IS
/	ALSO THE BLOCKS OF THE TWO FILES "%" [0020-0037] AND "$" [0040-0057]
/	RESPECTIVELY.  THIS DEFINES HOW CORE-IMAGES ARE HANDLED WITHIN P?S/8 WHEN
/	USING SUCH PROGRAMS AS "GET", "START", "ODT" AND "BSAVE".  THE CORE IMAGE MAY
/	ALSO BE MOVED TO TWO DESIGNATED TINY FILE SYSTEM [TFS] FILES TO DEFER SUCH
/	WORK TO A LATER TIME WHEN THE TWO FILES ARE RESTORED.  WHEN THE "GET" COMMAND
/	[OR EQUIVALENT] IS USED, THE PROGRAM STARTING ADDRESS IS 07632 TO IMPLEMENT
/	THIS DEFAULT ACTION.

SLOAD,	JMS	SYSIO			/SYSTEM LOADER ENTRY POINT; DEFAULT VALUES
					/SAVE ALL OF FIELD 0 THEN RESTART.
SCORE,	0000				/BUFFER STARTING ADDRESS; DEFAULT VALUE
					/IS LOCATION 00000. 
SFUN,	00^100+WRITE+.-.		/FUNCTION WORD; DEFAULT IS WRITE 40 BLOCKS FROM
					/THE FIELD 0 BUFFER [LOCATIONS 00000-07777].
					/AS REQUIRED, THE LOGICAL UNIT BITS WILL BE
					/FILLED IN TO INDICATE THE BOOTUP UNIT.
SBLOCK,	BINADR				/STARTING BLOCK NUMBER; DEFAULT IS 0020.
	JMP I	.+1/(SYSTART)		/START AT THE INDICATED STARTING ADDRESS.
SYSTART,SBOOT				/STARTING ADDRESS; DEFAULTS TO THE SYSTEM
					/RESTART ADDRESS.

/	THE DEVICE HANDLER I/O ROUTINES ENTER HERE.  THE CALLER MUST SET THE DATA
/	FIELD TO THE CALLING FIELD; THE CONTENTS OF THE AC AND LINK ARE IGNORED WHEN
/	ENTERED.  UPON RETURN THE AC WILL BE CLEAR; THE LINK CONTENTS ARE GENERALLY
/	NOT MEANINGFUL; HOWEVER, MOST P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS ALSO CLEAR THE LINK
/	[IF POSSIBLE].

/	SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER ENTRY POINT.

/	THIS ABSOLUTELY *MUST* BE AT LOCATION 07640.

	IFNZRO	SZA CLA-. <ERROR .>	/ENSURE ENTRY POINT MATCHES "SZA CLA" VALUE.

SYSIO,	2530/MONSTRT			/SYSTEM HANDLER ENTRY POINT.  INITIALIZED FOR
					/THE BOOTSTRAP TO STARTUP THE KEYBOARD MONITOR.
	CLA CLL IAC			/CLEAR THE LINK AND SET AC TO 0001.
S6000,	SKON				/SKIP THE NEXT IF INTERRUPTS ARE ON AND
					/ALSO TURN THE INTERRUPT SYSTEM OFF.
	IAC				/AC=1 IF INTERRUPTS WERE ON, AC=2 IF
					/INTERRUPTS WERE OFF.
	TAD	S6000/(IOF-2)		/NOW HAVE "IOF" INSTRUCTION IF INTERRUPTS WERE
					/OFF OR "ION" INSTRUCTION IF INTERRUPTS WERE ON
					/BEFORE THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER WAS CALLED.
	DCA	SINTXIT			/STORE IN LINE FOR EXIT LATER.
	JMP	SMORE			/CONTINUE THERE.
/	SUPPORT ROUTINES FOR THE ROM-BASED CODE; THIS CODE CANNOT BE MOVED.

GETWRD,	.-.				/GET A WORD FROM THE CALLER'S BUFFER ROUTINE.
GETFLD,	.-.^10+CDF			/WILL BE "CIF CDF XX" WHERE XX IS THE
					/DATA FIELD OF THE CALLER'S BUFFER.
	TAD I	BUFPTR			/GET A WORD FROM THE BUFFER.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	GETWRD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

PUTWRD,	READLP+6			/PUT A WORD INTO THE CALLER'S BUFFER ROUTINE.
					/THIS WORD IS INITIALIZED FOR DIRECT OVERLAY
					/DURING THE BOOTUP OPERATION [PRESENTLY].
PUTFLD,	.-.^10+CDF			/WILL BE "CIF CDF XX" WHERE XX IS THE
					/DATA FIELD OF THE CALLER'S BUFFER.
	DCA I	BUFPTR			/PUT A WORD INTO THE BUFFER.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	PUTWRD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

/	ROM-DETECTED DECTAPE ERRORS COME HERE.

SERR,	CLA CLL				/ENSURE LINK IS CLEAR FOR RETRIES.
	HLT				/HALT ON ALL ERRORS.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	SROMGO/(ROMGO)		/RETRY THE LATEST BLOCK.

/	CALL HANDLING CONTINUES HERE.

/	BY PASSING THE LOGICAL UNIT BIT DIRECTLY TO THIS PART OF THE ROM SETUP CODE,
/	THE RETRY COUNTER SUPPORT IS BYPASSED [ALLOWING THE RETRY COUNTER WORD TO BE
/	REPURPOSED].  [LOOK IN THE OS/8 HANDLER FOR HOW TO INTERACT WITH THE RETRY
/	COUNTER; THIS MAKES TAPE OPERATIONS LESS RELIABLE COMPARED TO P?S/8.]

SMORE3,	TAD	SUNIT			/GET LOGICAL DRIVE UNIT.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	SF2SET5/(F2SET+5)	/CONTINUE SETUP THERE IN THE ROM.

/	NOTE: THE FOLLOWING WORD IS SUBJECT TO DESTRUCTION WHEN THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK
/	0000 ARE LOADED INTO 07360-07557.  THE RECOVERY ROUTINE WILL HAVE TO RECREATE
/	THE PROPER CONTENTS PRIOR TO MOVING THE ENTIRE PAGE CONTENTS FROM 07360-07557
/	TO 07600-07777; THIS CAN BE AVOIDED BY ONE OF SEVERAL METHODS:

/	1)  REWORK THE CODE NORMALLY LOADED AT 07620 TO AVOID THE DESTRUCTION [AND
/	    HOPEFULLY NOT MERELY CAUSE SOME ALTERNATE DESTRUCTION].

/	2)  REPURPOSE THE LOCATION TO BECOME AN UNIMPORTANT VALUE [OR COMPLETELY
/	    UNREFERENCED] THUS THE DESTRUCTION WOULD BECOME SUPERFLUOUS.

SROMGO,	ROMGO				/POINTER TO "ROMGO" ROUTINE TO START SEARCH.
/	NOTE: THE PREVIOUS FOUR WORDS MAY BE REWORKED INTO THE FOLLOWING:

/S70,	70				/CONSTANT 0070.
/SROMGO,ROMGO				/POINTER TO "ROMGO" ROUTINE TO START SEARCH.

/	ZBLOCK	1			/EMPTY SPACE THAT MAY BE USEFUL.

/	ZBLOCK	1			/EMPTY SPACE THAT CAN BE HARMLESSLY DESTROYED.

WRQUAD,	.-.				/WRITE A 12-BIT WORD TO THE DECTAPE ROUTINE.

/	THE ACTUAL CODE IS IN THE ROM; THIS IS USED ONLY TO ESTABLISH THE PROPER
/	SUBROUTINE LINKAGE AS IT IS CALLED FROM SEVERAL PLACES.

	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO THE ROM FIELD.
	JMP	WRQ			/CONTINUE THERE.

/	COMES BACK HERE FROM THE ROM-BASED CODE THAT CARRIES OUT THE SUBROUTINE CODE.

CIFR,	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO THE ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	WRQUAD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

/	NOTE: THE PREVIOUS WORD IS SUBJECT TO DESTRUCTION WHEN THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK
/	0000 ARE LOADED INTO 07360-07557.  THE RECOVERY ROUTINE WILL HAVE TO RECREATE
/	THE PROPER CONTENTS PRIOR TO MOVING THE ENTIRE PAGE CONTENTS FROM 07360-07557
/	TO 07600-07777; THIS CAN ONLY BE AVOIDED BY REWORKING THE CODE THAT STARTS UP
/	AT 07400 [NORMALLY IN 07620 ONWARD] IF POSSIBLE.  WHILE THIS IS UNLIKELY, IT
/	SHOULD BE POSSIBLE TO ALLOW THE RECOVERY ROUTINE TO FIT INTO THE ALLOTED CODE
/	SPACE AND ALSO REBOOT THE SYSTEM BY AN ALTERNATE METHOD DESCRIBED BELOW WHILE
/	STILL RESTORING THE PREVIOUS WORD.  [CODE SPACE CAN BE OBTAINED BY OTHER
/	RELATED METHODS IN A VIABLE OVERALL SOLUTION.]

RDQUAD,	.-.				/READ A 12-BIT WORD FROM THE DECTAPE ROUTINE.
	SDSQ				/QUAD LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, READ IN THE LATEST 12-BIT WORD.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	RDQUAD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

EQUFUN,	6364/.-.			/EQUIVALENCE DATA WITH CHECKSUM ROUTINE.
	CMA				/INVERT THE DATA AND ,XOR. THE DATA WORD.
	DCA	EQUTMP			/SAVE IT.
	TAD	EQUTMP			/GET IT BACK.
	AND	SCKSUM			/GET THE CARRY BITS.
	CIA				/INVERT FOR SUBTRACTION.
	CLL RAL				/MOVE OVER.
	TAD	EQUTMP			/NOW ADD THE LATEST.
	TAD	SCKSUM			/ADD THE NON-CARRY BITS TO RUNNING SUM.
	DCA	SCKSUM			/STORE BACK.
	TAD	EQUTMP			/GET THE LATEST DATA BACK.
	CLL CMA				/NOW HAVE CLEAR LINK AND THE LATEST WORD.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	EQUFUN			/RETURN TO CALLER.
/	THE ROM CODE RETURNS HERE WHEN ALL TAPE OPERATIONS HAVE FINISHED.

SEXIT,	TAD	SUNIT			/GET LATEST DRIVE UNIT.
	SDLC				/STOP THE TAPE DRIVE.

/	RESTORE INTERRUPT STATUS PRIOR TO THE DEVICE HANDLER CALL.

SINTXIT,IOF				/OR ION.
SFLDXIT,CIF CDF	00			/WILL BE "CIF CDF" TO CALLER'S FIELD.
/	ISZ	SYSIO			/BUMP PAST BLOCK ARGUMENT.
	JMP I	SYSIO			/RETURN TO CALLER.

S70,	70				/CONSTANT 0070.
					/NOTE: THIS CONSTANT COULD BE MOVED TO ALLOW
					/THE "ISZ SYSIO" INSTRUCTION IN THIS SECTION.

/	ALTHOUGH THE ROM-BASED CODE SETS UP A RETRY COUNTER, THE P?S/8 DEVICE HANDLER
/	DOESN'T USE IT.  BY AVOIDANCE OF THAT SECTION OF THE ROM SETUP CODE, THE
/	LOCATION CAN BE REPURPOSED.

	TRYCNT=	.			/USED AS AN ERROR RETRY COUNT IN OTHER SYSTEMS.

SF2SET5,F2SET+5				/POINTER TO ROM-BASED SETUP WITHOUT RETRY COUNT.
DRVUNIT,0^4000				/ROM COPIES "SUNIT" HERE [REDUNDANT DATA].
SFUNCTI,0^4000				/FUNCTION WORD BIT[0]; CLEAR=READ, SET=WRITE.
					/[NOTE: ALL OTHER BITS ARE IGNORED.]
SCKSUM,	0/.-.				/RUNNING CHECKSUM WORD.
SBLK,	0000				/LATEST DECTAPE BLOCK.
SUNIT,	0^4000				/DRIVE UNIT FOR LATEST FUNCTION IN BIT[0].

/	COMES HERE TO CONTINUE THE HANDLER CALL.

SMORE2,	TAD I	SYSIO			/GET THE FUNCTION WORD AGAIN.
					/NOTE: THE PREVIOUS WORD MAY BE MOVABLE.
	RAR				/UNIT BIT TO LINK.
	CLA RAR				/UNIT BIT NOW IN AC[0].
	DCA	SUNIT			/SET THE DRIVE UNIT FOR ROM.
	ISZ	SYSIO			/BUMP PAST FUNCTION WORD.
	TAD I	SYSIO			/GET STARTING BLOCK NUMBER FOR TRANSFER.
	DCA	SBLK			/STORE IT.
	ISZ	SYSIO			/BUMP PAST BLOCK ARGUMENT.
					/NOTE: THE PREVIOUS WORD MAY BE REMOVABLE.
SCIDF,	CIF CDF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0; ALSO CONSTANT 6203.
	JMP	SMORE3			/CONTINUE THERE.

/	NOTE: THE "JMP SMORE3" ABOVE COULD BE REMOVED; THE CODE AT "SMORE3" COULD BE
/	PLACED HERE IF OTHER CHANGES THROUGHOUT THE HANDLER ARE MADE.

/	TAD	SUNIT			/GET LOGICAL DRIVE UNIT.
/	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
/	JMP I	SF2SET5/(F2SET+5)	/CONTINUE SETUP THERE IN THE ROM.
EQUTMP,	0/.-.				/TEMPORARY FOR EQUIVALENCE ROUTINE.
BUFADRE,SBOOT/.-.			/READ/WRITE BUFFER POINTER.
PAGCNT,	20^200/.-.			/TRANSFER PAGE COUNT.
BUFPTR,	./.-.				/BUFFER POINTER FOR WORD TRANSFERS.
WORDCT,	./.-.				/WORD COUNTER FOR LATEST BLOCK TRANSFER.
/	ROMGO				/THIS CAN USED BY THE RECOVERY ROUTINE OR HELP
					/AVOID THE NEED TO RECOVER A CONSTANT.
/	NOTE: THE CODE CAN BE REWORKED TO USE THE ADDRESS CONSTANT ABOVE DIRECTLY.

	ZBLOCK	7756-.			/EMPTY SPACE.

	SPARM=	.			/=XXXX PARAMETER PASSED HERE.

	SFILES=	.+1			/FILE LIST PASSED HERE THROUGH 07777.

/	THE CONTENTS OF THIS AREA IS THE RECOVERY ROUTINE THAT IS LOADED WITH THE SAME
/	OFFSET AS THE ROUTINE THAT CAUSED THE DESTRUCTION OF TWO WORDS.  THIS IS DUE
/	TO THE OFFSET APPLIED EARLIER [AT 07400 AND FOLLOWING INSTEAD OF THE NORMAL
/	07620].

/	*7356				/WHERE THIS CODE IS WHEN OFFSET LOADED.

	CLA CLL				/CLEAN UP.
	SDLC				/STOP THE TAPE.

/	NOTE: THE PREVIOUS TWO INSTRUCTIONS ARE REDUNDANT AS THE AC IS ALREADY CLEAR
/	AND THE TAPE WAS STOPPED EARLIER.  IF THE CODE THAT STARTS AT 07400 IS CHANGED
/	TO REMOVE THE "SDLC" THERE, IT WOULD BE NECESSARY TO RETAIN IT HERE.

	TAD	S7405-20/(ROMGO)	/GET A COPY OF THE DESTROYED VALUE.
	DCA	SROMGO-20		/PUT IT BACK.
	TAD	S5671-20/(JMP I WRQUAD)	/GET A COPY OF THE DESTROYED VALUE.
	DCA	CIFR+1-20		/PUT IT BACK.
SMVLUP,	TAD I	SFROM-20		/GET A WORD.
	DCA I	STO-20			/PUT A WORD.
	ISZ	SFROM-20		/BUMP TO NEXT WORD TO MOVE.
	ISZ	STO-20			/BUMP POINTER/COUNTER.
	JMP	SMVLUP-20		/KEEP GOING UNTIL ALL ARE MOVED.
	JMP I	S7600-20		/GO START UP A COMPLETE REBOOT.

/	THE CODE ABOVE ACTUALLY EXECUTES AS DEFINED IN THE COMMENTS BELOW.

/	TAD	S7405&177+7420		/GET A COPY OF THE DESTROYED VALUE.
/	DCA	GETFLD&177+7420		/PUT IT BACK.
/	TAD	S5671&177+7420		/GET A COPY OF THE DESTROYED VALUE.
/	DCA	PUTFLD&177+7420		/PUT IT BACK.
/SMVLUP,TAD I	SFROM&177+7420		/GET A WORD.
/	DCA I	STO&177+7420		/STORE A WORD.
/	ISZ	SFROM&177+7420		/BUMP TO NEXT WORD TO MOVE.
/	ISZ	STO&177+7420		/BUMP POINTER/COUNTER.
/	JMP	SMVLUP&177+7420		/KEEP GOING UNTIL ALL ARE MOVED.
/	JMP I	S7600&177+7420		/GO START UP A COMPLETE REBOOT.
S7600,	SBOOT				/SYSTEM RESTART ADDRESS.
SFROM,	STARTIT-20			/BLOCK 0000 DATA LOADED HERE.
STO,	SBOOT				/BLOCK 0000 DATA NEEDED HERE.
S7405,	ROMGO				/ADDRESS OF "ROMGO" ROUTINE.
S5671,	JMP I	WRQUAD			/"JMP I WRQUAD" INSTRUCTION VALUE.

/	NOTE: FUTURE RELEASES OF THIS HANDLER MUST INCLUDE A CALL TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE
/	HANDLER FOR THE PURPOSE OF RELOADING BLOCK 0001-0017; THE REBOOT AT 07600
/	["SBOOT"] WILL BE REWORKED TO USE THE ROM-BASED BOOTUP ROUTINE.

	ZBLOCK	-.			/EMPTY SPACE.

	RELOC				/TURN RELOCATION OFF.
/	ADDITIONAL SYSTEM-DEPENDENT P?S/8 COMPONENTS.

/	UNLIKE OS/8, P?S/8 HAS ADDITIONAL COMPONENTS SPECIFIC TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE
/	TYPE.  WHEN A SYSTEM IS CREATED, ALL DEVICE-SPECIFIC CODE OVERLAYS AN
/	OTHERWISE GENERIC OPERATING SYSTEM AND RELATED COMPONENTS.

/	REWIND AND UNLOAD ROUTINE.

/	THE P?S8 KEYBOARD MONITOR SUPPORTS TWO COMMANDS DESIGNATED FOR TAPE-BASED OR
/	OTHER SYSTEMS WHERE THE SYSTEM DEVICE CAN BE REWOUND AND/OR UNLOADED; THE
/	TEMPLATE FOR THESE COMMANDS IS THE IMPLEMENTATION FOR THE TC01/TC08 DECTAPE
/	CONTROLLER.  ON THIS CONFIGURATION, COMMANDS CAN BE TYPED THAT ALLOW OVERLAP
/	OF TAPE MOTION WITH KEYBOARD MONITOR COMMANDS [CONTROL-R TO REWIND THE TAPE]
/	AND UNLOAD THE THE SYSTEM DEVICE ENTIRELY [WHICH REQUIRES WAITING FOR THE
/	OPERATION TO COMPLETE].

/	WHERE POSSIBLE, ATTEMPT TO MIMIC [OR IMPROVE] THIS BEHAVIOR FOR EACH
/	CONFIGURATION.  THE TD8E CONTROLLER CANNOT ALLOW OVERLAP WITH TAPE REWIND
/	BECAUSE THERE IS NO AUTOMATIC END ZONE DETECTION; AS SUCH, THE SPECIFIC
/	IMPLEMENTATION DIFFERS IN SOME WAYS.  BECAUSE THERE ARE NO BRAKE-RELATED
/	ISSUES, THE UNLOAD OPERATION CAN BE INITIATED AND WILL NOT WAIT FOR COMPLETION
/	[HOWEVER, THE USER HAS TO STOP THE TAPE FROM INFINITE FLAPPING].

	FIELD	0			/FIELD WHERE THIS CODE IS WRITTEN OUT FROM
					/WHEN GENERATING A COMPLETE OPERATING SYSTEM.

	*REWIND-1			/THESE ROUTINES ARE NOT RELOCATED.

L3000,	3000				/CONSTANT 3000.

/	THE ROUTINES ARE ENTERED HERE.  OTHER THAN COMMON OPERATIONS, EACH COMMAND
/	WILL PERFORM ADDITIONAL PROGRAMMING TO COMPLETE THE SPECIFIC TASK.

REWIND,	JMS	TAPMOVE			/THE AC CONTAINS 0000, START A REWIND
					/ON THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT.
	TAD	TEMP1			/GET LATEST CHARACTER.
	TAD	L7756/[-"R!300]		/COMPARE TO <^R>.
	SNA CLA				/SKIP IF OTHER [<^T>].
	JMP	ENDWAIT			/WAIT FOR END ZONE THERE.

/	WE WANT TO UNLOAD THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT; SELECT THE OTHER DRIVE WHILE
/	INITIATING MOTION AS WELL [AND THEN TURN MOTION OFF ON THE OTHER UNIT].  THE
/	DESELECTION PROCESS DONE THIS WAY WILL CAUSE THE ORIGINAL UNIT TO FALL OFF THE
/	TAKEUP REEL.

	NL0001				/SET UNIT REVERSAL BIT.
	JMP	DESELECT		/CONTINUE THERE.
/	TAPE MOVE IN REVERSE ROUTINE.  CALL WITH AC=0000 TO REWIND THE CURRENT SYSTEM
/	DEVICE UNIT; CALL WITH AC=0001 TO REWIND THE OPPOSITE SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT.

TAPMOVE,.-.				/REWIND THE TAPE ROUTINE.
	TAD	UNIT			/ADD ON THE CURRENT LOGICAL DEVICE UNIT.
	CLL RTR				/MOVE DESIRED UNIT TO AC[0].
	TAD	L3000/(3000)		/ADD ON REVERSE DIRECTION AND MOTION BITS.
	SDLC				/LOAD THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	JMP I	TAPMOVE			/RETURN TO CALLER.

/	COMES HERE TO WAIT FOR THE END ZONE ON THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT.

ENDWAIT,SDSS				/SINGLE-LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT
	SDRC				/YES, GET THE COMMAND REGISTER CONTENTS.
	AND	L77/[77]		/JUST MARK WINDOW BITS.
	TAD	L7756/[-22]		/COMPARE TO END ZONE VALUE.
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF IN END ZONE NOW.
	JMP	ENDWAIT			/NOT YET, KEEP WAITING.

/	WE'RE IN THE END ZONE HAVING REWOUND THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT.  FALL
/	THROUGH TO THE NEXT ROUTINE WHICH WILL REDUNDANTLY SELECT THE SAME SYSTEM
/	DEVICE UNIT, THEN CLEAR THE MOTION BIT.

	DESELEC=.			/DESELECT THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT HERE.

/	IF THE AC IS SET TO 0001, THE OTHER SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT WILL BE SELECTED WITH
/	REVERSE DIRECTION AND MOTION SET.  THIS WILL CAUSE THE CURRENT DEVICE UNIT
/	[WHICH WAS SELECTED FOR REVERSE DIRECTION AND MOTION SET] TO LOSE CONTROL; THE
/	TAPE WILL COMPLETELY UNLOAD AND NOT STOP TAPE FLAPPING UNTIL THE OPERATOR
/	STOPS THE TAPE MANUALLY.  IF THE AC IS SET TO 0000 THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE
/	UNIT WILL BE REDUNDANTLY SELECTED FOR REVERSE DIRECTION AND MOTION [WHICH IS
/	HARMLESS].

	JMS	TAPMOVE			/SELECT EITHER THE CURRENT SYSTEM DEVICE
					/LOGICAL UNIT OR THE OTHER SYSTEM DEVICE
					/LOGICAL UNIT [DEPENDING ON HOW WE GOT HERE].
	SDRC				/GET THE CURRENT COMMAND REGISTER.
	TAD	L7000/[-1000]		/REMOVE THE MOTION BIT.
/	TAD	L7000/(7000)		/REMOVE THE MOTION BIT. **** VERSION 8Z ****
	SDLC				/LOAD THE COMMAND REGISTER TO
					/STOP THE CURRENT DECTAPE UNIT.
	JMP	CHLOOP			/KEEP GOING THERE.

/L7000,	7000				/CONSTANT 7000 **** VERSION 8Z ****

/	ZBLOCK	.!177-.			/EMPTY SPACE [IF ANY]. **** VERSION 8Z ****

/	JMP I	PCHLOOP/[CHLOOP]	/CONTINUE THERE. **** VERSION 8Z ****
/	DEVICE LOGICAL SIZE.

/	THE SYSTEM GENERATION ROUTINES NEED TO KNOW THE OVERALL SIZE OF ANY ONE DEVICE
/	ADDRESSED BY THE SYSTEM HANDLER; CERTAIN ENTRIES WILL BE MADE IN VARIOUS
/	STRUCTURES BASED ON THIS VALUE.  [AS A PRACTICAL MATTER, THIS SIZE MUST BE
/	GREATER THAN 1000 BLOCKS TO BE OF MUCH USE.]

/	ON VERY LARGE DEVICES, THIS COULD BE AS MUCH AS 10000 BLOCKS [WHICH SHOULD BE
/	EXPRESSED AS 0000]; THIS IS THE UPPER LIMIT OF ADDRESSABILITY FOR ANY P?S/8
/	SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER [BUT MAY NOT BE THE UPPER LIMIT OF THE ACTUAL DEVICE].

/	THE P?S/8 SHELL IS CAPABLE OF ADDRESSING MORE STORAGE BLOCKS THAN THE BASIC
/	P?S/8 SYSTEM THROUGH THE USE OF NON-SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLERS.  FOR EXAMPLE, ON
/	AN RK8E/RK05 SYSTEM CONFIGURED FOR LOGICAL DRIVES 0 THROUGH 3 [WHERE EACH
/	LOGICAL UNIT IS AN ENTIRE RK05 PACK], THE MAXIMUM POSSIBLE SYSTEM BLOCK
/	AVAILABLE TO THE BASIC SYSTEM IS 07777 AS DEFINED ABOVE.  HOWEVER, THE MAXIMUM
/	BLOCK AVAILABLE TO THE P?S/8 SHELL ENVIRONMENT WOULD BE 31277, WHERE BLOCKS
/	10000-31277 ARE ONLY ACCESSIBLE THROUGH NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS AS USED IN THE
/	SHELL ENVIRONMENT [AS WELL AS THE BASIC SYSTEM PROGRAM "BLKCPY" WHEN IT IS
/	UPGRADED TO THE NEW SPECIFICATION FOR NON-SYSTEM HANDLERS IN THE VERSION 9A
/	RELEASE].  AS SUCH, BLOCKS 00000-07777 REPRESENT THE MAXIMUM VALUE FOR THE
/	BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM THAT CAN BE CONFIGURED; SMALLER VALUES OF MAXIMUM BLOCK MAY
/	BE USED IN PRACTICAL CONFIGURATIONS WHERE THE BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM IS NOT
/	REQUIRED TO SUPPORT THAT MANY STORAGE BLOCKS.  THE SYSTEM MAY BE CONFIGURED
/	SUCH THAT MORE OF THE AVAILABLE STORAGE IS ALLOCATED TO THE SHELL ENVIRONMENT
/	AS REQUIRED.  [NOTE: IT IS POSSIBLE TO CONFIGURE A BASIC P?S/8 SYSTEM TO ONLY
/	ADDRESS BLOCKS BLOCKS 00000-00377 OR PERHAPS SOMEWHAT LESS, WITH THE REST OF
/	THE DEVICE STORAGE CONFIGURED FOR USE IN THE P?S/8 SHELL ENVIRONMENT ONLY.]

	*DEVSIZE			/OVER SYSTEM GENERATION ROUTINES.

	TDSIZE				/DEFINE OUR DEVICE SIZE.
/	P?S/8 BINARY FILE SLURP LOADER AND BOOTSTRAP FOR THE TD8E WITH MR8E-C ROM.

	FIELD	SLPGEN&70%10		/GENERATION FIELD FOR SLURP LOADER.

	*SLPGEN&7600			/GENERATION ADDRESS FOR SLURP LOADER.

SLPCORE,NOPUNCH				/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	FIELD	0			/WHERE IT LOADS LATER.

	*SLPCORE			/RESET THE ORIGIN BACK IN CASE FIELD CHANGES IT.

	ENPUNCH				/UN-FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	RELOC	SBOOT			/WHERE THE SLURP LOADER AND BOOTSTRAP EXECUTES.

/	DEVICE DEPENDENT BOOTSTRAP STARTING AT 07600.  ALSO USED WITH THE P?S/8
/	VERSION OF THE PAPER-TAPE "BIN" LOADER [INSTEAD OF THE SLURP LOADER].

/	P?S/8 BOOTSTRAP CONVENTIONS ARE AS FOLLOWS:

/	1) AC CONTENTS UNIMPORTANT.

/	2) LINK CONTENTS UNIMPORTANT.

/	3) IN GENERAL, THE INTERRUPT SYSTEM SHOULD BE TURNED OFF; HOWEVER, BECAUSE
/	   INTERRUPTS ARE PARTICULARLY A PROBLEM FOR TD8E-BASED SYSTEMS, THIS
/	   BOOTSTRAP FORCES INTERRUPTS OFF AS THERE IS SUFFICIENT CODE SPACE FOR THE
/	   "IOF" INSTRUCTION.  [NOTE: IF THE SYSTEM DEVICE OPERATES BY DMA OR PROGRAM
/	   TRANSFER WITHOUT CRITICAL TIMING, THIS WILL NOT BE A PROBLEM.  THE CODING
/	   OF THE P?S/8 KEYBOARD MONITOR AND (EMBEDDED EDITOR) IS SUCH THAT INTERRUPTS CAUSE AN ADDITIONAL SYSTEM RESTART WITH
/	   INTERRUPTS TURNED OFF.]

/	4) THE DATA FIELD MUST BE 0.

/	5) ALL WORDS OF THE BOOTSTRAP MUST BE INTACT.  AS LONG AS ONLY WELL-BEHAVED
/	   PROGRAMS ARE LOADED, THIS SHOULD NEVER BE A PROBLEM.

SBOOT,					/BOOTSTRAP RELOAD STARTS HERE.
	IOF				/TURN INTERRUPTS OFF.
B7670,	SNA SZL CLA/(-110)		/CONSTANT 7670; CLEARS THE AC AND MIGHT SKIP.
BROMBOO,ROMBOOT/(SNA SZL)		/ENTRY POINT INTO THE ROM TO REBOOT; CANNOT
					/SKIP BECAUSE THE AC IS NOW CLEAR.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	BROMBOOT/(ROMBOOT)	/GO TO BOOTUP ROUTINE.

	SLBTEND=.			/ACTUAL END OF BOOTSTRAP-REQUIRED CODE.
/	NOTE: AS OF VERSION 8Z, THERE IS AN SPECIFIC UPPER LIMIT ON THE SIZE OF THE
/	CODE NEEDED TO REBOOT P?S/8 WHEN REMOVED FROM THE SLURP LOADER [TO BE USED
/	WITH THE P?S/8 VERSIONS OF THE BIN AND RIM LOADERS].  VARIOUS EARLIER RELEASES
/	MAY HAVE MADE DIFFERENT DEMANDS ON THIS CODE LENGTH [AND VERSION 9A WILL
/	CHANGE THIS UPPER LIMIT TO ONE WORD LESS].  IT IS NOT EXPECTED THIS NEW
/	REQUIREMENT WILL AFFECT TOO MANY SYSTEM CONFIGURATIONS; EACH WILL HAVE TO BE
/	RECODED AS REQUIRED.  HOWEVER, THIS CONFIGURATION USES ONE OF THE SMALLEST
/	BOOTUP SECTIONS; AS SUCH, NO CHANGES WILL BE NEEDED FOR THE P?S/8 TD8E WITH
/	MR8E-C ROM VARIANT.  [NOTE: THE TC01/TC08 SYSTEM, ONCE REASSEMBLED FOR USE
/	WITH VERSION 9A, IS ONE WORD SHORTER THAN THE NEW LIMITATION.  EACH SYSTEM
/	DEVICE HANDLER WILL BE EVALUATED REGARDING THIS POINT.]

/	ALL BOOTSTRAP CODE MUST BE WITHIN 07600-07642 IN P?S/8 VERSION 8Z.  [NOTE:
/	IN VERSION 9A, THIS WILL BE FURTHER RESTRICTED TO 07600-07641.  THIS WILL
/	FACILITATE IMPROVING THE P?S/8 VERSION OF THE PAPER-TAPE "BIN" LOADER BY
/	ALLOWING ONE ADDITIONAL WORD TO BE AVAILABLE.  THIS WILL BE USED TO IMPROVE
/	THE LOADER; WHEN COMPLETED, IT WILL BE IMMUNE TO INTERNAL DESTRUCTION BY THE
/	EXECUTION OF ALL PROGRAMS FOR THREE-CYCLE DATA BREAK DEVICES.  BY CONTRAST,
/	NO OTHER VERSION OF THE PAPER-TAPE BIN LOADER IS ROBUST ENOUGH TO PREVENT
/	DESTRUCTION BY THE EXECUTION OF PROGRAMS FOR AT LEAST ONE OF THE PREVAILING
/	DEVICES [DF32/RF08, TC58 MAGNETIC TAPE, TC01/TC08 DECTAPE].

/	THE SLURP LOADER AND BOOTSTRAP ARE WRITTEN OUT TO A SYSTEM-DESIGNATED BLOCK.
/	THE P?S/8 "BIN" PROGRAM WILL USE THE ENTIRE BLOCK CONTENTS WHEN LOADING TFS
/	BINARY FILES; ONLY THE BOOTSTRAP WILL BE USED IN CONJUNCTION WITH THE P?S/8
/	VERSION OF THE PAPER-TAPE "BIN" AND "RIM" LOADERS.  THIS WILL ALLOW RELOAD OF
/	P?S/8 AS LONG AS THE LOCATIONS FROM 07600-ONWARD ARE NOT DESTROYED BY THE
/	PROGRAMS LOADED BY BIN [OR RIM].

/	ENSURE THIS SIZE CONVENTION IS MET.

	IFNZRO	SBOOT+BBLEN-.&4000	<ERROR	.>

/	DETAILED DESCRIPTION OF SLURP FORMAT P?S/8 BINARY FILES.

/	EVERY BLOCK OF A SLURP FILE IS INDEPENDENT OF ALL OTHER BLOCKS OTHER THAN ANY
/	CONSIDERATION OF CURRENT LOADING ORIGIN AND CURRENT LOADING FIELD INHERITED
/	FROM THE PREVIOUS BLOCK PROCESSED.  TO ENSURE PROPER LOADING, EVERY SLURP FILE
/	CONTAINS INITIAL FIELD 0 AND ORIGIN 0200 SETTINGS; THIS AVOIDS DEPENDENCE ON
/	ORDER OF LOADING, EVEN IN THE CASE OF MULTIPLE OUTPUT FILES CREATED BY A
/	SINGLE ASSEMBLY [OR BINARY PAPER-TAPE CONVERSION].  SINCE THIS FIXED SEQUENCE
/	IS PART OF THE OPENING WORDS OF A BINARY FILE, CONFORMING PROGRAMS CAN
/	DETERMINE THE FILE IS BINARY.  [NOTE: P?S/8 SYSTEM PROGRAMS SUCH AS "FOCAL"
/	USE BOTH BINARY AND TEXT FILES; BY USING THIS FIXED SEQUENCE AS PART OF A
/	SIGNATURE CHECK, THE PROGRAM IS ABLE TO DIFFERENTIATE AND PROCESS THE FILES
/	PASSED IN A MIXED SEQUENCE; FOCAL REQUIRES ALL BINARY FILES TO BE PASSED PRIOR
/	TO ALL TEXT FILES, ETC.]

/	WHILE TFS FILES ARE RESTRICTED TO AT MOST 16 BLOCKS OF DATA, THE SLURP FORMAT
/	DOES NOT HAVE ANY INHERENT BOUNDARY RESTRICTIONS.  FUTURE RELEASES OF P?S/8
/	WILL SUPPORT THE "BINCON" UTILITY WITH THE ABILITY TO CREATE EXTENDED-LENGTH
/	BINARY FILES THAT CAN BE CONVERTED TO OS/8 BINARY FORMAT AFTER PROCESSING TO
/	REMOVE THE PROBLEMS ASSOCIATED WITH THE DEFECTIVE IMPLEMENTATION OF THE DTORG
/	DIRECTIVE.  THIS IN TURN WILL FACILITATE FUTURE METHODS OF P?S/8 SYSTEM
/	GENERATION.
/	SLURP LOADER BLOCK FORMAT.

/	WITHIN ANY SLURP FORMAT BINARY BLOCK THERE ARE 18 GROUPS OF SEVEN WORDS.  ANY
/	ADDITIONAL WORDS ARE IGNORED.

/	SINCE SLURP LOADERS OPERATE AT THE PHYSICAL DEVICE LEVEL, EACH SLURP LOADER
/	HAS THE SPECIFIC RESPONSIBILITY TO ACCESS THE DATA IN A MANNER ANALOGOUS TO A
/	TYPICAL DEVICE HANDLER, BUT ALSO HAS THE ADDITIONAL RESPONSIBILITY TO DEAL
/	WITH PREVAILING WORDS KNOWN TO EXIST THAT ARE NOT SPECIFICALLY PART OF THE
/	BINARY DATA.  SINCE PDP-8 FORMAT DECTAPES ARE 129 WORDS PER BLOCK,
/	DECTAPE-SPECIFIC SLURP LOADERS [FOR THE TC01/TC08 AND THE TD8E] MUST IGNORE
/	THE THREE ADDITIONAL DATA WORDS IN THE BLOCK [AND ANY ADDITIONAL WORDS THAT
/	ARE PRESENT AS PART OF THE OVERALL LOW-LEVEL DECTAPE FORMAT].  [NOTE: SLURP
/	LOADERS FOR OTHER DEVICES GENERALLY FUNCTION IN AN ANALOGOUS MANNER; HOWEVER
/	CERTAIN DEVICE-SPECIFIC QUIRKS MAY APPLY.]  ALL P?S/8 RELEASES ALSO SUPPORT A
/	DEVICE-INDEPENDENT VIRTUAL SLURP LOADER AS A COMMAND-LINE OPTION OF THE "BIN"
/	SYSTEM PROGRAM THAT LAUNCHES A SLURP LOADER [WHEN LOADING THE CONTENTS OF
/	BINARY FILES, ONE OF SEVERAL OPERATING MODES OF THE BIN PROGRAM]; FOR SYSTEM
/	CONFIGURATIONS THAT DO NOT SUPPORT A DEVICE-SPECIFIC SLURP LOADER, THE VIRTUAL
/	SLURP LOADER IS ALWAYS USED BY DEFAULT.

/	NOTE: SLURP BINARY FORMAT IS ALSO USED IN SEVERAL OTHER OPERATING SYSTEMS,
/	INCLUDING CERTAIN OPERATING SYSTEMS SOLD BY DEC.  [IT IS EXTREMELY LIKELY DEC
/	MANAGEMENT PERSONNEL NEVER UNDERSTOOD THIS DESPITE THE OBVIOUS COPYRIGHT
/	IMPLICATIONS.]  THESE INCLUDE THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM [WHICH WAS WRITTEN BY
/	RICHARD LARY AND LENNY ELEKMAN WITH ADDITIONAL CONTRIBUTIONS BY HANK MAURER,
/	JACK BURNESS, JOE FISCHETTI, ROD DORMAN AND CHARLES LASNER], DIBOL-8 [A
/	HACKED-UP VARIANT OF THE R-L MONITOR SYSTEM TRIVIALLY CREATED BY HANK MAURER
/	TO SUPPORT THE FIRST DIBOL COMPILER AS AN R-L MONITOR SYSTEM PROGRAM] AND ALL
/	VARIANT RELEASES OF EDU-SYSTEM 3O [WHICH IS IN TURN BASED ON POLY BASIC, A
/	PRODUCT OF RICHARD LARY AND LENNY ELEKMAN WHILE STUDENTS AT BROOKLYN POLYTECH
/	AND AS SUCH, NOT EMPLOYEES OF DEC].  POLY BASIC WAS DEVELOPED AS A STANDALONE
/	OPERATING SYSTEM ON THE R-L MONITOR, THE PROGENITOR SYSTEM FROM WHICH P?S/8 IS
/	DESCENDED; WHILE THERE IS NO INTERNAL FILE SYSTEM ASSOCIATED WITH SLURP FORMAT
/	IN THESE BASIC-ONLY SYSTEMS, IT IS THE INTERNAL FORMAT OF ALL BINARY
/	OPERATIONS FOR THE INTERNAL RUN-TIME SYSTEM.

/	SLURP GROUP FORMAT.

/	EACH OF THE [UP TO] 18 GROUPS IN ANY ONE LOGICAL BLOCK IS PROCESSED IN
/	SEQUENCE BEFORE MOVING ON TO THE NEXT BLOCK IN THE FILE [IF APPLICABLE].  THE
/	CONTROL [OR FLAG] WORD [FOR THE NEXT SIX DATA WORDS] IS PROCESSED FIRST TO
/	DETERMINE THE DISPOSITION OF EACH OF THE SIX DATA WORDS THAT FOLLOW.

/	EACH CONTROL WORD CONTAINS SIX PAIRS OF CONTROL BITS, EACH FOR THE
/	CORRESPONDING DATA WORD.  [BIT PAIRS ARE RECKONED LEFT TO RIGHT.]
/	WITHIN EACH BIT PAIR, THE FOLLOWING DEFINITIONS APPLY:

/	00 - THE CORRESPONDING WORD IS DATA TO BE LOADED INTO THE LOCATION INDICATED
/	     BY THE CURRENT LOADING ORIGIN AND THE CURRENT LOADING FIELD.  THE
/	     LOADING ORIGIN IS INCREMENTED FOR ANY ADDITIONAL DATA WORD LOADING.

/	10 - THE CORRESPONDING WORD IS AN ORIGIN SETTING; THE LOADING ORIGIN WILL BE
/	     UPDATED TO THE CONTENTS OF THIS WORD FOR ANY ADDITIONAL DATA WORD
/	     LOADING.

/	11 - THE CORRESPONDING WORD IS A FIELD SETTING EXPRESSED AS A "CDF X" [62X1]
/	     INSTRUCTION TO THE NEW LOADING FIELD X; THE CONTENTS OF THIS WORD WILL BE
/	     STORED INLINE TO CHANGE THE EFFECTIVE LOADING FIELD FOR ANY ADDITIONAL
/	     DATA WORDS.

/	01 - END OF FILE HAS BEEN DETECTED; IGNORE ALL ADDITIONAL DATA WORDS IN THIS
/	     DATA BLOCK AND ANY ADDITIONAL BLOCKS IN THE CURRENT FILE.  THE SLURP
/	     LOADER WILL MOVE ON TO PROCESSING ADDITIONAL SLURP FILES [IF APPLICABLE].

/	NOTE: WHEN APPROPRIATE COMMAND-LINE OPTIONS ARE INVOKED, THE P?S/8 "BIN"
/	PROGRAM IS ALSO CAPABLE OF READING BINARY PAPER-TAPES [FROM EITHER THE
/	LOW-SPEED OR HIGH-SPEED READER] AND CONVERTING THE BINARY DATA INTO THE
/	CONTENTS OF SPECIFIED SLURP BINARY TFS FILES.  USING ADDITIONAL COMMAND-LINE
/	OPTIONS, THE SLURP LOADER CAN BE INVOKED IN THE SAME COMMAND TO LOAD THE
/	CONTENTS OF THE FILES CREATED EARLIER IN THE COMMAND OPERATION.

/	NOTE: THE P?S/8 "BIN" PROGRAM IS ALSO CAPABLE OF ADDITIONAL OPERATIONS TO
/	PUNCH THE CONTENTS OF SLURP BINARY FILES OR TO PLACE A COPY OF THE RIM LOADER
/	AND A CUSTOMIZED VERSION OF THE BIN LOADER INTO MEMORY WITH A COPY OF THE
/	SLURP LOADER BOOTSTRAP AS DEFINED ABOVE.  OTHER THAN THE DEVICE-DEPENDENT
/	BOOTSTRAP WORDS, THIS OPERATION IS DEVICE-INDEPENDENT; APPROPRIATE
/	COMMAND-LINE OPTIONS ARE AVAILABLE FOR ALL OPERATING MODES OF THE P?S/8 "BIN"
/	SYSTEM PROGRAM.

/	SINCE NO CHECKSUM SCHEME IS USED IN THE SLURP FILE FORMAT, ADVANCED USERS CAN
/	FREELY PATCH ANY BINARY FILE AS REQUIRED.  THIS IS USEFUL WHEN MODIFYING
/	PAPER-TAPE SOURCED/CONVERTED BINARY FILES WHICH MAY REQUIRE ALTERNATE MEMORY
/	FIELDS THAN THE PROGRAM WAS ORIGINALLY ASSEMBLED FOR LOADING.

/	SELF-STARTING BINARY PROGRAM ISSUES.

/	"SPARM" [07756] CONTAINS THE 12-BIT STARTING ADDRESS OF THE PROGRAM.  BY
/	DIRECTLY LOADING INTO THIS ADDRESS, THE PROGRAM WILL ALWAYS START ACCORDING
/	TO THE USER'S PREFERENCE.

/	"BCIF" [07746] CONTAINS THE STARTING FIELD IN THE FORM OF A "CIF CDF XX"
/	INSTRUCTION WHERE XX IS THE STARTING FIELD [IN BITS(6-8)].  [NOTE: THIS
/	SPECIFIC ADDRESS APPLIES TO VERSION 8Z AND IS SUBJECT TO CHANGE.]

/	NOTE: SELF-STARTING INFORMATION IS IGNORED IF THE VIRTUAL BINARY SLURP LOADER
/	IS IN EFFECT [AS ALL ATTEMPTS TO LOAD INTO 07600-07777 ARE IGNORED].
/	COMES HERE TO STOP THE LATEST DECTAPE DRIVE UNIT AT THE END OF EVERY FILE.

BREAD2,	TAD	BUNIT			/GET LATEST UNIT INTO AC[0].
	SDLC				/STOP THE LATEST TAPE UNIT WE WERE READING.

/	FALLS THROUGH TO PROCESS THE NEXT FILE [IF ANY].

/	THIS IS THE SLURP LOADER STARTING ADDRESS.  THE FILE POINTER MUST BE
/	INCREMENTED BEFORE USING TO OBTAIN THE LATEST FILE BLOCK AND UNIT BIT
/	DATA.  IF THE LATEST FILE LIST ELEMENT OBTAINED IS 0000 THERE ARE NO
/	ADDITIONAL FILES TO LOAD.  [NOTE: IF THE FILE LIST IS THE LARGEST SIZE ENDING
/	IN 07777, THE 0000 VALUE IS OBTAINED BECAUSE THE "ISZ BFLPTR" INSTRUCTION
/	SKIPS THE NEXT INSTRUCTION.  AS SUCH, ONLY SHORTER FILE LISTS ARE TERMINATED
/	WITH A SENTINEL 0000 VALUE PAST THE LAST PASSED FILE ELEMENT.]

BREAD,	ISZ	BFLPTR			/BUMP TO NEXT FILE.
	TAD I	BFLPTR			/GET THE NEXT FILE HANDLE WORD.
	AND	B7770/(7770)		/JUST FILE BLOCK BITS.
	SNA				/SKIP IF AN ACTVIE ENTRY.
	JMP	BCIF			/JUMP IF ALL FILES PROCESSED.
	DCA	BBLK			/SAVE LATEST FILE BLOCK.
	TAD I	BFLPTR			/GET FILE HANDLE AGAIN.
	RAR				/LOW-ORDER UNIT BIT TO LINK.
	CLA RAR				/LOW-ORDER UNIT BIT TO AC[0].
	DCA	BUNIT			/SAVE UNIT BIT FOR NOW.
BCONT,	SPA CLA				/SKIP NEXT IF CLEAR.
BTURNX,	NL4000				/SET REVERSING BIT.
	TAD	BDIRGO			/GET CURRENT DIRECTION AND GO BITS.
	DCA	BDIRGO			/SAVE [WITH POTENTIALLY UPDATED] DIRECTION.
	TAD	BDIRGO			/GET CURRENT DIRECTION AND GO BITS
	CLL RAR				/MOVE TO HARDWARE DIRECTION AND GO BITS.
	TAD	BUNIT			/ADD ON CURRENT UNIT BIT.
	SDLC				/GET THE TAPE MOVING IN THE DESIRED DIRECTION.
	JMS	BGETWD			/WAIT FOR
	JMS	BGETWD			/SOME LINES.
BSRCHMO,SDSS				/SINGLE LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRC				/YES, READ THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	CLL RTL				/MOVE UP TWO BITS; DIRECTION TO LINK.
	AND	B374/(374)		/JUST MARK TRACK BITS.
	TAD	B7670/(-110)		/COMPARE TO END ZONE VALUE.
	SNA				/SKIP IF NOT IN THE END ZONE.
	JMP	BTURNX			/REVERSE DIRECTION IF SO.
	TAD	B7760/(-130+110)	/COMPARE TO BLOCK MARK VALUE.
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF AT BLOCK MARK.
	JMP	BSRCHMORE		/GO BACK AND KEEP SEARCHING.
	SDRD				/GET LATEST BLOCK.
	SZL				/SKIP IF GOING FORWARD.
	TAD	B4002			/ADD ON UNDERSHOOT VALUE IF REVERSE SEARCH.
	CIA				/INVERT FOR TESTING.
	TAD	BBLK			/COMPARE TO DESIRED BLOCK.
	SZA				/SKIP IF IT MATCHES.
	JMP	BCONT			/DETERMINE WHAT TO DO NEXT THERE.
/	THE SELECTED DECTAPE IS MOVING FORWARD AND THE DESIRED BLOCK MARK FOUND.

/	NOW WE NEED TO LOCATE THE 40 MARK WINDOW VALUE [PART OF THE LOCK WORD].

BFNDLOC,SDSS				/SINGLE LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRC				/YES, GET THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	AND	B77/(77)		/JUST MARK WINDOW BITS.
	TAD	B7740/(-40)		/COMPARE TO MARK WINDOW VALUE [LOCK MARK].
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF IT MATCHES.
	JMP	BFNDLOCK40		/JUMP IF NOT.

/	READ ONE MORE WORD; WE WILL BE LOCATED AT THE FIRST DATA WORD OF THE BLOCK.

	JMS	BGETWD			/READ THE LAST NON-DATA WORD.
B7740,	SMA SZA CLA			/CLEAN UP; THIS MIGHT SKIP.
B374,	374				/CONSTANT 0374; EXECUTES AS A HARMLESS
					/"AND" INSTRUCTION IF NOT SKIPPED.

/	START LOADING THE LATEST BLOCK BINARY DATA.

BNWREC,	TAD	B7756/(-22)		/22 GROUPS IN ONE BLOCK.
	DCA	BGRPCT			/INTO GROUP COUNTER.
BNWGRP,	TAD	B7772/(-6)		/6 BINARY WORDS IN A GROUP.
	DCA	BWDCT			/INTO WORD COUNTER.
	JMS	BGETWD			/GET LATEST CONTROL WORD.
	CLL RAR				/DO AN INITIAL SHIFT.
	SKP				/DON'T GET RUNNING VALUE FIRST TIME THROUGH.
BGRPLP,	TAD	BRLBTS			/GET CURRENT CONTROL WORD.
	RTL				/ROTATE NEXT PAIR TO LINK AND AC[0].
	DCA	BRLBTS			/STORE BACK FOR NEXT TIME.
	TAD	BRLBTS			/RESTORE LATEST TO AC[0].
	SPA CLA				/<EOF> OR FIELD SETTING?
	JMP	BEOF			/YES.  [LINK IS OFF IF <EOF>]
	JMS	BGETWD			/NO, GET DATA OR ORIGIN VALUE.
BCDF,	CDF	00+.-.			/PREVAILING FIELD BITS WILL BE FILLED IN.
	SNL				/SKIP IF ORIGIN SETTING.
	DCA I	BLOC			/STORE DATA WORD.
	CDF	00			/RESTORE OUR FIELD.
	ISZ	BLOC			/BUMP STORAGE POINTER [EVEN IF WRONG TO DO SO].
	SZL				/SKIP IF DATA WORD; UNIMPORTANT IF SKIPPED.
	DCA	BLOC			/SET NEW ORIGIN IF NOT DATA WORD.
BNXTWD,	ISZ	BWDCT			/DONE SIX WORDS IN THIS GROUP YET?
	JMP	BGRPLP			/NO, GO DO ANOTHER ONE
	ISZ	BGRPCT			/DONE 22 GROUPS IN THIS BLOCK YET?
	JMP	BNWGRP			/NO, GO DO ANOTHER ONE.

/	ALL BINARY DATA IN THIS BLOCK IS NOW LOADED; GO ON TO THE NEXT BLOCK.

	ISZ	BBLK			/BUMP TO NEXT BLOCK.
	JMP	BCONT			/KEEP GOING.
/	COMES HERE IF THE LATEST WORD IS EITHER A FIELD SETTING OR AT THE END OF DATA
/	WITHIN THE LATEST FILE.

BEOF,	SNL CLA				/FIELD SETTING?
	JMP	BREAD2			/NO, IT'S THE END OF THIS FILE.
	JMS	BGETWD			/YES, GET THE NEXT WORD.
	DCA	BCDF			/STORE IN-LINE AS A "CDF" INSTRUCTION
					/TO THE LATEST FIELD.
	JMP	BNXTWD			/GO DO ANOTHER WORD.

BGETWD,	.-.				/GET A NEW WORD ROUTINE.
	SDSQ				/QUAD LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, GET THE DATA WORD.
	JMP I	BGETWD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

/	WHEN ALL FILES HAVE BEEN LOADED, START THE PROGRAM HERE.  THE "BIN" PROGRAM
/	STORED THE STARTING FIELD HERE; THE STARTING ADDRESS IS IN "SPARM" [07756].

BCIF,	.-.^10+CIF CDF			/CHANGE FIELD TO STARTUP FIELD
	JMP I	SPARM			/AND START THE USER PROGRAM.

BLOC,	.-.				/MEMORY STORAGE POINTER.
BRLBTS,	.-.				/CONTROL FLAGS WORD STORED HERE.
BGRPCTR,.-.				/GROUP COUNTER.
BWDCT,	.-.				/WORD COUNTER.

/	DEFAULT STARTING ADDRESS [SAFE HALT].

BERR,					/DEFAULT STARTING ADDRESS.
B7772,	SPA SNA SZL CLA HLT		/THIS HALTS.	
B77,	77				/CONSTANT 0077; IF CONTINUE IS PRESSED, THIS
					/EXECUTES AS A HARMLESS "AND" INSTRUCTION THAT
					/CAN BE SKIPPED.

/	IN CASE CONTINUE IS PRESSED, JMP BACK TO THE SAFE HALT.

	JMP	BERR			/INFINITE LOOP IF CONTINUE PRESSED.

B7756,	7756				/CONSTANT 7756.
B7760,	7760				/CONSTANT 7760.
BBLK,	.-.				/LATEST FILE BLOCK.
BUNIT,	.-.				/CURRENT DRIVE IN BIT[0].
BDIRGO,	2000+.-.			/CURRENT TAPE DIRECTION AND GO BITS.
B4002,	4002				/CONSTANT 4002.
B7770,	7770				/CONSTANT 7770.
BFLPTR,	SFILES-1			/FILE LIST POINTER [INCREMENT BEFORE USING].
	0
SPARM,	BERR				/STARTING ADDRESS PASSED HERE [DEFAULT SET].
SFILES,	ZBLOCK	-.			/FILE HANDLES PASSED HERE.

	RELOC				/TURN OFF RELOCATION.
/	P?S/8 SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER RELOADER.

/	WHEN THE DEVICE-DEPENDENT BINARY FILE SLURP LOADER [AS OPPOSED TO THE
/	DEVICE-INDEPENDENT VIRTUAL BINARY FILE SLURP LOADER SUPPORTED ON ALL P?S/8
/	SYSTEMS] IS IN EFFECT, THE CONTENTS OF 07600-07777 NO LONGER CONTAIN THE
/	SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER; A REBOOT ROUTINE IS PROVIDED AT 07600 [AND SOME
/	ADDITIONAL WORDS FOLLOWING] TO ALLOW A TOTAL SYSTEM RELOAD CONSISTENT WITH
/	THE MORE USUAL RESTARTING OF THE SYSTEM AT THE SAME ADDRESS.

/	AT FIRST GLANCE, IT WOULD APPEAR THAT AS PART OF THE TRADEOFF OF LOADER
/	CHOICE, EITHER MORE EFFICIENT BINARY FILE LOADING [WITH NO NEED TO
/	WRITE-ENABLE THE SYSTEM DEVICE] AND THE LACK OF ACCESS TO THE NORMAL SYSTEM
/	KERNEL IS PROVIDED [OR THE SYSTEM DEVICE MUST BE WRITE-ENABLED AND THE SYSTEM
/	KERNEL IS RESIDENT IN MEMORY AS IS NORMALLY THE CASE].

/	HOWEVER, THIS IS NOT THE CASE.  BY INVOKING THE P?S/8 "BIN" PROGRAM FOR BINARY
/	FILE LOADING AND INCLUDING THE '/I' COMMAND-LINE OPTION SWITCH, AT THE END OF
/	ALL LOADING OF THE ENTIRETY OF THE CONTENTS OF ALL BINARY FILES, IT IS
/	POSSIBLE TO ALSO HAVE THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER AS WELL!  THERE ARE A FEW
/	PROVISOS:

/	1)	THE BLOCK JUST PAST THE END OF THE LAST FILE LOADED FROM THE SYSTEM
/		DEVICE WILL DETERMINE THE FINAL POSITION OF THE DEVICE.  [NOTE:
/		THE DESCRIPTION USED HERE ENVISIONS DECTAPE AND RELATED SYSTEMS].
/		HOWEVER, IF THE '/I' COMMAND-LINE OPTION SWITCH IS INVOKED, THERE WILL BE
/		SEVERAL ADDITIONAL DEVICE OPERATIONS THAT WILL TEND TO LEAVE THE
/		SYSTEM DEVICE POSITIONED JUST PAST BLOCK 0000 [WHICH WILL BE RELOADED
/		AS PART OF THE EXTRA TAPE MOTION].

/		ASSUMING A PROGRAM NORMALLY [EVENTUALLY] EXITS TO 07600, THIS IS NOT
/		AN OVERALL ISSUE PER SE; THE DEVICE MUST BE REWOUND TO BLOCK 0000 AS
/		PART OF THE SYSTEM RESTART OPERATION.  AS SUCH, THIS IS MERELY A
/		MATTER OF REWINDING BEFORE THE PROGRAM STARTS AS OPPOSED TO WHEN THE
/		PROGRAM EXITS.

/	2)	PART OF THE ADDITIONAL OPERATIONS IS A BINARY LOADING OPERATION OF
/		BLOCK 0060 ON THE SYSTEM DEVICE.  THIS IS THE MECHANISM THAT PERFORMS
/		THE SEEMINGLY IMPOSSIBLE TASK OF CONVERTING THE CONTENTS OF MEMORY
/		LOCATIONS 07600-07777 FROM THE SYSTEM RESTART BOOTSTAP [AND BINARY
/		LOADER ARTIFACTS] INTO THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000 [THE IMAGE OF THE
/		SYSTEM KERNEL].

/	3)	BLOCK 0060 IS APPENDED TO THE LIST OF SLURP BINARY INPUT FILES; THE
/		RELOAD OPERATION MUST BE PERFORMED AFTER THE NORMAL BINARY LOADING IS
/		OTHERWISE COMPLETE.  AS SUCH, BLOCK 0060 BECOMES THE LAST FILE HANDLE
/		IN THE PASSED FILE LIST.  [THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0060 ARE GENERALLY
/		CONSISTENT WITH A SMALL SLURP BINARY FILE THAT ENDS WITHIN THE FIRST
/		BLOCK.]

/		IF THE '/I' COMMAND-LINE OPTION IS IN EFFECT, THE MAXIMUM NUMBER OF
/		INPUT FILES IS LOWERED FROM 17 TO 16 TO ENSURE A FILE HANDLE CAN BE
/		PLACED AT THE END OF THE FILE LIST.
/		SINCE SLURP BINARY FILE FORMAT APPROACHES 6/7 OF THE LOADING
/		EFFICIENCY OF CORE-IMAGE DATA, THIS LOWERS THE MAXIMUM AMOUNT OF
/		BINARY DATA THAT CAN BE LOADED WITH A SINGLE COMMAND LINE FROM
/		APPROXIMATELY 7.25 MEMORY FIELDS TO 6.9 MEMORY FIELDS; HOWEVER, SINCE
/		TYPICAL PROGRAM LOADING FOR PRACTICAL PROGRAMMING PROJECTS SELDOM
/		EXCEEDS 8K OF PROGRAM MEMORY, THIS IS NOT AN IMPORTANT CONSIDERATION.
/		[NOTE: EXTREMELY LARGE PROGRAMS MIGHT REQUIRE TWO COMMANDS TO LOAD
/		ALL PROGRAM COMPONENTS.]
/		
/		FUTURE RELEASES OF P?S/8 WILL INCLUDE A BINARY CONVERSION PROGRAM
/		KNOWN AS "BINCON" WHICH HAS SEVERAL MODES OF OPERATION.  ADVANCED
/		USERS OF BINCON WILL BE ABLE TO CONCATENATE THE CONTENTS OF A GROUP OF
/		TFS SLURP BINARY FILES INTO A SINGLE EXTENDED-LENGTH FILE; AS SUCH,
/		PROGRAM LOADING WILL BE MORE EASILY ACCOMPLISHED IN FEWER UNIQUE FILE
/		NAMES.  THE MINOR RESTRICTION ON MAXIMUM PASSED FILES WILL BECOME
/		UNIMPORTANT.

/	THE FOLLOWING PROGRAMMING GENERATES THE IMAGE IN MEMORY OF A CONTRIVED SLURP
/	BINARY FILE TO BE WRITTEN OUT TO BLOCK 0060 OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE.  [NOTE:
/	SINCE THIS BLOCK IS READ ONLY BY THE SLURP LOADER, IT DOES NOT HAVE TO CONFORM
/	TO THE USUAL ORIGIN AND FIELD SETTING CONVENTIONS; THIS IS DONE TO MINIMIZE
/	THE SIZE OF THE DATA, ALL OF WHICH MUST FIT INTO A SINGLE BLOCK.]

	FIELD	IGEN&70%10		/RELOADER BLOCK GENERATION FIELD.

	*IGEN&7600			/RELOADER BLOCK GENERATION ADDRESS.

ICOR,	NOPUNCH				/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	FIELD	0			/WHERE THIS LOADS.

	*ICOR				/RESTORE ORIGIN [IN CASE FIELD CHANGES IT].

	ENPUNCH				/UN-FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	OR^4+FL^4+OR^4+OR^4+DA^4+DA	/CONTROL WORD.

	0200				/*200.  NOTE: THIS IS SUPERFLUOUS.

	CDF	00			/FIELD 0.

	0200				/*200.  NOTE: THIS IS SUPERFLUOUS.
/	MOST OF THE CODE LOADS IN OVER THE [NOW UNNEEDED] FILE LIST AREA.

	SFILES				/*SFILES; THIS IS WHERE THE CODE STARTS LOADING.

	RELOC	SFILES			/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

/	SINCE THE RELOADER SLURP FILE BLOCK IS THE LAST ELEMENT IN THE FILE LIST, THE
/	ENTIRE FILE LIST AREA IS AVAILABLE FOR THE PURPOSE OF [SELECTIVELY] USING THE
/	SLURP LOADER CODE FOR THE PURPOSE OF RELOADING THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.  THE
/	FOLLOWING CODE LOADS IN THE FILE LIST AREA WHICH IS ALLOWED AT THIS POINT.

RLPOINT,SBOOT/CLA!400			/CLEAR THE AC; ALSO STORAGE POINTER.

	TAD	BCIF			/GET PROGRAM STARTING FIELD INSTRUCTION.

	DA^4+DA^4+DA^4+DA^4+DA^4+DA	/NEXT CONTROL WORD.

	RELOC	.-1			/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	DCA	7772			/STORE OUT OF HARM'S WAY.

/	NOW READ THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IN ONE WORD AT A TIME.

RELOOP,	SDSQ				/QUAD LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, GET THE DATA WORD.
	DCA I	RLPOINTER		/STORE A WORD.
	ISZ	RLPOINTER		/BUMP TO NEXT.

	DA^4+DA^4+DA^4+DA^4+DA^4+DA	/NEXT CONTROL WORD.

	RELOC	.-1			/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	ISZ	RLCNTR			/DONE ENOUGH WORDS?
	JMP	RELOOP			/NO, KEEP GOING.
	SDLC				/YES, STOP THE DECTAPE.
RLCIF,	.-.^10+CIF CDF			/WILL BE THE STARTING FIELD INSTRUCTION.
	JMP I	SPARM			/GO START THE PROGRAM UP.

RLCNTR,	SBOOT-SPARM			/COUNT OF WORDS TO MOVE.
/	THE RELOADER CODE HAS NOW BEEN READ INTO THE FILE LIST AREA; THE SLURP LOADER
/	WILL NOW BE MODIFIED TO READ IN THE CONTENTS OF BLOCK 0000.

	OR^4+DA^4+OR^4+DA^4+OR^4+DA	/NEXT CONTROL WORD.

/	ALL OF THE RELOADER CODE WORDS HAVE BEEN READ IN.  THE SLURP LOADER IS NO
/	LONGER NEEDED FOR ITS NORMAL PURPOSE.  THE SLURP LOADER WILL NOW BE MODIFIED
/	TO CAUSE IT TO READ IN BLOCK 0000 DATA.

/	AFTER THE END OF FILE IS DETECTED IN THE [STILL-FUNCTIONING] SLURP LOADER, THE
/	CODE GOES BACK TO [NORMALLY] DETECT THE END OF THE FILE LIST [WHICH WOULD THEN
/	START THE PROGRAM UP].  BEFORE THAT HAPPENS, IT WILL BE MODIFIED TO INSTEAD
/	SEARCH FOR BLOCK 0000.  WHEN THE SEARCH COMPLETES AND BLOCK 0000 DATA IS
/	AVAILABLE, PREVENT THE SLURP LOADER READ OPERATIONS, AND INSTEAD EXECUTE THE
/	RELOAD CODE READ IN OVER THE [AVAILABLE] FILE LIST AREA.

	RELOC	BNWREC			/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	BNWREC				/*BNWREC.

	JMP	RLPOINTER		/RELOAD BLOCK 0000 INSTEAD OF SLURP LOADING.

/	THE SLURP LOADER SEARCH ROUTINE SUPPORTS BOTH DRIVE UNITS.  THE FOLLOWING CODE
/	FORCES ACCESS TO THE SYSTEM DRIVE UNIT [0].

	RELOC	BTURNX+5		/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	BTURNX+5			/*BTURNX+5.

	NOP				/THIS FORCES THE SYSTEM DEVICE UNIT [0].

/	WHEN THE SLURP LOADER GOES BACK TO DETECT ANOTHER FILE, FORCE IT TO INSTEAD
/	USE BLOCK 0000 AND START A SEARCH ON THAT BLOCK.

	RELOC	BREAD			/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER

	BREAD				/*BREAD.

	JMP	BREAD+5			/FORCE BLOCK 0000 TO BE LOCATED FOR READING.

	RELOC	SFILES+12		/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER [REALLY DOESN'T MATTER].

	EF^4+EF^4+EF^4+EF^4+EF^4+EF	/NEXT CONTROL WORD.

	ZBLOCK	6			/NO DATA, JUST END OF FILE.

/	THIS IS THE END OF FILE.  THE SLURP LOADER WILL NOW GO BACK AND START LOADING
/	IN [MOST OF] BLOCK 0000.
	RELOC				/TURN OFF RELOCATION.

/	END OF THE RELOADER FILE IMAGE WHICH MUST FIT IN ONE PAGE.  CHECK TO MAKE SURE
/	THE CODE DOES NOT EXCEED THIS RESTRICTION.  [MOST SLURP FILES ARE SEVERAL
/	BLOCKS LONG; THIS IS NOT CONSIDERED A "FILE".]

	ICOREND=ICOR+200		/FIRST LOCATION AFTER MAXIMUM
					/SIZE RELOADER CODE IMAGE.

	IMAGEND=.			/FIRST LOCATION AFTER ACTUAL RELOADER IMAGE.

	IM=ICOREND-IMAGEND		/COUNT OF ADDITIONAL WORDS AVAILABLE.

	IFNZRO	IM&4000	<ERROR	.>	/ENSURE CODE LIMIT NOT EXCEEDED.
/	STUFF TO PUT IN SOMEWHERE ELSE LATER.  [AFTER I FIGURE OUT MORE THINGS!]

/	SYSTEM NAME TDRM [THIS IS MEANINGFUL TO THE LOGICAL CONSOLE OVERLAY SUPPORT
/	SECTION OF THIS FILE].

/	FOR NOW, THE MR8E-C SOURCE CODE IS NOT INCLUDED; IT IS NEEDED DURING THE
/	ACTUAL USAGE ON NON-ROM SYSTEMS.  HOWEVER, THE SOURCE CODE IN THIS SECTION
/	CAN BE ASSEMBLED WITHOUT THE INCLUSION OF THAT SOURCE MODULE [BEING DEVELOPED
/	SEPARATELY AND IN PRE-FINAL STATE].  WHEN IT IS STABLE, IT WILL BE ADDED TO
/	THIS SOURCE FILE FOR COMPLETENESS.

	$				/THAT'S ALL FOR NOW, FOLKS!



/	THE ROM FOLLOWS:


/	SOURCE RECREATION BY:

/	CHARLES LASNER	P?S NY

/	LAST EDIT:	26-JAN-2016	07:00:00	CJL
	XLIST	OFF			/TURN LISTING OFF NOW.

	IFNDEF	OFF	<OFF=	1>	/TURN LISTING OFF WITH THIS.

	IFNDEF	ON	<ON=	0>	/TURN LISTING ON WITH THIS.

	XLIST	ON			/TURN LISTING ON NOW.

/	NOTE: MUST BE ASSEMBLED WITH THE '/0' SWITCH CLEAR [P?S/8 PAL].

/	NOTE: MUST BE ASSEMBLED WITH THE '/O' SWITCH SET [OS/8 PAL8].

/	THE MR8E-C ROM IS USED WITH THE PRIMARY TD8E CONTROLLER [DEVICE CODE 77] TO
/	ACHIEVE BOOTABLE OPERATING SYSTEMS REQUIRING MINIMAL READ/WRITE MEMORY.  THE
/	PDP-8 CONVENTION OF THE ENTIRE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER BEING CONTAINED IN THE
/	READ/WRITE MEMORY OF LOCATIONS 07600-07777 CAN BE ACHIEVED [PERHAPS WITH SOME
/	EFFORT].  DEPENDING ON THE PARTICULAR OPERATING SYSTEM, THIS MAY BE A
/	MANDATORY REQUIREMENT.  ALTERNATIVELY, THE OPERATING SYSTEM MUST BE
/	RECONFIGURED TO HANDLE THE SYSTEM REQUIREMENTS IN SOME OTHER SYSTEM-DEPENDENT
/	MANNER [SUCH AS REQUIRING ADDITIONAL MEMORY] WHICH IMPACTS ON OTHER ASPECTS OF
/	THE OPERATING SYSTEM AND/OR MACHINE COSTS.  THE MR8E-C ROM ALLOWS A VIABLE
/	SOLUTION AS LONG AS THE DEVICE HANDLER CAN FIT INTO THE DESIGNATED CODE SPACE
/	OF 07600-07777 [WITH EACH SYSTEM RESERVING CERTAIN INTERNAL LOCATIONS THAT
/	INTERACT WITH THE ROM] WITHOUT UNDUE COMPROMISE.

/	SINCE PDP-8 MEMORY CONVENTIONS ARE SUCH THAT ONLY ENTIRE 4096 WORD MEMORY
/	FIELDS ARE UTILIZED, THAT A PORTION OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER IS ACTUALLY
/	IMPLEMENTED AS A ROM IS UNDETECTABLE TO THE REST OF THE OPERATING SYSTEM; THE
/	ROM HAS NO IMPACT ON MEMORY SIZE.  THERE IS NO GENERAL EXPECTATION OF THE
/	AVAILABILITY OF THE HIGHEST TWO PAGES OF MEMORY [77400-77777] UNLESS ALL LOWER
/	ADDRESSES IN THE ENTIRE MEMORY SPACE [00000-07377] EXIST.  AS SUCH, THE ROM IS
/	ESSENTIALLY "INVISIBLE" TO ALL CONFORMING COMPONENTS OF ALL OPERATING SYSTEMS
/	COMPATIBLE WITH THIS UNUSUAL MEMORY CONFIGURATION.  THE ONLY PRACTICAL
/	CONSIDERATION IS THE EFFECTIVE MAXIMUM MEMORY SIZE CAN NEVER BE THE FULL 32K
/	ADDRESS SPACE OF THE PDP-8; THE LARGEST MEMORY SIZE IS AT MOST 28K.

/	THE MR8E-C WAS DESIGNED FOR USE IN SMALLER CONFIGURATIONS; IF MORE MEMORY IS
/	AVAILABLE, VARIATIONS OF THE SYSTEM CONFIGURATION WILL GENERALLY BE POSSIBLE
/	TO TAKE ADVANTAGE.  THUS, ON LARGER SYSTEMS, THE MAXIMUM MEMORY SIZE
/	ACHIEVABLE WOULD BE THE USUAL 32k.  [NOTE: DURING THE ECONOMIC LIFETIME OF THE
/	TD8E AND COMPATIBLE PDP-8 MODELS, AN ADDITIONAL 4K OF MEMORY MIGHT BE
/	PROHIBITIVELY EXPENSIVE COMPARED TO THE SMALLER COST OF THE MD8E-C ROM; THUS,
/	FOR MANY CUSTOMERS WITH MODEST BUDGETS, THE MR8E-C PROVIDED TO BE A
/	COST-EFFECTIVE ALTERNATIVE.]

/	FOR SOME CUSTOMERS, THE CONVENIENCE OF BOOTING THE SYSTEM WITHOUT HAVING TO
/	TOGGLE IN A BOOTSTRAP ROUTINE JUSTIFIED THE MI8E DIODE-BASED BOOTSTRAP LOADER
/	WHICH IS INITIATED FROM THE FRONT PANEL.  THE MR8E-C ROM HAS A BUILT-IN
/	BOOTSTRAP THAT IS FUNCTIONALLY EQUIVALENT.  THUS, FOR A WHOLE CLASS OF
/	ECONOMY-MINDED CUSTOMERS, THE MR8E-C WAS A WISE CHOICE.
/	THERE ARE AT LEAST FOUR OPERATING SYSTEMS THAT SUPPORT THIS CONFIGURATION:

/	1)	EDU-SYSTEM 15/30 IS AN OPERATING SYSTEM BASED ON EDU-SYSTEM 30, WHICH
/		IN TURN IS BASED ON POLY BASIC; ALL OF THESE SYSTEMS ONLY SUPPORT THE
/		BASIC COMPUTER LANGUAGE.  MOST OF THE WORK ON THESE SYSTEMS WAS DONE
/		BY RICHARD LARY AND LENNY ELEKMAN WHILE STUDENTS AT THE POLYTECHNIC
/		INSTITUTE OF BROOKLYN.

/		ALL OF THESE SYSTEMS ARE DESIGNED TO RUN ON 4K MACHINES; ANY EXTENDED
/		MEMORY [IF PRESENT] IS IGNORED.  WHEN THE SYSTEM DEVICE IS THE TD8E
/		SIMPLE DECTAPE CONTROLLER, THE ROM ALLOWS SUPPORT OF THIS SPECIFIC
/		CONFIGURATION.  [NOTE: FIELD 7 OF EXTENDED MEMORY IS NOT ALLOWED SINCE
/		MEMORY LOCATIONS 77400-77777 ARE ALWAYS OCCUPIED BY THE MR8E-C ROM.]

/		NOTE: IN GENERAL, EDU-SYSTEM 15/30 MAY REQUIRE THE KK8F OR KK8A CPU
/		DUE TO THE USE OF INSTRUCTIONS NOT FOUND ON PDP-8 MODELS PRIOR TO THE
/		PDP-8/E; HOWEVER, IT IS STRONGLY RECOMMENDED IN THE CASE OF THE MR8E-C
/		ROM-BASED CONFIGURATION TO ONLY USE THE KK8F CPU.  THIS WOULD EXCLUDE
/		THE PDP-8/A 400 SERIES BUT WOULD ALLOW THE PDP-8/A 600 SERIES WHICH
/		USES THE KK8F CPU AS FOUND IN THE PDP-8/E, PDP-8/F AND PDP-8/M.  THE
/		SAME RECOMMENDATION APPLIES TO THE OTHER SUPPORTED OPERATING SYSTEMS.
/		THE KK8A CPU AS FOUND IN THE PDP-8/A 400 SERIES MAY NOT BE FAST ENOUGH
/		FOR RELIABLE TD8E OPERATION DUE TO THE SLOWER SPEED OF THE CPU AND THE
/		OVERHEAD ASSOCIATED WITH THE MR8E-C ROM-BASED CODE.

/	2)	OS/8 CAN BE CONFIGURED TO RUN ON ANY MODEL PDP-8 WITH AT LEAST 8K OF
/		MEMORY.  [12K IS REQUIRED TO RUN OS/8 BATCH.]  WHEN THE MR8E-C ROM IS
/		USED WITH THE TD8E, THIS DESIRABLE CONFIGURATION IS ACHIEVABLE.  AT
/		LEAST 12K OF MEMORY IS NEEDED IN OS/8 IF THE MR8E-C ROM IS NOT USED
/		[WHEN THE TD8E IS USED AS THE SYSTEM DEVICE] WHICH IMPACTS NEGATIVELY
/		ON CERTAIN OS/8 CONFIGUURATIONS.  NOTE: CERTAIN OS/8 CONFIGURATIONS
/		REQUIRE 12K BECAUSE THERE IS NO ANALOG TO THE MR8E-C AVAILABLE.  PDP-8
/		PERIPHERALS ARE SOMETIMES INADEQUATELY DESIGNED AND WILL LEAD TO
/		DECREASED OS/8 PERFORMANCE; THE TD8E WITH THE MR8E-C ALLOWS OS/8 TO
/		AVOID THIS ISSUE ON SMALLER MACHINE CONFIGURATIONS.

/	3)	COS-300/310 CAN BE CONFIGURED TO RUN ON ANY MODEL PDP-8 WITH AT LEAST
/		8K MEMORY.  [INTERNALLY, IT IS LARGELY BASED ON OS/8 AND ALL OF THE
/		CONSIDERATIONS DISCUSSED IN 2) ABOVE APPLY TO COS-300/310 AS WELL.]

/	4)	P?S/8 IS AN OPERATING SYSTEM THAT CAN RUN ON ANY MODEL PDP-8 WITH AT
/		LEAST 4K OF RAM MEMORY.  VARIOUS P?S/8 COMPONENTS AND SYSTEM PROGRAMS
/		CAN TAKE ADVANTAGE OF ALL AVAILABLE MEMORY UP TO 32K.  IN THE SPECIFIC
/		CONFIGURATION OF THE TD8E AND THE MR8E-C ROM, MEMORY SIZE IS LIMITED
/		TO AT MOST 28K DUE TO THE PRESENCE OF THE ROM IN FIELD 7.  P?S/8 CAN
/		RUN ADEQUATELY IN 4K AND ALLOWS GENERAL-PURPOSE USE IN ANY SUPPORTED
/		LANGUAGE.
/	NUMERIC LOAD DEFINITIONS.

	NL7775=	CLA CLL CMA RTL		/LOAD AC WITH 7775.
	NL7777=	CLA CMA			/LOAD AC WITH 7777.

/	MR8E-C ROM DEFINITIONS.

/	THE MR8E-C ROM IS LOCATED AT THE HIGHEST MEMORY LOCATIONS OF THE 32K ADDRESS
/	SPACE; AS SUCH, IT WILL NEVER AFFECT ANY COMPATIBLE OPERATING SYSTEM THAT
/	DYNAMICALLY DETERMINES MEMORY SIZE BY DISCOVERING COMPLETE FIELDS OF
/	READ/WRITE MEMORY.

	F2SET=	7552			/CONTINUATION OF FUNCTION CALL ADDRESS.
	ROMADR=	7400			/ROM LOADS INTO 77400-77777.
	ROMBOOT=7470			/ROM-BASED BOOTSTRAP ROUTINE ADDRESS.
	ROMFLD=	70			/ROM LOADS INTO FIELD 7.
	ROMGO=	7405			/RETRY ADDRESS AFTER ERRORS.

/	SOURCE CONTROL DEFINITIONS.

/	BY ASSEMBLING THE SOURCE CODE INTO FIELD 3, THE BINARY OUTPUT CAN BE COMPARED
/	TO AN IMAGE LOADED INTO FIELD 5 USING A DESIGNATED BINARY COMPARE PROGRAM.

	IFNDEF	SRCFLD	<SRCFLD=ROMFLD>	/LOAD INTO FIELD 7 FOR OPERATIONAL USAGE.
/	SRCFLD=	30			/LOAD INTO FIELD 3 WHEN COMPARING TO IMAGE.

/	A SKELETAL COPY OF THE RESERVED LOCATIONS IN FIELD 0 CAN BE GENERATED AS PART
/	OF THIS ASSEMBLY.  THIS CAN BE DISABLED TO ALLOW INCLUSION OF THE ROM SOURCE
/	CODE INTO AN ASSEMBLY MODULE FOR THE DEVICE HANDLER OF ANY OF THE COMPATIBLE
/	SYSTEMS.

	IFNDEF	SYSKEL	<SYSKEL=1>	/GENERATE THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER SKELETON.
/	SYSKEL=	0			/DO NOT GENERATE THE DEVICE HANDLER SKELETON.

/	TD8E SIMPLE DECTAPE INPUT/OUTPUT DEFINITIONS.

	SDCODE=	77			/DEVICE CODE FOR TD8E SIMPLE DECTAPE [PRIMARY].

	SDSS=	SDCODE^10+6001		/SIMPLE DECTAPE SKIP ON SINGLE LINE FLAG.
	SDST=	SDCODE^10+6002		/SIMPLE DECTAPE SKIP IF TIME ERROR FLAG SET.
	SDSQ=	SDCODE^10+6003		/SIMPLE DECTAPE SKIP ON QUAD LINE FLAG.
	SDLC=	SDCODE^10+6004		/SIMPLE DECTAPE LOAD COMMAND REGISTER.
	SDLD=	SDCODE^10+6005		/SIMPLE DECTAPE LOAD DATA REGISTER.
	SDRC=	SDCODE^10+6006		/SIMPLE DECTAPE READ COMMAND REGISTER.
	SDRD=	SDCODE^10+6007		/SIMPLE DECTAPE READ DATA REGISTER.
/	FIELD 0 DEVICE HANDLER DEFINITIONS.

/	THE FOLLOWING LOCATIONS WITHIN THE LAST PAGE OF FIELD 0 [07600-07777] ARE
/	RESERVED BY THE ROM [AND IN SOME CASES ARE THE HEADERS OF COMPLETELY RESERVED
/	SUBROUTINES].  COMPATIBLE SYSTEMS MUST UTILIZE THESE LOCATIONS CONSISTENTLY
/	WITH THEIR FUNCTION WITHIN THE ROM.

/	NOTE: IF ENABLED, THE SKELETAL VERSION OF THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER CONTAINING
/	ALL RELEVANT ROUTINES WILL ILLUSTRATE THE FULL DEPENDENCIES.  IN GENERAL, THE
/	ROM-BASED ROUTINES REQUIRE THESE ROUTINES BE LOCATED IN FIELD 0 AS DEFINED.
/	HOWEVER, THE SPECIFIC OPERATING SYSTEM IS RESPONSIBLE FOR PLACING ALL SUCH
/	ROUTINES INTO FIELD 0 RAM MEMORY.

/	ALL ADDRESSES NOT SHOWN IN THE SKELETAL VERSION ARE AVAILABLE TO PERFORM
/	SYSTEM-DEPENDENT FUNCTIONS ACCORDING TO THEIR SPECIFIC REQUIREMENTS.  SEVERAL
/	LOCATIONS ARE REQUIRED TO ALLOW INDIRECT ACCESS TO ROM-BASED ROUTINES.
/	HOWEVER, THE HANDLERS CAN DESIGNATE WHERE TO PLACE THESE SPECIFIC POINTERS.

	BUFADR=	7751			/READ/WRITE BUFFER ADDRESS.
	BUFPTR=	7753			/BUFFER POINTER FOR READ AND STORE ROUTINES.
	CIFR=	7674			/INTERNAL RETURN ADDRESS FOR WRITING.
	DRVUNIT=7731			/DECTAPE DRIVE UNIT.
	EQUFUN=	7704			/EQUIVALENCE CHECKSUM ROUTINE.
	EQUTMP=	7750			/TEMPORARY FOR EQUIVALENCE CHECKSUM ROUTINE.
	GETWRD=	7647			/GET A WORD FROM THE CALLER'S BUFFER ROUTINE.
	PAGCNT=	7752			/TRANSFER PAGE COUNT.
	PUTWRD=	7654			/PUT A WORD INTO THE CALLER'S BUFFER ROUTINE.
	RDQUAD=	7676			/READ A 12-BIT WORD ROUTINE.
	SBLK=	7734			/LATEST DECTAPE BLOCK NUMBER.
	SCKSUM=	7733			/RUNNING CHECKSUM WORD.
	SERR=	7661			/ERROR HANDLER ADDRESS.
	SEXIT=	7722			/ROM EXIT WHEN FINISHED.
	SFUNCTI=7732			/FUNCTION WORD.
	SUNIT=	7735			/DRIVE UNIT FOR LATEST HANDLER CALL.
	TRYCNT=	7730			/RETRY COUNTER.
	WORDCT=	7754			/WORD COUNTER FOR LATEST DATA BLOCK.
	WRQ=	7724			/WRITE QUAD ROUTINE ROM CONTINUATION ADDRESS.
	WRQUAD=	7671			/WRITE A 12-BIT WORD ROUTINE.
	FIELD	SRCFLD%10		/BINARY LOADS INTO THIS FIELD.

	NOPUNCH				/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	FIELD	ROMFLD%10		/FIELD FOR DEFINITIONS.

	ENPUNCH				/UN-FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	*ROMADR				/LOADING ADDRESS FOR DEFINITIONS.

/	COMES HERE TO SEARCH FOR THE NEXT BLOCK IN A MULTIPLE BLOCK TRANSFER.

NXTBLK,	TAD I	PBUFADR/(BUFADRESS)	/GET THE BUFFER POINTER ADDRESS.
	TAD	ROM200/(200)		/UPDATE TO NEXT PAGE.
	DCA I   PBUFADR/(BUFADRESS)	/STORE THE BUFFER POINTER ADDRESS BACK.
	ISZ I	PSBLK/(SBLK)		/BUMP TO NEXT BLOCK.
	STL				/SECONDARY TRANSFERS ALWAYS SEARCH FORWARD.

/	COMES HERE WITH THE LINK SET TO THE COMPLEMENT OF THE DESIRED DIRECTION.

ROMGO,	CLA CML RTR			/INVERT LINK AND MOVE TO DIRECTION BIT.
        TAD     ROM1200/(1200)		/ADD ON GO BIT [ONLY HIGHEST FOUR BITS USED].
        TAD I   PSUNIT/(SUNIT)		/ADD ON DRIVE UNIT BIT.
        SDLC				/LOAD THE COMMAND REGISTER.

/	WE NEED TO DELAY TWO TAPE WORDS BEFORE CHECKING THE MARK REGISTER.

	SDSQ				/FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, CLEAR THE FLAG.
	SDSQ				/FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, CLEAR THE FLAG.

/	CHECK FOR END ZONE OR BLOCK MARK VALUES.

SRCHMOR,SDSS				/SINGLE LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRC				/YES, READ THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	CLL RTL				/MOVE THE DIRECTION BIT TO THE LINK; THE
					/MARK WINDOW IS MOVED TO AC BITS[4-9].
	AND	ROM374/(374)		/ISOLATE MARK TRACK BITS.
	TAD	ROM7670/(-110)		/COMPARE TO END ZONE MARK VALUE.
	SNA				/SKIP IF OTHER.
	JMP	ENDZONE			/HANDLE END ZONE THERE.
	TAD	ROM7760/(-130+110)	/COMPARE TO BLOCK MARK VALUE.
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF IT MATCHES.
	JMP	SRCHMORE		/KEEP GOING IF NEITHER MATCH.
/	A BLOCK MARK WAS DETECTED WHILE SEARCHING FOR THE DESIRED BLOCK.  THE LATEST
/	BLOCK VALUE AND DIRECTION OF TAPE MOTION WILL BE TESTED ACCORDINGLY.

/	DECTAPE SEARCHING RULES.

/	1)	WHILE SEARCHING FORWARDS, IF THE LATEST BLOCK IS LOWER THAN THE
/		DESIRED BLOCK, CONTINUE THE SEARCH MOVING FORWARDS.

/	2)	WHILE SEARCHING FORWARDS, IF THE LATEST BLOCK IS THE DESIRED BLOCK,
/		THEN SWITCH TO READ OR WRITE AS REQUIRED.

/	3)	WHILE SEARCHING FORWARDS, IF THE LATEST BLOCK IS HIGHER THAN THE
/		DESIRED BLOCK, THE TAPE MUST BE REVERSED.  [NOTE: IF THE FORWARD END
/		ZONE IS DETECTED, THE TAPE MOTION MUST BE REVERSED.]

/	4)	WHILE SEARCHING IN REVERSE, IF THE LATEST BLOCK IS HIGHER THAN THE
/		DESIRED BLOCK MINUS THE UNDERSHOOT FACTOR, CONTINUE THE SEARCH
/		MOVING IN REVERSE.

/	5)	WHILE SEARCHING IN REVERSE, IF THE LATEST BLOCK IS AT THE BLOCK EQUAL
/		TO THE DESIRED BLOCK MINUS THE UNDERSHOOT FACTOR, THE TAPE SHOULD BE
/		TURNED AROUND.  ONCE THE TAPE IS MOVING FORWARDS AND UP TO SPEED, 2)
/		ABOVE WILL APPLY.  [NOTE: FOR VERY LOW BLOCK NUMBERS, THIS WILL NEVER
/		HAPPEN; HOWEVER, THE END ZONE WILL BE DETECTED AND THE TAPE MOTION
/		MUST BE REVERSED.  THERE IS SUFFICIENT INTER-BLOCK ZONE WORDS JUST
/		AFTER THE END ZONE TO ALLOW DETECTION OF BLOCK 0000 AFTER TURNING THE
/		TAPE AROUND IN THE END ZONE.]

/	6)	WHILE SEARCHING IN REVERSE, IF THE LATEST BLOCK NUMBER IS LOWER THAN
/		THE DESIRED BLOCK MINUS THE UNDERSHOOT FACTOR, THE TAPE MOTION MUST BE
/		REVERSED.  ONCE THE TAPE IS MOVING FORWARDS AND UP TO SPEED, 1) ABOVE
/		APPLIES UNLESS NOW AT THE DESIRED BLOCK, IN WHICH CASE 2) APPLIES.

/	NOTE: IN MOST DECTAPE SEARCH IMPLEMENTATIONS, THE UNDERSHOOT FACTOR IS USUALLY
/	0002; HOWEVER, THE MORE CONSERVATIVE VALUE OF 0003 IS USED IN MOST TD8E
/	APPLICATIONS.  IT IS THEORIZED THAT THE EARLIEST TD8E SEARCH ROUTINE WAS
/	COPIED FROM A VERY OLD IMPLEMENTATION OF A DEVICE HANDLER FOR TC01/TC08
/	DECTAPE THAT WAS OBSOLETE; MOST TC01/TC08 SEARCH ROUTINES USE AN UNDERSHOOT
/	FACTOR OF 0002.  THERE ARE NO REPORTED PROBLEMS IN ANY SITUATION WHERE
/	PROPERLY ADJUSTED TU55 OR TU56 DRIVES ARE USED.  ALL KNOWN TD8E ROUTINES
/	WRITTEN BY DEC PERSONNEL CLEARLY IMITATE THE ORIGINAL TD8E SUBROUTINE THAT WAS
/	RELEASED PRIOR TO THE MR8E-C ROM.  AS SUCH, TURN-AROUND PERFORMANCE ON ALL
/	TD8E SYSTEMS RUNNING DEC SOFTWARE IS SOMEWHAT SLUGGISH.

/	SEARCH ALGORITHM DETAILS.

/	THE LATEST BLOCK IS COMPARED TO THE DESIRED BLOCK USING ONE'S COMPLEMENT
/	ARITHMETIC; THE LINK IS CLEAR IF THE CURRENT TAPE MOTION IS FORWARD AND SET IF
/	THE CURRENT TAPE MOTION IS IN THE REVERSE DIRECTION.
/	POSSIBLE SCENARIOS:

/	1)	THE TAPE IS MOVING FORWARDS AND POSITIONED AT A BLOCK LOWER THAN THE
/		DESIRED BLOCK.  THE LINK IS CLEAR INDICATING FORWARD MOTION.  THE
/		COMPARISON FAILS, AND THE LINK IS SET.  AS SUCH, THE SEARCH CONTINUES
/		WITHOUT CHANGING THE DIRECTION OF MOTION.

/	2)	THE TAPE IS MOVING FORWARDS AND POSITIONED AT THE DESIRED BLOCK.  THE
/		LINK IS CLEAR INDICATING FORWARD MOTION.  THE COMPARISON SUCCEEDS AND
/		THE LINK IS SET.  AS SUCH, THE TAPE CAN BE READ TO LOCATE ADDITIONAL
/		DATA WORDS TO POSITION THE TAPE FOR EITHER READING OR WRITING.

/	3)	THE TAPE IS MOVING FORWARDS AND POSITIONED PAST THE DESIRED BLOCK.
/		THE LINK IS CLEAR INDICATING FORWARD MOTION.  THE COMPARISON FAILS AND
/		THE LINK STAYS CLEAR.  AS SUCH, THE TAPE MOTION NEEDS TO BE REVERSED.

/	4)	THE TAPE IS MOVING IN REVERSE AND POSITIONED AT A BLOCK HIGHER THAN
/		THE DESIRED BLOCK MINUS THE UNDERSHOOT FACTOR.  THE LINK IS SET
/		INDICATING REVERSE MOTION.  THE COMPARISON FAILS AND THE LINK STAYS
/		SET.  AS SUCH, THE SEARCH CONTINUES WITHOUT CHANGING THE DIRECTION OF
/		MOTION.

/	5)	THE TAPE IS MOVING IN REVERSE AND POSITIONED AT THE DESIRED BLOCK
/		MINUS THE UNDERSHOOT FACTOR.  THE LINK IS SET INDICATING REVERSE
/		MOTION.  THE COMPARISON SUCCEEDS AND THE LINK IS CLEARED.  AS SUCH,
/		THE TAPE MOTION NEEDS TO BE REVERSED.

/	6)	THE TAPE IS MOVING IN REVERSE AND POSITIONED AT A BLOCK LOWER THAN THE
/		DESIRED BLOCK MINUS THE UNDERSHOOT FACTOR.  THE LINK IS SET INDICATING
/		REVERSE MOTION.  THE COMPARISON FAILS AND THE LINK IS CLEARED.  AS
/		SUCH, THE TAPE MOTION NEEDS TO BE REVERSED.

/	IN ALL CASES, DETECTION OF THE END ZONE REQUIRES REVERSAL OF TAPE DIRECTION.

	SDRD				/GET THE LATEST BLOCK NUMBER
	SZL				/SKIP IF GOING FORWARDS.
	TAD	ROM3/(3)		/ADD ON UNDERSHOOT FACTOR IF MOVING BACKWARDS.
	CMA				/INVERT FOR ONE'S COMPLEMENT COMPARISON.
	TAD I	PSBLK/(SBLK)		/COMPARE TO DESIRED BLOCK.
	CMA				/INVERT BACK FOR TEST.
	SNA				/SKIP IF NOT THE DESIRED BLOCK.
	JMP	BLKFND			/JUMP IF CORRECT BLOCK TO TEST FURTHER.
ROM7670,SNA SZL CLA			/SKIP IF THE LINK IS CLEAR; THE SNA IS
					/EXTRANEOUS AND USED TO FORM THE CONSTANT 7670.
	JMP	SRCHMORE		/KEEP GOING IN THE SAME DIRECTION IF LINK SET.

/	THE TAPE IS MOVING IN A DIRECTION COUNTER TO THE CURRENT INTENDED DIRECTION.
/	IF THE TAPE IS MOVING IN REVERSE OR THE REVERSE END ZONE HAS BEEN DETECTED,
/	THE DIRECTION OF MOTION MUST BE REVERSED.  THE LINK IS CLEAR WHICH ALLOWS THE
/	TAPE TO HARMLESSLY TURN AROUND.

/	IF THE TAPE IS MOVING FORWARD OR THE FORWARD END ZONE HAS BEEN DETECTED, AN
/	ERROR CONDITION IS INDICATED [WHICH CAN BE TOLERATED UP TO THREE TIMES].
ENDZONE,SDRC				/READ THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	CLL RTL				/DIRECTION BIT TO LINK.
	SZL CLA				/SKIP IF TOO FAR UP THE TAPE OR IN THE END ZONE.
	JMP	ROMGO			/GO TURN THE TAPE AROUND THERE AND CONTINUE THE
					/SEARCH OPERATION.

/	AT THIS POINT, MOVING FORWARD IS CONSIDERED AN ERROR.

	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMP I	PSERR/(SERR)		/CHECK THE RETRY COUNT AND CONTINUE THERE.

/	WE ARE POSITIONED AT THE PROPER BLOCK NUMBER; CHECK IF THE TAPE IS MOVING
/	FORWARD; IF MOVING IN THE REVERSE DIRECTION, THE LINK IS CLEAR WHICH WILL
/	ALLOW THE TAPE TO TURN AROUND.

BLKFND,	SZL CLA				/SKIP IF MOVING FORWARD AT THE DESIRED BLOCK.
	JMP	ROMGO			/GO THERE TO TURN THE TAPE AROUND.

/	THE TAPE IS PROPERLY POSITIONED; CONTINUE SETTING UP THE DATA TRANSFER.

	TAD I	PBUFADR/(BUFADRESS)	/GET THE BUFFER ADDRESS FOR THE DATA TRANSFER.
	JMP I	PRWCONT/(RWCONT)	/CONTINUE THERE.	

ROM7600,7600				/CONSTANT 7600.
ROM200,	200				/CONSTANT 0200.
ROM374,	374				/CONSTANT 0374.
ROM7760,7760				/CONSTANT 7760.
ROM3,	3				/CONSTANT 0003.
PBUFADR,BUFADR				/POINTER TO BUFFER ADDRESS.
PSBLK,	SBLK				/POINTER TO LATEST BLOCK TO TRANSFER.
PSUNIT,	SUNIT				/POINTER TO SUNIT.
PSERR,	SERR				/POINTER TO SERR.
PRWCONT,RWCONT				/POINTER TO THE REST OF THE READ/WRITE CODE.
/	BUILT-IN BOOTSTRAP STARTS HERE.  SET THE FRONT-PANEL SWITCHES TO 7470.  PRESS
/	EXTENDED ADDRESS LOAD, ADDRESS LOAD, CLEAR AND THEN CONTINUE TO GET HERE.

ROMBOOT,CDF	00			/ENSURE FIELD 0 FOR INDIRECT STORAGE.

/	A SUBROUTINE MUST BE COPIED INTO FIELD 0 TO READ THE DATA WORDS ON THE DECTAPE
/	INTO MEMORY ACCORDING TO THE STANDARD TD8E CONVENTIONS.

        TAD     STRWRD+1+RLFACTOR	/GET "DCA I STRPTR".
	DCA I	PSTRW1/(STRWRD+1)	/STORE IT IN FIELD 0 WHERE IT EXECUTES.
        TAD     STRWRD+2+RLFACTOR	/GET "CIF 70".
	DCA I	PSTRW2/(STRWRD+2)	/STORE IT IN FIELD 0 WHERE IT EXECUTES.
        TAD     STRWRD+3+RLFACTOR	/GET "JMP I STRWRD".
	DCA I	PSTRW3/(STRWRD+3)	/STORE IT IN FIELD 0 WHERE IT EXECUTES.
        TAD     STRPTR+RLFACTOR		/GET "BOOTBUFFER".
	DCA I	PSTRPTR/(STRPTR)	/STORE IT IN FIELD 0 WHERE IT IS USED.

/	REWIND THE TAPE UNTIL REVERSE END ZONE MARKS ARE FOUND.

	TAD	RM3000/(3000)		/GET REVERSE DIRECTION AND GO BITS [ON UNIT 0].
	SDLC				/LOAD THE COMMAND REGISTER TO START REWINDING.
EZLOOP,	SDSS				/SINGLE LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRC				/YES, READ IN THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	AND	RM77/(77)		/JUST MARK WINDOW BITS.
	TAD	ROM7756/(-22)		/COMPARE TO END ZONE MARK VALUE.
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF IT MATCHES.
	JMP	EZLOOP			/KEEP GOING IF NOT.

/	THE END ZONE HAS BEEN FOUND WHILE GOING BACKWARDS.  WE NEED TO TURN THE
/	DECTAPE AROUND AND SEARCH FORWARD FOR THE 31 CODE [PART OF THE BLOCK MARK AND
/	THE REVERSE GUARD MARK].

ROM1200,TAD	ROM1200/(1200)		/SET UNIT 0 AND GO BITS TO GO FORWARD; [NOTE:
					/ONLY BITS[0-3] ARE USED.]
	SDLC				/START THE DECTAPE MOVING FORWARD.
LOOP31,	SDSS				/SINGLE LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRC				/YES, READ IN THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	AND	RM77/(77)		/JUST MARK WINDOW BITS.
	TAD	ROM7747/(-31)		/COMPARE TO 31 CODE.
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF IT MATCHES
	JMP	LOOP31			/GO BACK IF NOT.
/	WE ARE POSITIONED AT THE 31 MARK WINDOW VALUE.  START STORING DATA WORDS WHERE
/	THEY BELONG.  [NOTE: THE FIRST FOUR ARE BEFORE THE DATA WORDS OF BLOCK 0000.]

BTRDLP,	SDSQ				/QUAD FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, READ IN A DATA WORD.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS I	PSTRWRD/(STRWRD)	/STORE A DATA WORD.
	ISZ I	PSTRPTR/(STRPTR)	/BUMP TO NEXT; MORE TO DO?
	JMP	BTRDLP			/YES, KEEP GOING.
	CIF	00			/NO, GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMP	STARTIT			/STARTUP THE BOOT CODE FROM THE DECTAPE.

ROM7756,7756				/CONSTANT 7756.
ROM7747,7747				/CONSTANT 7747.
RM3000,	3000				/CONSTANT 3000.
RM77,	77				/CONSTANT 0077.
PSTRWRD,STRWRD				/POINTER TO "STRWRD" ROUTINE IN FIELD 0.
PSTRW1,	STRWRD+1			/POINTER TO "STRWRD+1" IN FIELD 0.
PSTRW2,	STRWRD+2			/POINTER TO "STRWRD+2" IN FIELD 0.
PSTRW3,	STRWRD+3			/POINTER TO "STRWRD+3" IN FIELD 0.
PSTRPTR,STRPTR				/POINTER TO "STRPTR" IN FIELD 0.
	NOPUNCH				/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	FIELD	0			/WHERE THIS LOADS.

	ENPUNCH				/UN-FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	RMADDR=	.			/WHERE THE FOLLOWING CODE IS LOCATED WITHIN
					/THE ROM.

	RELOC	7340			/WHERE THE FOLLOWING CODE WILL BE LOCATED WHEN
					/LOADED INTO FIELD 0.

	GOADDR=	.			/WHERE THE FOLLOWING WILL BE LOADED INTO RAM.

/	SUBROUTINE TO STORE DATA WORDS READ IN FROM THE DECTAPE INTO MEMORY; ALL WORDS
/	WILL BE LOCATED HERE WHEN EXECUTED.

STRWRD,	.-.				/STORE A WORD ROUTINE.
	DCA I	STRPTR			/STORE AC INTO MEMORY
	CIF	70			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	STRWRD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

STRPTR,	BOOTBUFFER			/WHERE THE TAPE DATA IS STORED.

	RELOC				/TURN OFF RELOCATION.

	RLFACTO=RMADDR-GOADDR		/RELOCATION FACTOR FOR MOVED WORDS.

	FIELD	SRCFLD%10		/BINARY LOADS INTO THIS FIELD.

	NOPUNCH				/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.

	FIELD	ROMFLD%10		/FIELD FOR DEFINITIONS.

	ENPUNCH				/UN-FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.
/	COMES HERE TO CONTINUE THE CALL TO THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER.

/	SEVERAL PARAMETERS ARE SETUP BY THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER:

/	1)	THE DRIVE UNIT FOR THE TRANSFER IS PLACED IN BIT[0] OF "SUNIT".

/	2)	THE FIELD FOR DATA TRANSFER FROM THE CALLER'S DATA BUFFER IS PLACED IN
/		"GETFLD" IN THE FORM OF "CDF XX" WHERE XX IS THE FIELD OF THE CALLER'S
/		DATA BUFFER.  THIS INSTRUCTION IS CONTAINED IN THE "GETWRD" ROUTINE
/		LATER CALLED FROM ROM-BASED ROUTINES FOR WRITING TO THE DECTAPE.

/	3)	THE FIELD FOR DATA TRANSFERS TO THE CALLER'S DATA BUFFER IS PLACED IN
/		"PUTFLD" IN THE FORM OF "CDF XX" WHERE XX IS THE FIELD OF THE CALLER'S
/		DATA BUFFER.  THIS INSTRUCTION IS CONTAINED IN THE "PUTWRD" ROUTINE
/		LATER CALLED FROM ROM-BASED ROUTINES FOR READING FROM THE DECTAPE.

/	4)	"BUFPTR" IS SET TO THE LOWEST ADDRESS OF THE CALLER'S DATA BUFFER.

/	5)	THE STARTING DECTAPE BLOCK OF THE LATEST TRANSFER IS PLACED INTO
/		"SBLK".  THIS WILL BE INCREMENTED BY ROM-BASED CODE FOR EACH DECTAPE
/		BLOCK TRANSFERRED.  [NOTE: IT IS POSSIBLE TO DERIVE THE INITIAL SEARCH
/		DIRECTION FROM THE UPDATED BLOCK VALUE COMPARED TO THE NEXT CALLER'S
/		BLOCK VALUE.]

/	6)	THE DATA REGISTER IS SET TO THE FUNCTION WORD AS DEFINED IN OS/8,
/		COS-300/310 AND P?S/8 [WITH AN EXCEPTION NOTED BELOW]:

/		A) THE READ/WRITE FUNCTION IS PASSED IN BIT[0]; 1=WRITE, 0=READ.

/		B) THE PAGE COUNT FOR THE CALL IS CONTAINED IN BITS[1-5]; IF THIS
/		   VALUE IS SET TO 00000 THEN TRANSFER AN ENTIRE FIELD [32 PAGES].

/		C) BITS[6-10) ARE IGNORED.

/		D) BIT[11] IS SET TO THE INITIAL SEARCH DIRECTION FOR THE TRANSFER [IF
/		   AVAILABLE].  NOTE: OS/8 ONLY SETS THIS BIT IN THE FEW LOCATIONS
/		   WITHIN OS/8 WHERE THIS INFORMATION IS KNOWN ON A STATIC BASIS.
/		   SYSTEMS SUCH AS P?S/8 MAY CALCULATE THIS VALUE DYNAMICALLY FOR ALL
/		   CALLS TO PROVIDE NOTICABLY SUPERIOR PERFORMANCE.  WITHIN EACH
/		   OPERATING SYSTEM THERE IS NO UNIVERSALLY AGREED UPON DEFINITION FOR
/		   THIS BIT IN TERMS OF THE CALLER'S FUNCTION WORD.  THE INITIAL
/		   GUESS AS DEFINED ABOVE ONLY APPLIES TO OS/8 AND COS-300/310.  IN
/		   P?S/8 THIS BIT IS DEFINED AS THE LOGICAL DRIVE UNIT [0 OR 1] THUS
/		   THE NORMAL MEANING OF THE BIT MUST BE MASKED; IF THERE IS
/		   SUFFICIENT ADDITIONAL CODE SPACE IN THE HANDLER, THE DYNAMIC
/		   INITIAL SEARCH DIRECTION WILL BE CALCULATED AND INCLUDED IN THE
/		   DATA REGISTER; IN EDU-SYSTEM 15/30, THIS BIT IS ALWAYS CLEAR THUS
/		   ALL CALLS START THE DECTAPE IN THE REVERSE DIRECTION.

/		NOTE: WHILE OS/8 SPARSELY USES THE POTENTIALLY FORWARD INITIAL SEARCH
/		DIRECTION, THE DYNAMIC METHOD USED IN P?S/8 MAY BE SUCCESSFUL UP TO AS
/		MUCH AS 40% OF THE TIME DEPENDING ON SYSTEM USAGE SPECIFICS.
F2SET,	TAD I	PSUNIT/(SUNIT)		/GET THE DRIVE UNIT WITHOUT THE GO BIT LOADED
	SDLC				/INTO THE COMMAND REGISTER TO STOP THE DECTAPE.
	NL7775				/SETUP THE
	DCA I	PTRYCNT/(TRYCNT)	/RETRY COUNTER.
	SDRC				/GET THE UNIT BIT.
	DCA I	PDRVUNIT/(DRVUNIT)	/STASH IT THERE.
	SDRD				/GET FUNCTION WORD BACK FROM THE DATA REGISTER.
	CLL RAL				/MOVE UP AND
	AND	ROM7600/(7600)		/ISOLATE PAGE BITS.
	DCA I	PPAGCNT/(PAGCNT)	/SAVE AS TRANSFER PAGE COUNTER.
	SDRD				/GET FUNCTION WORD BACK FROM THE DATA REGISTER.
	DCA I	PSFUNCT/(SFUNCTION)	/STASH THE FUNCTION WORD.
	SDRD				/GET FUNCTION WORD BACK FROM THE DATA REGISTER.
	RAR				/INITIAL SEARCH DIRECTION TO LINK.
	JMP	ROMGO			/CONTINUE HERE

PTRYCNT,TRYCNT				/POINTER TO WHERE RETRY COUNT IS STORED.
PDRVUNI,DRVUNIT				/POINTER TO WHERE DRIVE UNIT IS STORED.
PPAGCNT,PAGCNT				/POINTER TO WHERE TRANSFER PAGE COUNT IS STORED.
PSFUNCT,SFUNCTION			/POINTER TO WHERE FUNCTION WORD IS STORED.

	ZBLOCK	.&7600+200-.		/EMPTY SPACE.
	PAGE				/NEXT PAGE.

/	COMES HERE TO CONTINUE THE LATEST READ/WRITE OPERATION.

/	THE AC CONTAINS THE LATEST BUFFER ADDRESS.

RWCONT,	DCA I	PBUFPTR/(BUFPTR)	/SAVE THE BUFFER ADDRESS.
        TAD     RM7600/(-200)		/SETUP THE TRANSFER WORD
        DCA I   PWORDCT/(WORDCT)	/COUNT FOR LATEST BLOCK.

/	NOW LOCATE THE REVERSE GUARD MARK.

RGLOOP,	SDSS				/FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRC				/YES, READ THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	AND	ROM77/(77)		/JUST THE MARK WINDOW BITS.
	TAD	ROMM32/(-32)		/COMPARE TO REVERSE GUARD MARK VALUE.
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF IT MATCHES.
	JMP	RGLOOP			/ELSE GO WAIT FOR IT.
	TAD I	PSFUN2/(SFUNCTION)	/GET THE FUNCTION WORD.
ROM7700,SMA CLA				/SKIP IF WRITING; ALSO USED AS CONSTANT 7700.
	JMP	READIT			/JUMP IF READING.
	SDRC				/GET THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	AND	ROM300/(300)		/JUST WRITE-LOCKOUT AND SELECT/TIME ERROR BITS.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0 EITHER WAY.
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF NO ERRORS.
	JMP	SERR			/GO THERE TO COMPLAIN ABOUT ERRORS.
	JMS	RDQUAD			/READ THE REVERSE GUARD WORD.
RM7600,	CLA!400				/CLEAN UP; ALSO USED AS CONSTANT 7600.
	TAD	ROM1400/(1400)		/GET GO AND WRITE BITS.
        TAD I   PDRVU2/(DRVUNIT)	/ADD ON DRIVE UNIT BIT.
        SDLC				/START UP THE WRITE OPERATION.
	NL7777				/SET ALL ONES.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	WRQUAD			/WRITE REVERSE PCC [CHECKSUM] WORD.
	NL7777				/SETUP ALL ONES AGAIN.
	DCA I	PSCKSUM/(SCKSUM)	/SETUP THE INITIAL CHECKSUM.
WRILUP,	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	GETWRD			/GET A DATA WORD FROM THE CALLER'S BUFFER.
	CIF CDF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
        JMS     WRQUAD			/WRITE OUT A DATA WORD.
        ISZ I   PBUFPTR/(BUFPTR)	/BUMP TO NEXT.
ROM77,	77				/CONSTANT 0077; PLACED HERE IN CASE IT SKIPS.
        ISZ I   PWORDCT/(WORDCT)	/DONE ENOUGH DATA WORDS?
        JMP     WRILUP			/NO, GO BACK FOR MORE.
	CIF	00			/GOTO FIELD 0.
	JMS	WRQUAD			/WRITE 0000 AS 129TH DATA WORD.
        JMP     RWCOMMON		/CONTINUE THERE TO WRITE OUT THE CHECKSUM.
/	COMES HERE WITH CHECKSUM IN AC[0-5] IF WRITING.

WRITEND,CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	WRQUAD			/WRITE THE PCC WORD.

/	AN EXTRA WRITE IS USED TO ENSURE THE CHECKSUM IS WRITTEN.  [APPARENTLY, THE
/	TD8E WRITE OPERATION IS INHERENTLY DOUBLE-BUFFERED, THUS EACH WRITE OPERATION
/	MERELY SETS UP THE DATA FOR THE NEXT WORD.]

	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	WRQUAD			/THIS FINISHES THE CHECKSUM WRITING.

/	FALLS THROUGH IF WRITING WITH A CLEAR AC.  IF READING, THE CHECKSUM IS IN THE
/	AC WHICH [HOPEFULLY] IS ZERO.

READEND,CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0 EITHER WAY.
	SDST				/TIMING ERROR?
	SZA CLA				/NOT A TIMING ERROR, IS THE CHECKSUM CLEAR?
	JMP	SERR			/JUMP IF EITHER ERROR.	
	TAD I	PPAGC2/(PAGCNT)		/GET THE CURRENT PAGE COUNTER.
        TAD     RM7600/(-200)		/SUBTRACT ONE PAGE.
	SNA				/SKIP IF STILL MORE TO GO.
	JMP	SEXIT			/ALL ROM OPERATIONS ARE DONE; RETURN TO HANDLER.
	DCA I	PPAGC2/(PAGCNT)		/STORE BACK UPDATED PAGE COUNT.
	CIF	70			/STAYING IN FIELD 7 AFTER ALL.
	JMP I	PNXTBLK/(NXTBLK)	/GO DO ANOTHER BLOCK.

/	COMES HERE IF READING.

READIT,	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	RDQUAD			/READ THE REVERSE GUARD WORD.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	RDQUAD			/READ THE LOCK WORD.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	RDQUAD			/GET THE REVERSE PCC WORD.
	AND	ROM77/(77)		/JUST THE CHECKSUM BITS.
	TAD	ROM7700/(7700)		/ADD ON SET HIGH-ORDER BITS.
	DCA I	PSCKSUM/(SCKSUM)	/STORE INITIAL CHECKSUM VALUE.
READLP,	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	RDQUAD			/GET A DATA WORD.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	EQUFUN			/INCLUDE DATA IN RUNNING CHECKSUM.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	PUTWRD			/STORE THE DATA WORD IN THE CALLER'S BUFFER.
	CDF	00			/BACK TO FIELD 0.
	ISZ I	PBUFPTR/(BUFPTR)	/BUMP TO NEXT ADDRESS.
ROM300,	300				/CONSTANT 0300; PLACED HERE IN CASE IT SKIPS.
	ISZ I	PWORDCT/(WORDCT)	/DONE ALL DATA WORDS?
	JMP	READLP			/NO, KEEP GOING.
/	GET THE 129TH [FINAL] WORD [CHECKSUMMED ONLY, NOT TRANSFERRED].

	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	RDQUAD			/GET THE FINAL DATA WORD.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	EQUFUN			/INCLUDE FINAL DATA WORD IN RUNNING CHECKSUM.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	RDQUAD			/GET THE PCC WORD.
	AND	ROM7700/(7700)		/JUST CHECKSUM BITS.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	EQUFUN			/INCLUDE IN RUNNING CHECKSUM.
	JMP	RWCOMMON		/CONTINUE THERE.

/	THIS CODE CONTINUES THE WRQUAD SUBROUTINE IN FIELD 0.

WRQ,	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	EQUFUN			/LATEST DATA WORD .EQU. CHECKSUM.
	SDSQ				/QUAD FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDLD				/YES, SEND LATEST DATA WORD.
	CLA CLL				/CLEAN UP
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMP	CIFR			/CONTINUE THERE TO RETURN TO ORIGINAL CALLER.

/	COMES HERE AT THE END OF THE LATEST BLOCK READ/WRITE OPERATION AFTER THE DATA
/	IS PROCESSED.  [NOTE: THE DATA FIELD IS 00.]

RWCOMMO,CLA				/CLEAN UP.
	TAD I	PSCKSUM/(SCHKSUM)	/GET THE ACCUMULATED CHECKSUM.
	CLL CMA RTL;RTL;RTL		/INVERT AND MOVE UP SIX BITS.
	CIF	00			/GOING TO FIELD 0.
	JMS	EQUFUN			/EQUIVALENCE LOW-ORDER HALF TO HIGH-ORDER HALF.
	CLA CLL				/CLEAN UP.
	TAD I	PSFUN2/(SFUNCTION)	/GET THE FUNCTION WORD.
	RAL				/MOVE READ/WRITE BIT TO LINK
	CLA				/THROW THE REST AWAY.
	TAD I	PSCKSUM/(SCHKSUM)	/GET THE FINAL CHECKSUM VALUE.
	AND	ROM7700/(7700)		/JUST THE SIX-BIT CHECKSUM.
	SNL				/SKIP IF WRITING.
	JMP	READEND			/JUMP IF READING.

/	WE NEED TO WRITE OUT THE PCC MARK (CHECKSUM) WORD.

	JMP	WRITEND			/CONTINUE THERE.
PBUFPTR,BUFPTR				/POINTER TO DATA BUFFER POINTER.
PWORDCT,WORDCT				/POINTER TO WORD COUNTER.
ROMM32,	-32				/CONSTANT 7746.
PSFUN2,	SFUNCTION			/POINTER TO FUNCTION WORD.
ROM1400,1400				/CONSTANT 1400.
PDRVU2,	DRVUNIT				/POINTER TO DRIVE UNIT.
PSCKSUM,SCKSUM				/POINTER TO CHECKSUM WORD.
PPAGC2,	PAGCNT				/POINTER TO PAGE COUNT.
PNXTBLK,NXTBLK				/POINTER TO NEXT BLOCK ROUTINE.

	ZBLOCK	.&7600+200-.		/EMPTY SPACE.

	ROMEND=	.			/END OF ROM CODE.
	NOPUNCH				/FOOL THE ASSEMBLER.






	FIELD	0			/LOADS IN FIELD 0.

/	THE ROM-BASED BUILT-IN BOOTSTRAP IS COMPATIBLE WITH THE STANDALONE TD8E
/	BOOTSTRAP; SOME OF THE LOADING CONVENTIONS ARE SHARED BY BOTH ROUTINES.

/	THE STANDALONE BOOTSTRAP FOLLOWS TO DEFINE THE RELEVANT LOADING AND STARTING
/	ROUTINES.

	*7300				/THIS IS WHERE THE STANDALONE BOOTSTRAP LOADS.

K1000,	TAD	GET/(2000)		/GET REVERSE DIRECTION BIT.  ALSO CONSTANT
					/1000 [ONLY THE HIGH-ORDER FOUR BITS ARE USED].
	JMS	GET			/LOCATE REVERSE END ZONE.
	JMS	GET			/LOOK FOR 31 CODE GOING FORWARD.
RD,	SDSQ				/QUAD FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, READ IT IN.
	DCA I	WCT			/STORE IT INTO MEMORY.
	ISZ	WCT			/BUMP TO NEXT; ALL DONE YET?
	JMP	RD			/NO, KEEP GOING.

/	AT THIS POINT BLOCK 0000 HAS BEEN READ INTO 07360-07557 AND BLOCK 0001 HAS
/	BEEN READ INTO 07600-07777.  [NOTE: ONLY OS/8 AND COS-300/310 REQUIRE THE
/	BLOCK OOO1 DATA LOAD; EDU-SYSTEM 15/30 AND P?S/8 REQUIRE ONLY BLOCK 0000 BE
/	LOADED INTO MEMORY; THE OFFSET WILL BE EXPECTED AND DEALT WITH ACCORDINGLY.]

	JMP I	STRT/(STARTIT)		/GO START IT UP.

/	GET TO DESIRED MARK WINDOW VALUE ROUTINE.  [NOTE: INITIALIZED TO SAVE A WORD.]

GET,	2000				/GET TO DESIRED MARK ROUTINE.
	TAD	K1000/(1000)		/ADD ON UNIT [0] AND GO BITS TO DIRECTION BIT
					/PASSED IN AC.  NOTE: LOW-ORDER BITS IGNORED.
	SDLC				/LOAD COMMAND REGISTER TO START THE TAPE MOVING.
MRKSRCH,SDSS				/NEW LINE IN?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRC				/YES, READ THE COMMAND REGISTER.
	AND	K77/(77)		/JUST MARK WINDOW BITS.

/	THE FIRST TIME THIS ROUTINE IS USED, THE REVERSE END ZONE MARK VALUE IS BEING
/	SEARCHED FOR; THE SECOND TIME, THE 31 CODE IS BEING SEARCHED FOR.

MARKSEL,TAD	MARK			/COMPARE TO DESIRED [END MARK OR 31 CODE].
	SZA CLA				/SKIP IF IT MATCHES.
	JMP	MRKSRCH			/GO BACK IF NOT.
	ISZ	MARKSEL			/BUMP TO NEXT MARK CODE FOR NEXT TIME.
	JMP I	GET			/RETURN TO CALLER.

WCT,	BOOTBUFFER			/WORD COUNT AND STORE POINTER.
MARK,	-22				/REVERSE END ZONE MARK CONSTANT.
	-31				/31 CODE MARK CONSTANT.
K77,	77				/CONSTANT 0077.
STRT,	STARTIT				/WHERE TO START THE BOOT CODE READ IN.
	*7354				/TAPE BLOCK 0000 CONTENTS STARTS LOADING HERE.

BOOTBUF,ZBLOCK	4			/THESE WORDS ARE LOADED BEFORE THE DATA WORDS.

	BLKDATA=.			/THE DATA WORDS IN BLOCK 0000 ARE LOADED HERE.

	ZBLOCK	7400-.			/THE FIRST 0020 WORDS OF BLOCK 0000 LOAD HERE.

	STARTIT=.			/THE REST OF THE WORDS OF BLOCK 0000 LOAD HERE
					/AND CONTROL IS TRANSFERRED HERE; THE CODE
					/LOADED HERE NEEDS TO BE AWARE OF THIS TO
					/CONTINUE THE SYSTEM-SPECIFIC BOOTUP PROCESS.
NOPUNCH


	XLIST	OFF			/TURN LISTING OFF.

	IFNZRO	SYSKEL	<

	XLIST	ON
	*7600				/LAST PAGE IN FIELD 0.

/	CERTAIN ROUTINES WITHIN THE SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER MUST BE LOCATED AT SPECIFIC
/	ADDRESSES AS DETERMINED WITHIN THE ROM.  ALL MEMORY LOCATIONS DEFINED BELOW IN
/	FIELD 0 MUST BE LOADED ACCORDINGLY; IT IS THE RESPONSIBILITY OF THE OPERATING
/	SYSTEM TO LOAD THE CORRECT VALUES INTO THE STATED MEMORY LOCATIONS.

/	THE OPERATING SYSTEM SETS UP THE ROM-DESIGNATED VARIABLES FROM THE PARAMETERS
/	PASSED TO IT ACCORDING TO THAT SYSTEM'S SUBROUTINE CALL CONVENTIONS.  THIS
/	INCLUDES PASSING SOME ASPECT OF THE FUNCTION WORD OR OTHER DATA IN THE DATA
/	REGISTER USING THE "SDLD" INSTRUCTION.  WHEN ALL VARIABLES ARE SET, THE ROM IS
/	ENTERED BY THE FOLLOWING:

/	CDF	00			/SETUP OUR FIELD
/	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
/	JMP I	PF2SET/(F2SET)		/START UP THE ROM.

/	SUPPORT ROUTINES FOR THE ROM-BASED CODE.

	*GETWRD				/WHERE THIS BELONGS.

GETWRD,	.-.				/GET A WORD FROM THE CALLER'S BUFFER ROUTINE.
GETFLD,	.-.^10+CDF			/WILL BE "CDF XX" WHERE XX IS THE
					/DATA FIELD OF THE CALLER'S BUFFER.
	TAD I	BUFPTR			/GET A WORD FROM THE BUFFER.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	GETWRD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

PUTWRD,	.-.				/PUT A WORD INTO THE CALLER'S BUFFER ROUTINE.
PUTFLD,	.-.^10+CDF			/WILL BE "CDF XX" WHERE XX IS THE
					/DATA FIELD OF THE CALLER'S BUFFER.
	DCA I	BUFPTR			/PUT A WORD INTO THE BUFFER.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	PUTWRD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

/	ERRORS COME HERE.

SERR,	CLA CLL				/CLEAN UP.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	ISZ	TRYCNT			/TOO MANY ERRORS?
/	JMP I	PROMGO/(ROMGO)		/NO, TRY AGAIN.

/	TOO MANY RETRIES; HANDLE FATAL ERROR HERE.

	*WRQUAD				/WHERE THIS BELONGS.

WRQUAD,	.-.				/WRITE A 12-BIT WORD TO THE DECTAPE ROUTINE.

/	THE ACTUAL CODE IS IN THE ROM; THIS IS USED ONLY TO ESTABLISH THE PROPER
/	SUBROUTINE LINKAGE AS IT IS CALLED FROM SEVERAL PLACES.

	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO THE ROM FIELD.
	JMP	WRQ			/CONTINUE THERE.
/	COMES BACK HERE FROM THE ROM-BASED CODE THAT CARRIES OUT THE SUBROUTINE CODE.

CIFR,	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO THE ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	WRQUAD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

RDQUAD,	.-.				/READ A 12-BIT WORD FROM THE DECTAPE ROUTINE.
	SDSQ				/QUAD LINE FLAG UP?
	JMP	.-1			/NO, WAIT FOR IT.
	SDRD				/YES, READ IN THE LATEST 12-BIT WORD.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	RDQUAD			/RETURN TO CALLER.

EQUFUN,	.-.				/EQUIVALENCE DATA WITH CHECKSUM ROUTINE.
	CMA				/INVERT THE DATA AND ,XOR. THE DATA WORD.
	DCA	EQUTMP			/SAVE IT.
	TAD	EQUTMP			/GET IT BACK.
	AND	SCKSUM			/GET THE CARRY BITS.
	CIA				/INVERT FOR SUBTRACTION.
	CLL RAL				/MOVE OVER.
	TAD	EQUTMP			/NOW ADD THE LATEST.
	TAD	SCKSUM			/ADD THE NON-CARRY BITS TO RUNNING SUM.
	DCA	SCKSUM			/STORE BACK.
	TAD	EQUTMP			/GET THE LATEST DATA BACK.
	CLL CMA				/NOW HAVE CLEAR LINK AND THE LATEST WORD.
	CIF	ROMFLD			/GOING TO ROM FIELD.
	JMP I	EQUFUN			/RETURN TO CALLER.

/	THE ROM RETURNS HERE WHEN ALL TAPE OPERATIONS HAVE FINISHED.

SEXIT,	/				/DO FINAL HOUSEKEEPING AND RETURN TO CALLER.

	*TRYCNT				/WHERE THIS BELONGS.

TRYCNT,	.-.				/RETRY COUNTER.
DRVUNIT,.-.				/DECTAPE DRIVE UNIT.
SFUNCTI,.-.				/FUNCTION WORD BIT[0]; CLEAR=READ, SET=WRITE.
					/[NOTE: ALL OTHER BITS ARE IGNORED.]
SCKSUM,	.-.				/RUNNING CHECKSUM WORD.
SBLK,	.-.				/LATEST DECTAPE BLOCK.
SUNIT,	.-.				/DRIVE UNIT FOR LATEST FUNCTION.

	*EQUTMP				/WHERE THIS BELONGS.

EQUTMP,	.-.				/TEMPORARY FOR EQUIVALENCE ROUTINE.
BUFADRE,.-.				/READ/WRITE BUFFER POINTER.
PAGCNT,	.-.				/TRANSFER PAGE COUNT.
BUFPTR,	.-.				/BUFFER POINTER FOR WORD TRANSFERS.
WORDCT,	.-.				/WORD COUNTER FOR LATEST BLOCK TRANSFER.

/	END OF SYSTEM DEVICE HANDLER SKELETON.
	XLIST	OFF	>

	XLIST	ON

	$				/THAT'S ALL FOLK!



